(window.COURSE_MODULES = window.COURSE_MODULES || []).push({"id": "P3", "code": "Paper 3", "title": "Paper 3: Research, Audience, Purpose and Production", "overview": "<div class=\"box navy p032n\" style=\"border:2px solid #F5C542;border-radius:12px;padding:12px 14px;margin:6px 0 12px\"><p style=\"margin:0 0 6px\"><b>📢 CXC notice for the 2027 examination: Use of Notes in Communication Studies Examination by Candidates</b></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 4px\">i. Candidates writing the Paper 032 for Communication Studies Units 1 &amp; 2 will be permitted to take into the examination room, a single sheet, double-sided A-4 paper, with no more than 10 points (and nothing else) related to the topic of their response.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 8px\">ii. The topic is Non-communicable Diseases in Children, and the intended audience are Parents.</p><p style=\"margin:0\"><b>What this means for you:</b> for the 2027 examination your topic is <b>non-communicable diseases in children</b>, written for <b>parents</b>. Your notes go on <b>one A4 sheet</b> (your <b>notes sheet</b>), which you may use on <b>both sides</b>, with <b>no more than 10 points</b> and nothing else. Many samples in this course use the 2026 topic (natural disasters, for homeowners): use them to practise the skills, then apply them to the 2027 topic.</p></div>\n<p style=\"margin-top:14px\"><b>The format of Paper 032</b></p>\n<p>In this course, <b>Paper 3 means Paper 032</b>, the Paper 3 written paper. It lasts <b>2 hours 15 minutes</b>, is worth <b>60 marks</b> and contributes <b>20 per cent</b> of your final assessment. Marks are awarded for <b>content, organisation and expression</b>.</p>\n<p>The paper is built on the <b>topic and intended audience set for your exam year</b> (for example, 2026: natural disasters for homeowners; 2027: non-communicable diseases in children for parents). It has <b>three compulsory questions</b>, covering the three Modules and based on <b>one central stimulus</b>. <b>Section A</b> (20 marks) has two short-answer questions: Question 1 tests Module 1 skills (challenges in receiving information, media, sources) and Question 2 tests Module 2 skills (dialectal variety and register, message points, slogans). <b>Section B</b> (40 marks) asks you to write a piece of 200–250 words on the topic (25 marks) and a <b>50–100 word justification</b> of its purpose, intended audience and context of communication (15 marks).</p>\n<p>For the 2027 examination you may bring <b>one A4 sheet of notes, which you may use on both sides,</b> with <b>no more than 10 points</b> related to the topic of your response, and nothing else. Work through the five units in order: they follow the paper from Section A to Section B, and every unit ends with a writing task that includes a justification.</p>", "objectives": ["By the end you will be able to describe the format of Paper 032: its sections, question types, marks, timing, central stimulus and notes sheet rules.", "By the end you will be able to state the topic and intended audience for your exam year and plan your preparation around them.", "By the end you will be able to explain, not just identify, the challenges a target group may face in receiving information.", "By the end you will be able to justify suitable traditional and digital media for a specific audience and comment on credible local sources of information.", "By the end you will be able to explain why a message uses a particular dialectal variety and language register, and use Creole and Standard English deliberately.", "By the end you will be able to select the most important message points for an audience and create a slogan using a named literary technique.", "By the end you will be able to profile an audience and follow the planning chain: Audience, Purpose, Context, Message, Language and Register, Tone and Medium.", "By the end you will be able to write a reflective or expository piece of 200–250 words on the year's topic, using at least one deliberate rhetorical appeal.", "By the end you will be able to write a 50–100 word justification of purpose, intended audience and context of communication.", "By the end you will be able to prepare a 10-point notes sheet from evaluated research and manage your time on exam day."], "glossary": [{"term": "Paper 032", "def": "The Paper 3 written paper: 2 hours 15 minutes, 60 marks, 20 per cent of the final assessment."}, {"term": "Central stimulus", "def": "The single piece of material (for example a public service announcement, article or set of figures) on which all three questions are based."}, {"term": "Topic and intended audience", "def": "The subject and target group set for each exam year, e.g. 2026: natural disasters, homeowners; 2027: non-communicable diseases in children, parents."}, {"term": "Section A", "def": "The short-answer section (20 marks): Question 1 on Module 1 skills and Question 2 on Module 2 skills, each worth 10 marks."}, {"term": "Section B", "def": "The production section (40 marks): a 200–250 word piece on the topic (25 marks) and a 50–100 word justification (15 marks)."}, {"term": "Notes sheet", "def": "Your notes for Paper 032: a single sheet of double-sided A4 paper with no more than 10 points related to the topic of your response, and nothing else."}, {"term": "Justification", "def": "A 50–100 word paragraph explaining the purpose, intended audience and context of communication of your piece."}, {"term": "Target group", "def": "The specific people a message is meant to reach, described by age, educational background, location and needs."}, {"term": "Traditional media", "def": "Long-established ways of communicating, such as radio, television, film, telephone, word of mouth and mobile loudspeaker (and print)."}, {"term": "Digital media", "def": "Internet-based ways of communicating, such as websites, social media, messaging apps and email."}, {"term": "Orature", "def": "Oral tradition and spoken communication; many Caribbean people are especially receptive to information delivered orally."}, {"term": "Local source of information", "def": "A person or outlet within the community that people can consult, such as a health worker, teacher, religious leader, Justice of the Peace or an official government website."}, {"term": "Credibility and authority", "def": "Whether a source is produced by someone qualified and trustworthy."}, {"term": "Currency and relevance", "def": "Whether a source is recent enough and actually addresses your topic, audience and place."}, {"term": "Reliability", "def": "Whether a source is accurate, consistent, shows its evidence and agrees with other trustworthy sources."}, {"term": "Dialectal variety", "def": "The variety of a language used, for example Standard English (acrolect) or Creole (basilect or mesolect)."}, {"term": "Acrolect", "def": "The variety on the creole continuum closest to Standard English, used for official communication."}, {"term": "Register", "def": "The level of formality of language chosen to suit the audience, purpose and context."}, {"term": "Message point", "def": "A single, essential piece of information the audience must know or act on."}, {"term": "Slogan", "def": "A short, memorable phrase used in a campaign to sum up its message and encourage action."}, {"term": "Allusion", "def": "A reference to a well-known saying, text, song or event, e.g. \"A stick in time saves lives\" alludes to \"A stitch in time saves nine\"."}, {"term": "Rhetorical question", "def": "A question asked for effect rather than for an answer, to make the audience reflect."}, {"term": "Content", "def": "What is said: the ideas, facts and message of a piece."}, {"term": "Context of communication", "def": "Where and how a piece would appear in the real world: the publication, platform, event or occasion."}, {"term": "Logos, ethos, pathos, kairos", "def": "The four rhetorical appeals: to logic and evidence, to credibility, to emotion, and to timeliness (why now)."}, {"term": "Reflective piece", "def": "A personal, first-person piece that thinks about an experience and what was learnt from it."}, {"term": "Expository piece", "def": "A piece that explains or informs, such as an essay, a journalistic article or a journal entry."}], "units": [{"id": "P3U1", "title": "Inside Paper 032", "objectives": ["Describe the format of Paper 032: timing, marks, sections, the central stimulus and the three compulsory questions.", "Identify the topic and intended audience set for your exam year.", "State the notes sheet rules exactly and know the response types allowed."], "videos": [{"title": "CAPE Communication Studies Paper 032 overview", "q": "CAPE Communication Studies Paper 032 private candidates exam tips"}, {"title": "Creative vs expository vs reflective writing", "q": "difference between reflective expository and creative writing for students"}, {"title": "Reading an exam stimulus", "q": "how to analyse a stimulus passage before answering exam questions"}], "screens": [{"type": "learn", "title": "Paper 032 at a glance", "html": "<p>In this course \"Paper 3\" means <b>Paper 032</b>, the Paper 3 written paper. Here is the whole paper on one page.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What it means for you</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2 hours 15 minutes</b></td><td>Enough time to read, plan and check, if you use a routine.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>60 marks, 20 per cent</b></td><td>A fifth of your final assessment. Treat it as a major paper.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Three compulsory questions</b></td><td>No choice. They cover the three Modules.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>One central stimulus</b></td><td>All questions are based on it, e.g. a public service announcement (PSA) in a newspaper.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Marks for content, organisation and expression</b></td><td>What you say, how you arrange it, and how well you write it all count.</td></tr></table></div>\n<p><b>How the marks are laid out (specimen paper):</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Section</th><th>Question</th><th>Skills</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td rowspan=\"2\">A</td><td>Q1 (Module 1)</td><td>(a) explain TWO challenges a group faces in receiving information [4]; (b) justify TWO traditional media other than print [4]; (c) comment on ONE local source of information [2]</td><td>10</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Q2 (Module 2)</td><td>(a) explain reasons for the dialectal variety and register used [4]; (b) list the TWO most important message points [2]; (c) create a slogan using ONE literary technique [4]</td><td>10</td></tr>\n<tr><td>B</td><td>Q3</td><td>(a) a reflective OR expository piece of 200–250 words on the topic [25]; (b) a 50–100 word justification of purpose, intended audience and context of communication [15]</td><td>40</td></tr></table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Read every question carefully. Examiners alter one or two words: \"explain\" is not \"identify\", \"traditional media other than print\" rules out the newspaper, and \"reflective OR expository\" tells you which forms that year's question wants.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "The big numbers", "p": "<p>2 hours 15 minutes, 60 marks, 20 per cent, 3 compulsory questions, 1 stimulus.</p>"}, {"h": "Two sections", "p": "<p>Section A = two short-answer questions (Modules 1 and 2), 20 marks. Section B = one piece plus a justification, 40 marks.</p>"}, {"h": "Section B split", "p": "<p>Piece of 200–250 words = 25 marks. Justification of 50–100 words = 15 marks.</p>"}], "plain": "<p>Paper 032 is one written exam: <b>2 hours 15 minutes</b>, <b>60 marks</b>, and 20 per cent of your final result. All three questions must be answered, and all are based on one stimulus, such as a newspaper notice. <b>Section A</b> (Questions 1 and 2) has short answers worth 20 marks. <b>Section B</b> (Question 3) asks for a 200–250 word piece and a 50–100 word justification, worth 40 marks.</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: know what each question tests.</b> The three questions mirror the three Modules (Question 1 for Module 1, Question 2 for Module 2, Question 3 for Module 3), so revise the Module skills, not just the format. The most common costly error is treating the justification as an afterthought: at 15 marks it is worth more than all of Question 1. Another is over-writing Section A. A two-mark \"comment\" needs one point, clearly explained; a paragraph wastes minutes you will need for Section B.</p> <i>Your full exam-day time plan is in 4.5, Section B exam strategy.</i>"}, {"type": "learn", "title": "Your year's topic and audience", "html": "<p>Paper 032 is based on the <b>topic and intended audience set for each year</b>. You know them in advance, so your research and notes sheet can be ready long before exam day.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Year</th><th>Topic</th><th>Intended audience</th></tr>\n<tr><td>2025</td><td>Climate change</td><td>Teenagers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2026</td><td>Natural disasters</td><td>Homeowners</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2027</td><td>Non-communicable diseases in children</td><td>Parents</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2028</td><td>Single parenthood</td><td>Teenagers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2029</td><td>Mental health challenges</td><td>Community groups</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2030</td><td>Cyberbullying</td><td>Parents</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2031</td><td>The increase in street children</td><td>Ministry of Youth</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2032</td><td>Autism</td><td>Teachers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2033</td><td>The challenges of having acne</td><td>Teenagers</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>In this course we practise most with <b>2026 (natural disasters, homeowners)</b> and <b>2027 (non-communicable diseases in children, parents)</b>. Think about what each pairing means. Homeowners across the Caribbean after a hurricane season care about roofs, shutters, insurance, cisterns and emergency kits. Parents worried about childhood NCDs care about lunch boxes, sweet drinks, screen time and cost.</p>\n<p>The <b>audience is part of the question</b>. A brilliant piece aimed at teenagers will miss the mark if the year's audience is homeowners.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Start a personal glossary for your year's topic now: for 2026, words like <i>storm surge</i>, <i>hurricane straps</i>, <i>evacuation</i>; for 2027, <i>non-communicable</i>, <i>hypertension</i>, <i>sedentary</i>. Write each word, its meaning, a synonym and a sentence of your own.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "Known in advance", "p": "<p>Each year has a set topic and audience. Find yours in the table.</p>"}, {"h": "Our focus", "p": "<p>2026: natural disasters, homeowners. 2027: NCDs in children, parents.</p>"}, {"h": "Audience counts", "p": "<p>Always write for the year's intended audience.</p>"}], "plain": "<p>Each exam year has a <b>topic</b> and an <b>intended audience</b>, published long before the exam. For the 2027 examination, the topic is <b>non-communicable diseases in children</b> and the intended audience is <b>parents</b>. (The 2026 topic was natural disasters, for homeowners; many samples in this course use it for practice.) So your research and notes can be ready early. Always write for your year's audience. In 2026, for example, homeowners in Dominica cared about roofs, landslides, insurance and water storage; a piece about storms aimed at teenagers would miss the mark.</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: the audience changes everything.</b> The same topic produces a different piece for a different audience: natural disasters for homeowners calls for practical advice on straps, shutters, insurance and cost, while the same topic for teenagers would focus on safety, fear and family roles. Your justification is marked on how clearly you connect your piece to the intended audience, so profile it precisely: \"first-time homeowners still paying a mortgage\" or \"retired homeowners in hillside districts\" is far stronger than \"homeowners\". A common misconception is that the examiner is the audience. You write <i>for</i> the year's audience in a real context; the examiner judges how well you do it. Build a topic glossary now, so precise terms are ready on exam day.</p>"}, {"type": "match", "game": true, "title": "Match the year to its topic and audience", "intro": "<p>Match each exam year to its topic and intended audience.</p>", "pairs": [{"l": "2025", "r": "Climate change, for teenagers"}, {"l": "2026", "r": "Natural disasters, for homeowners"}, {"l": "2027", "r": "Non-communicable diseases in children, for parents"}, {"l": "2029", "r": "Mental health challenges, for community groups"}, {"l": "2030", "r": "Cyberbullying, for parents"}, {"l": "2032", "r": "Autism, for teachers"}], "win": "Well done. Knowing your year's topic AND audience means you can research, build your notes sheet and practise long before exam day.", "tutor": [{"h": "Our two practice years", "p": "<p>2026 = natural disasters + homeowners. 2027 = NCDs in children + parents.</p>"}, {"h": "Pairs to remember", "p": "<p>Two years have parents as the audience: 2027 (NCDs in children) and 2030 (cyberbullying). Teachers appear for autism in 2032.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Match our two practice years (Foundation)", "pairs": [{"l": "2026", "r": "Natural disasters, for homeowners"}, {"l": "2027", "r": "Non-communicable diseases in children, for parents"}, {"l": "2025", "r": "Climate change, for teenagers"}], "win": "Good. 2026 homeowners, 2027 parents."}, "up": {"title": "Match the year to its topic and audience (Challenge)", "bloom": "Apply", "intro": "<p>Now apply what you know. Match each year's topic and audience to the piece that fits BOTH of them.</p>", "pairs": [{"l": "2025: climate change, for teenagers", "r": "A reflective journal entry by a student whose family's seaside village in Guyana is losing land to the sea"}, {"l": "2026: natural disasters, for homeowners", "r": "A newspaper feature explaining how to secure a roof before hurricane season"}, {"l": "2028: single parenthood, for teenagers", "r": "A journal entry in which a sixth-former reflects on helping her mother raise two younger brothers"}, {"l": "2029: mental health challenges, for community groups", "r": "An expository article for a church men's group on recognising the signs of depression"}, {"l": "2030: cyberbullying, for parents", "r": "An article explaining how parents can spot signs that a child is being bullied online"}, {"l": "2031: the increase in street children, for the Ministry of Youth", "r": "An expository essay recommending drop-in centres and school re-entry programmes"}, {"l": "2032: autism, for teachers", "r": "An essay explaining classroom routines that help autistic pupils feel secure"}, {"l": "2033: the challenges of having acne, for teenagers", "r": "A reflective piece by a Form 5 student on how acne affected her confidence"}], "win": "Excellent. Each piece fits the topic AND speaks to the intended audience. In the exam, a piece that fits only the topic loses marks for audience engagement and in the justification."}}, {"type": "learn", "title": "The notes sheet and the response types", "html": "<div class=\"box navy p032n\" style=\"border:2px solid #F5C542;border-radius:12px;padding:12px 14px;margin:6px 0 12px\"><p style=\"margin:0 0 6px\"><b>📢 CXC notice for the 2027 examination: Use of Notes in Communication Studies Examination by Candidates</b></p><p style=\"margin:0 0 4px\">i. Candidates writing the Paper 032 for Communication Studies Units 1 &amp; 2 will be permitted to take into the examination room, a single sheet, double-sided A-4 paper, with no more than 10 points (and nothing else) related to the topic of their response.</p><p style=\"margin:0 0 8px\">ii. The topic is Non-communicable Diseases in Children, and the intended audience are Parents.</p><p style=\"margin:0\"><b>What this means for you:</b> for the 2027 examination your topic is <b>non-communicable diseases in children</b>, written for <b>parents</b>. Your notes go on <b>one A4 sheet</b> (your <b>notes sheet</b>), which you may use on <b>both sides</b>, with <b>no more than 10 points</b> and nothing else. Many samples in this course use the 2026 topic (natural disasters, for homeowners): use them to practise the skills, then apply them to the 2027 topic.</p></div><p>For the 2027 examination you may bring <b>a single A4 sheet of notes (double-sided)</b>, with <b>no more than 10 points</b> related to the topic of your response, <b>and nothing else</b>. No second sheet, no printed article, no phone.</p>\n<p>For your production, the regulations allow:</p>\n<div class=\"trio\">\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Creative</span><p>Poetry, drama, prose fiction.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Expository</span><p>A journalistic piece, a journal entry, an essay.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Always</span><p>A 50–100 word justification: purpose, intended audience, context of communication.</p></div>\n</div>\n<p>In the specimen paper, Question 3(a) asked for a <b>reflective OR expository</b> piece of <b>200–250 words</b>. So the exact wording of your year's question decides which forms are acceptable. A reflective piece looks back on an experience in the first person (a journal entry is a natural fit); an expository piece explains or informs (an essay or a newspaper article).</p>\n<p><b>Worked example (2026).</b> Question: <i>\"Write a reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words based on the topic natural disasters.\"</i> Good choices: a homeowner's journal entry the night after a hurricane warning (reflective); a newspaper article advising homeowners how to secure their roofs (expository). A poem would not fit this wording.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Remember that expression is marked: Standard English, the correct word rather than the biggest word, and answering for the marks available.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "The notes sheet", "p": "<p>2027: one A4 sheet, both sides allowed, up to 10 points on your topic, nothing else.</p>"}, {"h": "Read the form", "p": "<p>If the question says \"reflective OR expository\", write one of those, such as a journal entry or an article.</p>"}, {"h": "Always justify", "p": "<p>Every piece comes with a 50–100 word justification.</p>"}], "plain": "<p>For 2027 you may bring <b>one</b> A4 sheet of notes (both sides), with <b>no more than 10 points</b> on your topic, and nothing else. Your piece may be <b>creative</b> (a poem, drama or story) or <b>expository</b> (an article, a journal entry or an essay), but the year's question decides which forms fit. If it says \"reflective OR expository\", a Montserrat homeowner's journal entry fits; a poem does not.</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: the form is a choice you must be able to justify.</b> Choose reflective when a personal turning point will move your audience (pathos); choose expository when your audience needs clear, organised advice (logos). Either way, a journal entry that only lists events is weak: reflective writing must arrive at insight, and expository writing needs a clear organisational pattern. Use your 10 notes-sheet points strategically: key terms, two or three credible facts, a source, a hook and reminders for your justification, not sentences to copy. A common misconception is that a longer card helps; a sheet crowded with tiny writing is useless under pressure. Whatever you choose, the 50–100 word justification is always required.</p>"}, {"type": "sort", "game": true, "title": "Allowed in the exam room?", "intro": "<p>Shania is packing for Paper 032 (2026 topic: natural disasters). Sort each item.</p>", "main": "Items in Shania's bag", "bins": ["Allowed", "Not allowed"], "items": [{"t": "A printed government hurricane guide", "bin": 1, "fb": "Nothing else is permitted. The key facts from the guide belong on the sheet as short points."}, {"t": "A notes sheet with 10 short points on natural disasters", "bin": 0, "fb": "Ten points is the maximum, and it is allowed if the points relate to her topic."}, {"t": "A notes sheet with 15 points in tiny writing", "bin": 1, "fb": "No more than 10 points. Fifteen breaks the rule, and tiny writing is useless under pressure anyway."}, {"t": "Her phone, switched off, \"for the figures\"", "bin": 1, "fb": "No phones. If a figure matters, it goes on the sheet."}, {"t": "One double-sided A4 sheet with 9 points on the exam topic", "bin": 0, "fb": "One sheet, A4, under 10 points, related to the topic. That is exactly what is permitted for 2027."}, {"t": "A second notes sheet \"just in case\"", "bin": 1, "fb": "Only a single sheet is allowed, even if she never looks at it."}], "win": "Well sorted. A single A4 sheet (both sides), no more than 10 points, related to your topic, and nothing else.", "tutor": [{"h": "Three checks", "p": "<p>One sheet? A4 (both sides allowed)? 10 points or fewer on the topic? All three must be yes.</p>"}, {"h": "Everything else", "p": "<p>Guides, phones, spare sheets: all \"nothing else\".</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Allowed or not? (Foundation)", "items": [{"t": "One double-sided A4 sheet with 9 points", "bin": 0, "fb": "Allowed."}, {"t": "A second notes sheet", "bin": 1, "fb": "Only one sheet."}, {"t": "Her phone", "bin": 1, "fb": "Nothing else is permitted."}], "win": "Good. One sheet, up to 10 points, nothing else."}, "up": {"title": "Allowed in the exam room? (Challenge)", "bloom": "Apply", "intro": "<p>Trickier cases. Shania is packing for Paper 032 (2026 topic: natural disasters, for homeowners). Apply every part of the rule: ONE sheet of A4 paper (both sides allowed), no more than 10 points, related to the topic, and nothing else.</p>", "items": [{"t": "One single sheet of double-sided A4 paper with 10 short points on hurricane preparedness for homeowners", "bin": 0, "fb": "Every part of the rule is met."}, {"t": "One single sheet of double-sided A4 paper with 9 points: six research facts and three planning prompts for her piece", "bin": 0, "fb": "All nine points relate to the topic of her response, and nine is within the limit."}, {"t": "One single sheet of double-sided A4 paper with 10 points that include two statistics and their sources", "bin": 0, "fb": "Short factual points with sources are exactly what the sheet is for."}, {"t": "One 5\" × 8\" card with 8 points", "bin": 1, "fb": "The notes must be on a single sheet of A4 paper. A card breaks the rule even with fewer points."}, {"t": "One single sheet of double-sided A4 paper with 10 points about cyberbullying", "bin": 1, "fb": "The points must relate to the topic of her response, natural disasters."}, {"t": "Sticky notes with extra points attached to the back of her sheet", "bin": 1, "fb": "Extra notes are extra materials. Only the single sheet is allowed."}, {"t": "A pocket dictionary", "bin": 1, "fb": "\"Nothing else\" means nothing else, including a dictionary."}, {"t": "Her smartwatch, switched off", "bin": 1, "fb": "No electronic devices. Anything she needs belongs on the sheet as a short point."}, {"t": "A printed copy of last year's stimulus for practice", "bin": 1, "fb": "The exam stimulus is provided. Nothing else may be brought in."}], "win": "Well applied. Size, number, relevance and \"nothing else\" all matter. Prepare the sheet early and practise expanding each point from memory."}}, {"type": "checkin", "title": "Quick check-in"}, {"type": "mcq", "title": "Which Module is it testing?", "passage": "<p class=\"passage\">A 2026-style stimulus: a newspaper PSA from a Disaster Management Office telling homeowners to register for a free roof inspection before hurricane season. One question reads: <i>\"Explain TWO reasons why the public service announcement uses the dialectal variety and the language register which it does.\"</i></p>", "q": "Which Module and question does this belong to?", "options": [{"t": "Module 1, Question 1", "fb": "Not quite. Question 1 (Module 1) is about receiving information, media and sources. Dialectal variety and register are language ideas."}, {"t": "Section B, Question 3", "fb": "Section B is where you write your own piece and justification. This is a short-answer Section A question about the stimulus."}, {"t": "It is not linked to a Module", "fb": "All three questions cover the three Modules. \"Dialectal variety\" is a clear Module 2 term."}, {"t": "Module 2, Question 2", "ok": true, "fb": "Correct. Dialectal variety (Standard English or Creole) and register (formal or informal) are Module 2 language and community skills, tested in Question 2(a)."}], "tutor": [{"h": "Key words", "p": "<p>\"Dialectal variety\" and \"register\" are language terms. Which Module is about language?</p>"}, {"h": "Map", "p": "<p>Q1 = Module 1 (information, media, sources). Q2 = Module 2 (language). Q3 = production.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Language question: which Module? (Foundation)", "q": "A question asks about the \"dialectal variety and language register\" of a PSA. Which Module is about language?", "options": [{"t": "Module 3", "fb": "Module 3 is mainly about producing communication."}, {"t": "Module 1", "fb": "Module 1 is about information and sources."}, {"t": "Module 2", "ok": true, "fb": "Yes. Language varieties and register are Module 2."}]}, "up": {"title": "Which Module is it testing? (Challenge)", "bloom": "Analyse", "passage": "<p class=\"passage\">Three questions from a 2026-style Paper 032 based on a newspaper PSA about free roof inspections:</p><ol><li>Justify TWO appropriate traditional media, other than the printed medium, for communicating this information to elderly homeowners.</li><li>Create a slogan for the campaign, using ONE literary technique, and explain how the technique works.</li><li>Write a paragraph of 50–100 words justifying the purpose, intended audience and context of communication of your piece.</li></ol>", "q": "Which option correctly matches ALL THREE questions to the Module they test?", "options": [{"t": "(i) Module 1; (ii) Module 2; (iii) Module 3", "ok": true, "fb": "Correct. Choosing media to reach a group is Module 1 (receiving information, media and sources). Slogans and literary techniques sit in Question 2, the Module 2 language question. The justification is part of Section B, Question 3, which draws on Module 3 production skills."}, {"t": "(i) Module 3; (ii) Module 2; (iii) Module 1", "fb": "Justifying media for a target group is Question 1, the Module 1 question, and the justification belongs to Question 3."}, {"t": "(i) Module 1; (ii) Module 3; (iii) Module 3", "fb": "Tempting, because a slogan is something you produce, but in Paper 032 the slogan is Question 2(c), the Module 2 language question."}, {"t": "(i) Module 2; (ii) Module 2; (iii) Module 3", "fb": "Media and their reach are Module 1 ideas, tested in Question 1."}]}}, {"type": "multi", "game": true, "title": "Paper 032 fact check", "intro": "<p>Quick-fire questions on the paper's format.</p>", "items": [{"text": "How long is Paper 032 according to the regulations?", "options": ["2 hours 15 minutes", "1 hour 30 minutes", "3 hours"], "answer": 0, "fb": "The regulations give 2 hours 15 minutes."}, {"text": "How much does it contribute to the final assessment?", "options": ["10 per cent", "20 per cent", "40 per cent"], "answer": 1, "fb": "60 marks, contributing 20 per cent."}, {"text": "How many marks is Section B worth?", "options": ["25", "20", "40"], "answer": 2, "fb": "Section B: piece (25) + justification (15) = 40."}, {"text": "What length is the Section B piece in the specimen?", "options": ["200–250 words", "400–500 words", "100–150 words"], "answer": 0, "fb": "Between 200 and 250 words."}, {"text": "The intended audience for 2027 is...", "options": ["Teenagers", "Parents", "Homeowners"], "answer": 1, "fb": "2027: non-communicable diseases in children, for parents."}], "win": "Spot on. You know the shape of the paper. Now we can build the skills for each question.", "tutor": [{"h": "Memory line", "p": "<p>2 hours 15, 60 marks, 20 per cent, A = 20, B = 40 (25 + 15).</p>"}, {"h": "Our years", "p": "<p>2026 homeowners, 2027 parents.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Fact check (Foundation)", "items": [{"text": "Are all three questions compulsory?", "options": ["Yes", "No"], "answer": 0, "fb": "Yes, all three."}, {"text": "How many notes sheets?", "options": ["Two", "One"], "answer": 1, "fb": "A single sheet."}, {"text": "Justification length?", "options": ["200–250 words", "50–100 words"], "answer": 1, "fb": "50–100 words."}], "win": "Good. All three questions, one sheet, 50–100 word justification."}, "up": {"title": "Paper 032 fact check (Challenge)", "bloom": "Apply", "intro": "<p>Harder questions: apply the format to decisions you will make on the day.</p>", "items": [{"text": "Question 1(b) asks for TWO traditional media OTHER THAN PRINT. Which pair fits?", "options": ["A flyer and a poster", "Instagram and TikTok", "Radio and television", "A newspaper and radio"], "answer": 2, "fb": "Radio and television are traditional and not print. Newspapers, flyers and posters are print; Instagram and TikTok are digital."}, {"text": "If you allocate time in proportion to marks, about how long should Section B take?", "options": ["About 90 minutes", "About 45 minutes", "About 2 hours"], "answer": 0, "fb": "Section B is 40 of 60 marks: two-thirds of 135 minutes is about 90 minutes."}, {"text": "How are the 15 justification marks earned?", "options": ["By explaining how the piece relates to its purpose, intended audience and context of communication", "By naming the three labels in one sentence", "By summarising the piece"], "answer": 0, "fb": "Naming is not enough. The top band explains how the piece connects to each of the three."}, {"text": "The 25-mark Section B piece is marked for...", "options": ["content, research and citation", "creativity, organisation and expression", "creativity and length only"], "answer": 1, "fb": "Creativity (10), organisation (7) and expression (8)."}, {"text": "The question says \"reflective OR expository\". Which piece does NOT fit?", "options": ["A homeowner's journal entry the night after a storm warning", "A poem about a hurricane", "An essay explaining home insurance", "A newspaper feature advising homeowners"], "answer": 1, "fb": "A poem is a creative form. This wording asks for reflective or expository writing."}, {"text": "How many points may your notes sheet hold?", "options": ["Exactly 10", "As many as fit", "No more than 12", "No more than 10"], "answer": 3, "fb": "No more than 10. Fewer is allowed."}, {"text": "Question 2(c) asks for a slogan using ONE literary technique [4 marks]. What earns full marks?", "options": ["The slogan, the technique named, and an explanation of how it works and links to the issue", "The slogan alone", "Three different slogans"], "answer": 0, "fb": "One mark for a relevant slogan, one for the technique, and two for explaining how it works and linking it to the issue."}], "win": "Excellent. You can now use the format to make decisions: what fits the question, how to spend your time and how marks are earned."}}, {"type": "write", "title": "Reflective journal entry: my first look at Paper 032", "genre": "Reflective journal entry", "prompt": "<p>Write a short <b>reflective journal entry</b> (120–220 words) by a CAPE student the evening after a practice Paper 032 on the 2026 topic, <i>natural disasters</i>, for homeowners. Reflect on what surprised you about the format, what was difficult and one change you will make.</p><p><b>Then, in the same box, write a justification of 50–100 words</b> under the heading \"Justification\", explaining the <b>purpose</b>, <b>intended audience</b> and <b>context of communication</b> of your journal entry.</p>", "min": 170, "max": 340, "frame": "<p><b>Date line:</b> e.g. \"Tuesday 14 October, my bedroom, Couva\".</p><p><b>Reflection:</b> what surprised you (Section A short answers? the 200–250 word limit?), what was hard.</p><p><b>Change:</b> one specific thing you will do differently.</p><p><b>Justification:</b> \"The purpose of this entry is to... The intended audience is... The context of communication is...\"</p>", "checklist": ["I wrote a date line and used the first person to reflect honestly.", "I mentioned real features of Paper 032 (sections, marks, notes sheet, justification).", "I named one specific change I will make.", "I wrote a separate 50–100 word justification covering purpose, intended audience and context of communication.", "I checked my Standard English."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "Reflective features (creativity)", "marks": 4, "look": ["\\b(monday|tuesday|wednesday|thursday|friday|saturday|sunday|january|february|march|april|may|june|july|august|september|october|november|december)\\b", "\\bI\\b", "\\b(feel|felt|realised|realized|learnt|learned|wonder|tonight)\\b"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your entry is dated, personal and genuinely reflective. Next step: show what you learnt, not just what happened.", "weak": "A reflective entry needs a date, the first person and real thinking. Try: \"Tuesday 14 October. Tonight I realised that Section A is not an essay at all.\""}, {"criterion": "Content: Paper 032 format", "marks": 4, "look": ["section a|section b", "notes sheet", "justification", "stimulus", "200|250|homeowner|natural disaster"], "min": 2, "strong": "You reflect accurately on the paper's format. Next step: make your \"one change\" specific with a when and a how.", "weak": "Mention real features of the paper, e.g. \"I did not expect Section A to have six short questions worth 20 marks in total.\""}, {"criterion": "Justification (purpose, audience, context)", "marks": 5, "look": ["purpose", "audience", "context", "justification"], "min": 3, "strong": "Your justification names purpose, audience and context clearly. Next step: link your informal register to your audience in one clause.", "weak": "Add 50–100 words naming all three: \"The purpose is to reflect on... The intended audience is myself and my teacher... The context of communication is a private study journal.\""}, {"criterion": "Expression", "marks": 2, "look": ["[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]", ","], "min": 2, "strong": "Clear, controlled Standard English. Next step: vary sentence length to show changing feelings.", "weak": "Check sentence boundaries and grammar. A personal piece is still marked for expression."}], "model": "<p><b>Tuesday 14 October, my bedroom, Couva</b></p><p>Tonight I feel tired but relieved. We sat our first practice Paper 032 today, on natural disasters for homeowners, and it was not what I expected.</p><p>Section A surprised me most. I thought it would be one essay, but it was six short questions about a newspaper notice on roof inspections. I wrote a whole paragraph for a two-mark question and then rushed the four-mark ones. Miss reminded us afterwards: answer for the marks available.</p><p>Section B was harder than it looked. Two hundred to 250 words feels short until you need an introduction, development and a conclusion. My journal entry about Hurricane season ran to 310 words, so I had to cross out a whole paragraph.</p><p>Worst of all, I nearly forgot the justification. It is worth 15 marks, and I gave it three rushed lines.</p><p>My change: in every practice, I will draft the justification straight after planning.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of this journal entry is to reflect honestly on my first Paper 032 practice and plan one improvement. The intended audience is mainly myself, with my Communication Studies teacher as a secondary reader checking my progress. The context of communication is a private study journal kept during CAPE preparation, so the register is informal and personal, though still in Standard English.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>Reflection means thinking about meaning, not just listing events. Keep your justification separate and labelled, and name purpose, intended audience and context in so many words, because examiners should never have to hunt for them.</p>", "easy": {"title": "Reflective journal entry: my first look at Paper 032 (Foundation)", "bloom": "Apply", "genre": "Reflective journal entry (guided)", "prompt": "<p>Write a short <b>journal entry</b> (80–150 words) by a CAPE student the evening after a practice Paper 032 on the 2026 topic, natural disasters, for homeowners. Answer these questions in order:</p><ol><li>What day is it, and how do you feel?</li><li>What surprised you about the paper?</li><li>What was difficult?</li><li>What ONE thing will you do differently next time?</li></ol><p><b>Then</b> write a <b>justification</b> of 50–80 words under the heading \"Justification\". Use the three sentence starters in the frame.</p><p>[10 marks]</p>", "min": 130, "max": 230, "frame": "<p><b>Tuesday 14 October</b><br>Tonight I feel ... because ...<br>I was surprised that ...<br>The hardest part was ...<br>Next time, I will ...</p><p><b>Justification</b><br>The purpose of this journal entry is to ...<br>The intended audience is ...<br>The context of communication is ...</p>", "checklist": ["I wrote a date and used \"I\" to share my thoughts and feelings.", "I mentioned at least one real feature of Paper 032.", "I named one thing I will do differently.", "I wrote a justification with purpose, audience and context.", "I used capital letters and full stops correctly."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "My reflection", "marks": 3, "look": ["\\b(monday|tuesday|wednesday|thursday|friday|saturday|sunday)\\b", "\\bI (feel|felt|was)\\b", "next time|I will"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your entry is dated and personal, and it ends with a plan. Next step: say why you felt the way you did.", "weak": "Start with a date, say how you feel, and finish with \"Next time, I will...\""}, {"criterion": "Paper 032 facts", "marks": 3, "look": ["section a|section b", "notes sheet|justification|stimulus", "200|250|homeowner|natural disaster"], "min": 2, "strong": "You mention real features of the paper. Next step: add one number, such as the 200–250 word limit.", "weak": "Mention real parts of the paper, such as \"Section A has short answers\" or \"the justification is 50–100 words\"."}, {"criterion": "Justification", "marks": 3, "look": ["purpose", "audience", "context"], "min": 3, "strong": "Your justification names purpose, audience and context. Next step: add \"because...\" to one of them.", "weak": "Use all three starters: \"The purpose of this journal entry is to... The intended audience is... The context of communication is...\""}, {"criterion": "Clear writing", "marks": 1, "look": ["[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]"], "min": 1, "strong": "Your sentences are clear and correctly punctuated.", "weak": "Begin each sentence with a capital letter and end it with a full stop."}], "model": "<p><b>Tuesday 14 October</b></p><p>Tonight I feel tired but pleased, because I finished my first practice Paper 032.</p><p>I was surprised that Section A had six short questions instead of one long essay. They were all about one newspaper notice on roof inspections for homeowners.</p><p>The hardest part was Section B. Writing between 200 and 250 words sounds easy, but my piece was too long, and I nearly forgot the justification.</p><p>Next time, I will count my words as I write and plan the justification first.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of this journal entry is to reflect honestly on my first practice paper and plan one improvement for next time. The intended audience is myself and my Communication Studies teacher, who reads my journal each week. The context of communication is my private study journal, written on the evening after the practice paper.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>A journal entry is personal, so use \"I\" and say how you feel. Finish with one clear plan. Keep the justification separate, under its own heading.</p>"}, "up": {"title": "Reflective journal entry: my first look at Paper 032 (Challenge)", "bloom": "Evaluate", "genre": "Reflective journal entry with justification (Section B standard, 40 marks)", "prompt": "<p>Write a <b>reflective journal entry of 200–250 words</b> by a CAPE student the evening after a practice Paper 032 on the 2026 topic, natural disasters, for homeowners. Your entry must:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>open with a vivid moment from the practice paper, not a summary;</li><li><b>evaluate</b> TWO strategies you used (for example, how you used your notes sheet or how you divided your time): what worked, what did not and why;</li><li>reflect on what writing for <b>homeowners</b>, rather than for your teacher, demanded of you;</li><li>end with a specific plan and the insight behind it.</li></ol><p><b>Then</b>, under the heading \"Justification\", write <b>50–100 words</b> explaining how your entry relates to its <b>purpose</b>, <b>intended audience</b> and <b>context of communication</b>.</p><p>[40 marks: piece 25 (Creativity 10, Organisation 7, Expression 8); justification 15 (purpose 5, intended audience 5, context of communication 5)]</p>", "min": 250, "max": 350, "frame": "<p><b>Date line and opening moment:</b> drop the reader into one moment (\"At 10:40 I looked up and realised...\").<br><b>Strategy 1 evaluated:</b> what you did → what happened → why.<br><b>Strategy 2 evaluated:</b> what you did → what happened → why.<br><b>The audience lesson:</b> what homeowners needed that you had not given them.<br><b>Plan and insight:</b> one specific change, and the principle behind it.</p><p><b>Justification:</b> purpose (precise verb) → intended audience (specific) → context (where, when, why this register).</p>", "checklist": ["My entry opens with a vivid moment and stays in a reflective first-person voice.", "I evaluated two strategies: what worked, what did not, and why.", "I reflected on what writing for homeowners demanded.", "I ended with a specific plan and the insight behind it, and my entry is 200–250 words.", "My 50–100 word justification explains how the entry relates to its purpose, intended audience and context of communication."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "Creativity (unique use of genre, audience engagement, originality)", "marks": 10, "look": ["\\b(realised|realized|understood|learnt|learned|noticed|wondered)\\b", "worked|did not work|didn't work|helped|failed|backfired", "homeowner|roof|strap|insurance|mortgage", "\\b(because|which meant|so that)\\b", "next (time|paper|practice)|from now on|I will"], "min": 3, "strong": "Your entry genuinely reflects: it evaluates strategies, discovers something about writing for homeowners and earns its final insight. Next step: make the opening moment even more specific (a time, a sound, a line you wrote).", "weak": "Reflection is more than reporting. Open with one vivid moment, then evaluate: \"Using my notes sheet as a script backfired, because I copied points instead of writing for the reader.\" Show what writing for homeowners demanded (\"they needed costs, not definitions\") and end with a plan you have reasoned your way to."}, {"criterion": "Organisation (introduction, development, conclusion, coherence)", "marks": 7, "look": ["\\b(first|then|later|by the time|afterwards|looking back|tonight|now|but|however)\\b", "\\n\\s*\\n|</p>\\s*<p>"], "min": 1, "strong": "The entry moves from a vivid opening through two evaluated strategies to an earned conclusion. Next step: make sure the final line connects back to the opening moment.", "weak": "Shape the entry: an opening moment, a paragraph for each strategy, a paragraph on the audience lesson and a short closing insight. Link them with time and contrast signals (\"By the time...\", \"Looking back...\", \"However...\")."}, {"criterion": "Expression (register suited to genre, grammar, punctuation, proofreading)", "marks": 8, "look": ["[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]", ",", "[;:—–]"], "min": 2, "strong": "A personal, reflective register in accurate Standard English, with varied sentences that track your changing thoughts. Next step: proofread for one small slip in agreement or punctuation.", "weak": "Keep a personal but accurate register: first person, complete sentences, careful punctuation. Vary sentence length; a short sentence after a long one marks a turning point."}, {"criterion": "Justification: purpose", "marks": 5, "look": ["purpose", "\\bto (reflect|evaluate|plan|identify|record|improve)"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your purpose is precise and clearly connected to what the entry does.", "weak": "State the purpose with a precise verb and connect it to the entry: \"The purpose is to evaluate two exam strategies and plan improvements.\""}, {"criterion": "Justification: intended audience", "marks": 5, "look": ["audience", "myself|teacher|study partner|reader"], "min": 2, "strong": "You identify a specific audience and explain how it shaped your voice.", "weak": "Name the specific audience (yourself, with your teacher as a secondary reader) and explain how that shaped your honest, first-person voice."}, {"criterion": "Justification: context of communication", "marks": 5, "look": ["context", "journal|evening|after the (practice|paper)|study", "register|informal|personal|standard english"], "min": 2, "strong": "You place the entry in a real context and link it to your register.", "weak": "Give the context (a private study journal written the evening after the practice paper) and say how it explains your personal but accurate register."}], "model": "<p><b>Tuesday 14 October, the gallery, Sauteurs</b></p><p>At 10:40 this morning, with thirty-five minutes left, I looked at my Section B draft and realised I had written a textbook, not a newspaper feature.</p><p>My first strategy had been the notes sheet. I had packed it with ten tidy definitions: storm surge, watch, warning. In the exam, I copied them in order, and the piece read like a glossary. A homeowner in Sauteurs does not need a definition of \"storm surge\"; she needs to know whether her house is in its path and what to do this weekend. The sheet worked as a memory aid but failed as a plan, because I wrote it for the examiner, not for the reader.</p><p>My second strategy, timing, worked better. I gave Section A forty minutes and stopped, even though I wanted to polish Question 2. That decision left me time to rescue the piece by cutting two definitions and adding a cost comparison, which is what homeowners actually weigh up.</p><p>What surprised me most is that the audience is not decoration. It decides which facts earn a place.</p><p>Tonight I rewrote my sheet: three facts, one local detail, one cost, one hook. Every point now answers a homeowner's question.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of this journal entry is to evaluate two exam strategies and plan improvements before the next practice paper. The intended audience is myself, with my Communication Studies teacher as a secondary reader monitoring my progress. The context of communication is my private study journal, written the evening after the practice paper, which explains the honest first-person voice and personal yet accurate Standard English.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>At Section B standard, reflection means evaluation: say what worked, what did not and <i>why</i>, then let the insight emerge from that reasoning. Keep the piece within 200–250 words and the justification within 50–100. Split your justification into three clear moves, purpose, audience and context, and connect each one to a choice you made.</p>"}}]}, {"id": "P3U2", "title": "Section A, Question 1: Receiving Information, Media and Sources", "objectives": ["Explain (not just identify) the challenges a target group is likely to face in receiving information.", "Justify appropriate traditional media, other than print, and digital media for a specific audience.", "Comment on a credible local source of information, using the tests of credibility, currency and reliability."], "videos": [{"title": "Barriers to receiving information", "q": "barriers to communication literacy access language examples"}, {"title": "Traditional vs digital media", "q": "traditional media vs digital media advantages disadvantages explained"}, {"title": "Evaluating sources of information", "q": "how to evaluate sources credibility authority currency reliability"}], "screens": [{"type": "learn", "title": "Q1(a): explaining challenges in receiving information", "html": "<p>Question 1 usually begins like this: <i>\"Explain TWO challenges a [target group] is likely to face in receiving this information.\"</i> It is worth <b>4 marks</b>, and the mark logic is simple:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>What you write</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>A challenge that is <b>clearly explained</b> (what the problem is AND why it stops this group receiving the message)</td><td>2 each</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Two challenges <b>identified only</b>, with no explanation</td><td>1 in total</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>So naming \"literacy\" and \"cost\" earns very little. Explaining them earns full marks.</p>\n<p><b>Typical challenges</b> when a message is printed in a newspaper:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Literacy:</b> some people cannot read, so a printed notice is simply unavailable to them.</li>\n<li><b>Register:</b> formal official language may cause comprehension problems even for people who can read.</li>\n<li><b>Distribution and access:</b> some villages or districts are not on the newspaper's delivery route.</li>\n<li><b>Preference for orature:</b> many Caribbean people are more receptive to information that is spoken.</li>\n<li><b>Cost:</b> buying the daily paper may be too expensive for some households.</li>\n<li><b>Digital access:</b> if the message sends people to a website, those without internet, data or digital skills are left out.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Worked example (2026).</b> A PSA in the newspaper tells homeowners to register online for a free roof inspection. Target group: elderly homeowners in a hillside village.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Identified only (weak):</b> \"Literacy and internet.\"<br><b>Clearly explained (2 marks):</b> \"Many elderly homeowners in the village do not use the internet or own a smartphone, so even if they read the notice, they cannot complete the online registration it requires.\"</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Use the pattern <b>challenge + because + effect on this group</b>. Always name the group in your sentence.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "Explain, don't list", "p": "<p>Two marks need a reason. \"Cost\" = 0 to 1. \"The daily paper costs money that some pensioners cannot spare, so they never see the notice\" = 2.</p>"}, {"h": "Pattern", "p": "<p>Challenge + because + effect on this group.</p>"}, {"h": "Common challenges", "p": "<p>Literacy, formal register, distribution, preference for spoken information, cost, no internet.</p>"}], "plain": "<p>Question 1(a) asks you to <b>explain</b> TWO problems a group would have in getting a message. Naming a problem (\"literacy\") earns almost nothing. Say what the problem is <b>and</b> how it stops <b>this</b> group getting the message. For example: \"Many elderly farmers in rural Jamaica do not have internet access, so they cannot register online as the notice asks.\"</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: choose two challenges that are genuinely different.</b> \"No internet\" and \"no smartphone\" are really one access problem; pair an <b>access</b> challenge (distribution, cost, digital access) with a <b>comprehension</b> challenge (literacy, formal register, technical terms, a language the group does not speak). Distinguish receiving the information from acting on it: \"they would not be safe in a flood\" describes a consequence, not a reason they could not receive the message. Anchor every answer in the stimulus and the named group: quote the requirement that creates the problem (\"must register via the website\"). A common error is writing about the audience in general (\"people are busy\") when the question names a specific group with specific circumstances.</p>"}, {"type": "mcq", "title": "Which answer earns 2 marks?", "passage": "<p class=\"passage\">2027-style stimulus: A Ministry of Health PSA in a weekend newspaper tells parents that free blood-sugar screening for children aged 8–12 is available, but appointments must be booked through a new online portal.</p>", "q": "Which response would earn the FULL 2 marks for ONE challenge parents in a low-income district are likely to face in receiving this information?", "options": [{"t": "\"Some parents may not buy the weekend newspaper because of its cost, so they would never see the notice about free screening for their children.\"", "ok": true, "fb": "Full marks. The challenge (cost), the reason and the effect on this specific group are all there: the information never reaches them."}, {"t": "\"The newspaper is a good way to communicate with parents.\"", "fb": "This is not a challenge at all; it praises the medium. Read the command word: you must explain a difficulty in receiving the information."}, {"t": "\"Parents are busy.\"", "fb": "This hints at a challenge but does not explain how being busy stops them receiving the information. Add the link: \"...so many do not have time to read the paper on a working weekend.\""}, {"t": "\"Internet.\"", "fb": "This names a challenge area but does not explain it. At best it contributes to the 1 mark given for identifying two challenges without explanation."}], "tutor": [{"h": "Look for \"so\"", "p": "<p>A full-mark answer usually shows cause and effect: the challenge, then what it does to this group.</p>"}, {"h": "Check the group", "p": "<p>Does the answer mention parents and the screening message? It should.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Explained or just named? (Foundation)", "q": "Which answer EXPLAINS a challenge?", "options": [{"t": "\"Parents are busy.\"", "fb": "It needs a link to receiving the message."}, {"t": "\"Some parents cannot afford the paper, so they never see the notice.\"", "ok": true, "fb": "Yes. It names the problem and shows its effect."}, {"t": "\"Internet.\"", "fb": "That only names the problem."}]}, "up": {"title": "Which answer earns 2 marks? (Challenge)", "bloom": "Evaluate", "passage": "<p class=\"passage\">2026-style stimulus: A Disaster Management Office notice in a daily newspaper tells residents of a low-lying coastal district that flood warnings will now be sent only by text message to phones registered on the Office's website. Target group: Spanish-speaking migrant workers who rent rooms in the district.</p>", "q": "Which response would earn the FULL 2 marks for ONE challenge THIS group is likely to face in RECEIVING the information?", "options": [{"t": "\"The workers may not register their phones, so they would not receive the flood warnings and could be trapped by rising water.\"", "fb": "Close, but this describes what happens if they fail to act. It never explains WHY they would struggle to receive the notice itself, such as language or access to the newspaper."}, {"t": "\"Language and cost.\"", "fb": "Two challenges identified without explanation. Together they could earn only 1 mark."}, {"t": "\"Many of the workers read little English, so even if they see the newspaper notice, they may not understand that they must register their phones to receive the warnings.\"", "ok": true, "fb": "Full marks. The challenge (language), the reason and the effect on this specific group are all present: they cannot understand what the notice asks them to do."}, {"t": "\"Text messages are a fast and cheap way to warn people about floods.\"", "fb": "This praises the medium. The command asks for a challenge in receiving the information."}]}}, {"type": "learn", "title": "Q1(b): justifying media", "html": "<p>Question 1(b) in the specimen asks you to <b>justify TWO appropriate traditional media, other than the printed medium</b>. Note every word: <i>justify</i> (give reasons), <i>traditional</i> (not social media), <i>other than print</i> (no newspaper or flyer). Each medium clearly justified earns <b>2 marks</b>; naming two media without adequate explanation earns only <b>1 mark</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Traditional medium</th><th>Reasons you can use</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Radio</b></td><td>Cheap to own and run; reaches urban and rural areas; available 24/7; works in the background while people cook or drive; message can be repeated many times a day.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Television</b></td><td>Wide reach; visual and aural together make the message more appealing (e.g. showing how to fit hurricane straps); can be repeated.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Film</b></td><td>Can be shown many times at community centres; visual and aural appeal.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Telephone</b></td><td>Short direct messages to those with phones; costly for large audiences.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Word of mouth</b></td><td>Effective for people who prefer orature; fast in small groups; but messages can be distorted, so best backed by another medium.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Mobile loudspeaker</b></td><td>Reaches people where they are (a van through the village); suits oral preference; can suffer distortion.</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>Other versions of the question may ask about <b>digital media</b> (websites, social media, messaging apps). Justify them the same way: reach, cost, speed, interactivity, and whether this audience actually uses them.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Model (2 marks):</b> \"Radio is appropriate because it is cheap to own and reaches every district, including hillside villages; elderly homeowners can hear the roof-inspection message several times a day while they work around the house.\"</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Tie each reason to the target group and the message. A reason that would fit any audience is weaker than one that fits this one.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "Read the limits", "p": "<p>\"Traditional\" and \"other than print\" rule out social media and newspapers.</p>"}, {"h": "Justify = reasons", "p": "<p>Name the medium, then say why it suits this group: reach, cost, oral preference, repetition.</p>"}, {"h": "Common choices", "p": "<p>Radio, television, word of mouth, mobile loudspeaker.</p>"}], "plain": "<p>Question 1(b) asks you to <b>justify</b> TWO <b>traditional</b> media <b>other than print</b>. That means radio, television, film, telephone, word of mouth or a loudspeaker van, but not a newspaper, flyer or social media. Give each one a reason linked to the group. For example: \"Radio suits fishermen in Grenada because they listen on their boats every morning.\"</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: give each medium its own reason, tied to the group AND the message.</b> If both justifications say \"cheap and reaches everyone\", you have really justified one medium twice. Use the strengths that differ: radio repeats and travels into homes and boats; television can <i>demonstrate</i> (how to fit hurricane straps); a loudspeaker van reaches doorsteps and people who prefer spoken information; word of mouth through trusted leaders builds credibility. Strong candidates also know each medium's weakness and how to manage it: word of mouth can distort a message, so back it with radio; a loudspeaker van passes once, so announce the times twice. Read the limits before you write: \"traditional\" and \"other than print\" remove the options many candidates reach for first.</p>"}, {"type": "sort", "game": true, "title": "Does it fit the question?", "intro": "<p>The question asks: <i>\"Justify TWO appropriate <b>traditional</b> media, <b>other than the printed medium</b>, for informing elderly homeowners in a hillside village about free roof inspections.\"</i> Sort each medium.</p>", "main": "Media options", "bins": ["Acceptable answer", "Breaks the question's limits"], "items": [{"t": "An Instagram story", "bin": 1, "fb": "Social media is digital, not traditional, and many elderly homeowners may not use it."}, {"t": "A newspaper advertisement", "bin": 1, "fb": "The question says other than the printed medium. A newspaper is print."}, {"t": "A flyer in the post office", "bin": 1, "fb": "A flyer is printed, so it is excluded."}, {"t": "Word of mouth through church and village leaders", "bin": 0, "fb": "Traditional and suited to people who prefer orature, though best backed up by another medium."}, {"t": "Radio", "bin": 0, "fb": "Traditional, not print, and very well suited to older listeners across the island."}, {"t": "Television", "bin": 0, "fb": "Traditional, not print, with visual and aural appeal for demonstrating repairs."}, {"t": "A mobile loudspeaker van driving through the village", "bin": 0, "fb": "Traditional, oral, and reaches people where they live."}], "win": "Well done. You read the limits of the question: traditional, and other than print. Many candidates lose marks by naming the newspaper or social media.", "tutor": [{"h": "Two filters", "p": "<p>Is it traditional (not internet-based)? Is it something other than print? It must pass both.</p>"}, {"h": "Print family", "p": "<p>Newspapers, flyers, posters and brochures are all print.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Traditional, not print? (Foundation)", "items": [{"t": "Radio", "bin": 0, "fb": "Traditional and not print."}, {"t": "A newspaper ad", "bin": 1, "fb": "Print is excluded."}, {"t": "An Instagram story", "bin": 1, "fb": "Digital, not traditional."}], "win": "Good. Radio passes both filters."}, "up": {"title": "Does it fit the question? (Challenge)", "bloom": "Analyse", "intro": "<p>Harder cases. The question still asks: <i>\"Justify TWO appropriate <b>traditional</b> media, <b>other than the printed medium</b>, for informing elderly homeowners in a hillside village about free roof inspections.\"</i> Some options are disguised. Sort each one.</p>", "items": [{"t": "A morning programme on the national radio station", "bin": 0, "fb": "Traditional and not print."}, {"t": "A television news item showing an inspector checking a roof", "bin": 0, "fb": "Traditional, not print, and able to demonstrate the inspection."}, {"t": "An announcement from the pulpit at the village church", "bin": 0, "fb": "Spoken word of mouth through a trusted leader: traditional and not print."}, {"t": "A short film shown at the community centre", "bin": 0, "fb": "Film is a traditional medium, and a screening reaches people where they gather."}, {"t": "A telephone call from the village council office to each elderly homeowner", "bin": 0, "fb": "The telephone is a traditional medium; calls are direct and personal for a small group."}, {"t": "The radio station's live stream on its mobile app", "bin": 1, "fb": "Same station, but an app stream is digital media, and many elderly homeowners may not use it."}, {"t": "A notice pinned to the church noticeboard", "bin": 1, "fb": "A printed notice is print, wherever it is pinned."}, {"t": "A WhatsApp voice note forwarded to village groups", "bin": 1, "fb": "It is spoken, but it travels through a digital messaging app, so it is not traditional."}, {"t": "A large banner on the main road into the village", "bin": 1, "fb": "A banner is printed material, so it is excluded."}], "win": "Well analysed. Judge by the medium, not the message: a live stream is digital even when the station is traditional, and a voice note is digital even though it is spoken."}}, {"type": "learn", "title": "Q1(c): commenting on a local source of information", "html": "<p>Question 1(c) asks you to <b>comment on ONE local source of information</b> people could consult if they have questions. It is worth <b>2 marks</b>: a satisfactory explanation earns 2; a relevant source identified without adequate explanation earns 1.</p>\n<p><b>Good local sources</b> include authoritative people in the community (health care workers, teachers, religious leaders, Justices of the Peace, political representatives), official government websites where internet access is available, and testimonials from people who have already done the thing (for example a neighbour who had her roof inspected).</p>\n<p>Your Module 1 tests help you comment, not just name:</p>\n<div class=\"trio\">\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Credibility & authority</span><p>Is the person qualified or officially responsible? A district nurse or a building inspector has authority.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Currency & relevance</span><p>Do they have up-to-date information about this programme, in this community?</p></div>\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reliability</span><p>Is their information accurate and consistent with official sources? A rumour on a group chat fails this test.</p></div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Model (2 marks, 2027):</b> \"Parents could consult the nurse at their local community health clinic. She is trained, works directly with the Ministry's screening programme, and can answer questions about safety face to face in language parents understand.\"</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Ask yourself the pair-teaching question: \"What evidence supports your answer?\" Here, the evidence is the source's training, role and access to official information.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "Name + why", "p": "<p>Name the source, then say why people can trust it: authority, up-to-date knowledge, reliability.</p>"}, {"h": "Local means local", "p": "<p>A clinic nurse, a pastor, a teacher, a building inspector, an official website.</p>"}], "plain": "<p>Question 1(c) asks you to <b>comment on</b> ONE local person or place people could go to with questions. Name the source <b>and</b> say why it can be trusted. For example: \"Homeowners in Castries could ask the district building inspector, who is trained and runs the inspection programme, so his answers are accurate.\"</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: comment on credibility AND accessibility.</b> A source earns its place only if it is trustworthy (trained, official, up to date with this programme) and reachable by this group (nearby, available, speaks their language, trusted by them). A government website may be authoritative but useless to homeowners without internet; a village pastor may be trusted and accessible but needs to be briefed so that his answers are accurate. Testimonials from people who have already used the service add credibility through experience. Use your Module 1 criteria by name (credibility and authority, currency and relevance, reliability), because they show the examiner you are evaluating, not just naming. Avoid rumour-based sources such as group chats.</p>"}, {"type": "learn", "title": "Researching and evaluating sources for Section A", "html": "<p>Question 1(c) rewards a source you have <b>tested</b>, not just named. Because you know the year's topic and target group in advance, you can research local sources before the exam and carry the best of them into the room on your notes sheet.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1: research.</b> Gather information from a range of sources about the topic, the target group and the place: official guides and websites, community organisations, trained professionals and people with first-hand experience.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2: evaluate each source with the Module 1 tests.</b></p>\n<div class=\"trio\">\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Credibility & authority</span><p>Who produced it? Are they trained, qualified or officially responsible? A disaster management agency's preparedness guide passes; an anonymous voice note about \"hurricane-proof paint\" does not.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Currency & relevance</span><p>Is it recent, and is it about this topic, this place and this audience?</p></div>\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Reliability</span><p>Is it evidence-based and consistent with other good sources?</p></div>\n</div>\n<p><b>Step 3: check accessibility.</b> A source only helps the target group if they can reach it and understand it: nearby, available, in a language and register they use, and trusted by them. An official website may be authoritative but useless to homeowners without internet.</p>\n<p><b>Step 4: prepare it for the notes sheet.</b> Section A points are allowed on your sheet. Keep them short, for example: <i>Media: radio, loudspeaker van, word of mouth · Sources: district building inspector, community centre</i>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Weak 1(c) answer (1 mark)</th><th>Strong 1(c) answer (2 marks)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>\"They could ask the internet.\"</td><td>\"Homeowners could consult the district building inspector, who is trained, runs the inspection programme and can explain the requirements face to face.\"</td></tr></table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Use the Module 1 criteria by name (credibility and authority, currency and relevance, reliability). Naming the test shows the examiner you are evaluating the source, not just listing it.</p>", "plain": "<p>Before the exam, find local people and places the target group could ask for help. Test each one: Is it trustworthy? Is it up to date? Is it about this topic and place? Can these people actually reach it? Write the best one or two on your notes sheet so you can explain them in Question 1(c).</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: rank your sources.</b> For one past topic, list four possible local sources and score each out of 3 for credibility, currency and accessibility to the target group. Your highest-scoring source is your 1(c) answer; your second becomes a back-up in case the stimulus rules the first one out. Explain in one sentence why the lowest-scoring source fails.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "Test, then name", "p": "<p>Credibility and authority, currency and relevance, reliability, then accessibility for this group.</p>"}, {"h": "Carry it in", "p": "<p>Put one or two tested sources on your notes sheet in a few words.</p>"}]}, {"type": "checkin", "title": "Quick check-in"}, {"type": "pick", "game": true, "title": "Spot the full-mark answers", "intro": "<p>Six students answered Q1 on a 2026 stimulus (roof inspections, elderly homeowners in a hillside village). Tap the <b>THREE</b> answers that are clearly explained and would earn full marks for their part.</p>", "q": "Tap the three fully explained answers.", "lines": ["(a) \"Literacy.\"", "(a) \"Some elderly homeowners cannot read well, so a printed notice in the newspaper would not reach them at all.\"", "(b) \"Radio and Facebook.\"", "(b) \"A mobile loudspeaker van is appropriate because it brings the message to people's doorsteps in the village and suits older residents who prefer spoken information.\"", "(c) \"The internet.\"", "(c) \"Homeowners could ask the district building inspector, who is officially trained and knows the current inspection programme, so the information is reliable.\""], "correct": [1, 3, 5], "win": "Exactly. Each full-mark answer names the point AND explains it in relation to the target group. That is the difference between 1 mark and 2.", "miss": "Look for answers that give a reason linked to the elderly homeowners. One-word answers are only identification, and Facebook is not a traditional medium.", "tutor": [{"h": "One word = identify", "p": "<p>\"Literacy\", \"The internet\" and \"Radio and Facebook\" give no reasons.</p>"}, {"h": "Reason + group", "p": "<p>Full marks link the point to the target group: \"so a printed notice would not reach them\".</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Spot the explained answers (Foundation)", "intro": "<p>Tap the <b>TWO</b> answers that are explained.</p>", "q": "Tap the two explained answers.", "lines": ["\"Literacy.\"", "\"Some elderly homeowners cannot read well, so the printed notice would not reach them.\"", "\"The internet.\"", "\"Radio suits them because it is cheap and reaches every village, many times a day.\""], "correct": [1, 3], "win": "Good. Both give a reason linked to the group.", "miss": "Look for \"so\" or \"because\"."}, "up": {"title": "Spot the full-mark answers (Challenge)", "bloom": "Evaluate", "intro": "<p>Eight answers to Question 1 on the roof-inspection stimulus (target group: elderly homeowners in a hillside village). Some look complete but miss something.</p>", "q": "Tap EVERY answer that would earn full marks for its part.", "lines": ["(a) \"The notice requires online registration, but many elderly homeowners in the village have no smartphone or internet access, so they cannot act on it even if they read it.\"", "(a) \"Some people do not like reading.\"", "(a) \"The notice uses formal terms such as 'proof of ownership', which may confuse older residents with little schooling, so they may not understand what they must bring.\"", "(b) \"Television is appropriate because it is a good medium that many people watch.\"", "(b) \"Radio is appropriate because most elderly villagers keep a radio on at home during the day, so they would hear the inspection dates repeated several times.\"", "(b) \"A loudspeaker van and a flyer at the village shop.\"", "(c) \"They could ask the pastor at the village church, whom many elderly residents trust and see every week, if the Office briefs him so that his answers are accurate.\"", "(c) \"They could check what neighbours are saying in the village WhatsApp group.\""], "correct": [0, 2, 4, 6], "win": "Exactly. The four full-mark answers each explain the point AND tie it to elderly homeowners in the village. The television answer gives a reason that would fit any audience; the van and flyer answer includes print and has no reasons; and a WhatsApp group is neither reliable nor likely to reach this group.", "miss": "For each answer, ask: Is there a reason? Is the reason about THIS group? Does it obey the question's limits? A reason that fits any audience, a printed medium or a rumour-based source does not earn full marks."}}, {"type": "write", "title": "Section A practice: Question 1 short answers", "genre": "Short-answer responses + justification", "stimulus": "<blockquote class=\"passage\"><p><b>PUBLIC SERVICE ANNOUNCEMENT</b> (published in a daily newspaper)</p><p>The Disaster Management Office (an agency) wishes to inform homeowners that free roof and window inspections will be conducted from 1 to 31 May, ahead of the hurricane season. Homeowners must register via the Office's website by uploading a photograph of their property and proof of ownership. Approved households may qualify for a subsidy towards hurricane straps and shutters. Homeowners without internet access may register in person at their district community centre on Saturdays between 9:00 a.m. and noon.</p></blockquote>", "prompt": "<p>Answer these Question 1-style parts on the stimulus. The target group is <b>elderly homeowners in a hillside village</b>.</p><ol><li><b>(a)</b> Explain TWO challenges they are likely to face in receiving this information. [4]</li><li><b>(b)</b> Justify TWO appropriate traditional media, other than the printed medium, for communicating this information to them. [4]</li><li><b>(c)</b> Comment on ONE local source of information they could consult with questions about the subsidy. [2]</li></ol><p><b>Then write a justification of 50–100 words</b> under the heading \"Justification\" for a radio announcement you would broadcast to this group: explain its <b>purpose</b>, <b>intended audience</b> and <b>context of communication</b>.</p>", "min": 150, "max": 330, "frame": "<p><b>(a)</b> Challenge 1: ... because ... so ... / Challenge 2: ...</p><p><b>(b)</b> Medium 1 is appropriate because ... / Medium 2 ...</p><p><b>(c)</b> They could consult ..., who ... so ...</p><p><b>Justification:</b> \"The purpose of the radio announcement is to... The intended audience is... The context of communication is...\"</p>", "checklist": ["In (a) I explained two challenges, each linked to elderly homeowners.", "In (b) I chose two traditional media that are NOT print, and gave reasons for each.", "In (c) I named one local source and explained why it can be trusted.", "I wrote a 50–100 word justification covering purpose, intended audience and context of communication.", "I answered for the marks available, without padding."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "(a) Challenges explained", "marks": 4, "look": ["\\b(because|so|therefore|as a result|cannot|unable)\\b", "internet|online|website|read|literacy|cost|afford|formal|distribution|deliver", "elderly|older|homeowner|village"], "min": 2, "strong": "You explained your challenges and linked them to elderly homeowners, which is what earns 2 marks each. Next step: make sure your two challenges are genuinely different (e.g. access vs comprehension).", "weak": "Identifying a challenge earns little; you must explain it. Use the pattern challenge + because + effect: \"Many elderly homeowners do not use the internet, so they cannot complete the online registration the notice requires.\""}, {"criterion": "(b) Traditional media justified", "marks": 4, "look": ["radio|television|\\btv\\b|loudspeaker|word of mouth|telephone|film", "\\b(because|appropriate|suitable|reach|cheap|repeat|oral|spoken)"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your media are traditional, not print, and justified with reasons that fit the group. Next step: mention a weakness and how you would manage it (e.g. word of mouth can distort, so back it with radio).", "weak": "Name two traditional media other than print and give a reason for each: \"Radio is appropriate because it is cheap, reaches every district and can repeat the message several times a day.\" Avoid newspapers, flyers and social media."}, {"criterion": "(c) Local source commented on", "marks": 2, "look": ["inspector|nurse|pastor|priest|teacher|community centre|justice of the peace|representative|official website|neighbour", "\\b(trained|official|qualified|reliable|trust|knows|accurate|up.to.date)"], "min": 2, "strong": "You named a credible local source and explained why it can be trusted. Next step: say how people would actually reach this source.", "weak": "Name one local source and comment on it: \"They could consult the district building inspector, who is officially trained and knows the subsidy rules.\""}, {"criterion": "Justification (purpose, audience, context)", "marks": 5, "look": ["purpose", "audience", "context", "radio|broadcast|programme"], "min": 3, "strong": "Your justification for the radio announcement is complete and specific. Next step: name the programme slot and why it suits older listeners.", "weak": "Write 50–100 words naming all three: \"The purpose is to inform elderly homeowners... The intended audience is... The context of communication is a morning radio programme popular with older listeners.\""}], "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> First, many elderly homeowners in the village do not use the internet or own a smartphone, so even if they read the notice, they cannot complete the online registration it requires. Second, the notice uses a formal register with terms such as \"proof of ownership\" and \"subsidy\", which may confuse readers with limited schooling, so they may not understand what they must do.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Radio is appropriate because it is cheap to own, reaches hillside districts, and can repeat the message several times a day while homeowners work around the house. A mobile loudspeaker van is also appropriate because it brings the message to people's doorsteps and suits older residents who prefer spoken information; it could announce the Saturday registration times directly in the village.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Homeowners could consult the district building inspector, who is officially trained and works with the inspection programme, so the information about the subsidy would be accurate and current.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of the radio announcement is to inform elderly homeowners about the free inspections and persuade them to register in person on Saturdays. The intended audience is homeowners over sixty in hillside villages, many without internet access. The context of communication is a short announcement during a popular morning radio programme, repeated daily in April, using simple Standard English and a warm, respectful tone.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>Section A rewards precision, not length. Answer for the marks: two clearly explained points for a 4-mark part, one well-explained point for a 2-mark part. Always obey the limits in the question, such as \"traditional\" and \"other than print\", and always link your reasons to the named target group.</p>", "easy": {"title": "Section A practice: Question 1 short answers (Foundation)", "bloom": "Apply", "genre": "Short answers (guided)", "prompt": "<p>Answer these parts on the stimulus. The target group is <b>elderly homeowners in a hillside village</b>. Use the sentence frames.</p><ol><li><b>(a)</b> Explain ONE challenge they would face in receiving this information. [2]</li><li><b>(b)</b> Justify ONE traditional medium, NOT print and NOT social media, for reaching them. [2]</li><li><b>(c)</b> Name ONE local person they could ask about the subsidy, and say why that person can be trusted. [2]</li></ol><p><b>Then</b> write a short <b>justification</b> (50–80 words) for a radio announcement to this group, using the three starters. [3]</p><p>[Total: 9 marks]</p>", "min": 80, "max": 200, "frame": "<p><b>(a)</b> Many elderly homeowners ... so they ...<br><b>(b)</b> ... is a good choice because ...<br><b>(c)</b> They could ask ..., who ... so ...</p><p><b>Justification</b><br>The purpose of the radio announcement is to ...<br>The intended audience is ...<br>The context of communication is ...</p>", "checklist": ["In (a) I used \"so\" to show how the challenge affects elderly homeowners.", "In (b) I chose radio, television, a loudspeaker van or word of mouth, and gave a reason.", "In (c) I named a local person and said why they can be trusted.", "My justification has purpose, audience and context."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "(a) One challenge explained", "marks": 2, "look": ["internet|online|website|read|formal|cost|afford|deliver", "\\b(so|because|cannot|unable)\\b"], "min": 2, "strong": "You named a challenge and showed its effect on the group.", "weak": "Name the problem and show its effect: \"Many elderly homeowners do not use the internet, so they cannot register online.\""}, {"criterion": "(b) One medium justified", "marks": 2, "look": ["radio|television|\\btv\\b|loudspeaker|word of mouth|telephone|film", "\\b(because|reach|repeat|hear|spoken)\\b"], "min": 2, "strong": "You chose a traditional medium and gave a reason.", "weak": "Choose radio, television, a loudspeaker van or word of mouth, then add \"because...\""}, {"criterion": "(c) One local source", "marks": 2, "look": ["inspector|nurse|pastor|priest|teacher|community centre|justice of the peace|representative|neighbour", "\\b(trained|official|trust|knows|reliable)\\b"], "min": 2, "strong": "You named a local source and said why it can be trusted.", "weak": "Name one person, such as the district building inspector, and say why: \"who is trained and knows the subsidy rules\"."}, {"criterion": "Justification", "marks": 3, "look": ["purpose", "audience", "context"], "min": 3, "strong": "All three parts of the justification are there.", "weak": "Use all three starters: purpose, intended audience and context of communication."}], "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> Many elderly homeowners in the village do not use the internet, so they cannot register online as the notice asks.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Radio is a good choice because most elderly villagers listen at home every day, so they would hear the message many times.</p><p><b>(c)</b> They could ask the district building inspector, who is trained and works on the programme, so his answers are accurate.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of the radio announcement is to tell elderly homeowners about the free roof inspections and how to register in person. The intended audience is homeowners over sixty in hillside villages, many without internet. The context of communication is a short announcement on a morning radio programme in April.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>Every answer needs a reason. Use \"so\" or \"because\" in each part, and always name the group: elderly homeowners in the village.</p>"}, "up": {"title": "Section A practice: Question 1 short answers (Challenge)", "bloom": "Evaluate", "genre": "Short-answer responses with evaluation + justification", "prompt": "<p>Answer these parts on the stimulus. The target group is now harder to reach: <b>elderly homeowners in a hillside village, some of whom speak mainly Haitian Creole</b>.</p><ol><li><b>(a)</b> Explain TWO different challenges (one about <b>access</b>, one about <b>understanding</b>) they are likely to face in receiving this information. [4]</li><li><b>(b)</b> Justify TWO appropriate traditional media, other than the printed medium, giving each its OWN reason linked to this group. [4]</li><li><b>(c)</b> Comment on ONE local source of information about the subsidy, evaluating BOTH its credibility and how accessible it is to this group. [2]</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Evaluate</b> which of your two media would be MORE effective for this group, and explain how you would manage ONE limitation of the other. [4]</li></ol><p><b>Then</b>, under the heading \"Justification\", write <b>50–100 words</b> for a radio announcement to this group, explaining its <b>purpose</b>, <b>intended audience</b> and <b>context of communication</b>, including your choice of language. [6]</p><p>[Total: 20 marks]</p>", "min": 220, "max": 400, "frame": "<p><b>(a)</b> Access: ... because ... so ... / Understanding: ... because ... so ...</p><p><b>(b)</b> Medium 1 ... because (a reason only this medium offers) ... / Medium 2 ... because ...</p><p><b>(c)</b> ... is credible because ... and accessible because ...</p><p><b>(d)</b> ... would be more effective because ... However, ... so I would ...</p><p><b>Justification:</b> purpose → specific audience → context, including the language(s) used and why.</p>", "checklist": ["In (a) one challenge is about access and the other about understanding, each explained for this group.", "In (b) each traditional medium has its own reason; neither is print or digital.", "In (c) I evaluated credibility AND accessibility.", "In (d) I judged which medium is more effective and managed a limitation of the other.", "My 50–100 word justification explains purpose, audience and context, including my language choice."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "(a) Two different challenges explained", "marks": 4, "look": ["internet|online|website|smartphone|deliver|distribution|cost|afford|travel", "creole|french|english|language|formal|terms|read|literacy", "\\b(so|because|therefore|cannot|unable)\\b", "elderly|older|homeowner|village"], "min": 3, "strong": "You explained two genuinely different challenges, access and understanding, and tied both to this group. Next step: quote the requirement in the notice that creates each problem.", "weak": "Make the two challenges different: one about access (\"no internet, so they cannot register online\") and one about understanding (\"the notice is only in formal English, so Creole-speaking homeowners may not grasp what documents to bring\")."}, {"criterion": "(b) Two traditional media, each with its own reason", "marks": 4, "look": ["radio|television|\\btv\\b|loudspeaker|word of mouth|telephone|film|pulpit|church", "creole|both languages|bilingual|translat", "demonstrat|doorstep|repeat|trusted|face to face"], "min": 2, "strong": "Each medium has a reason only it can offer, linked to this group, including the language need. Next step: mention timing (when the group would hear it).", "weak": "Give each medium a different reason: \"Radio can broadcast the message in both English and Haitian Creole several times a day\" and \"A loudspeaker van brings it to the doorsteps of homeowners who rarely travel down the hill.\" Avoid repeating \"cheap and reaches everyone\"."}, {"criterion": "(c) Source: credibility and accessibility", "marks": 2, "look": ["inspector|nurse|pastor|priest|community centre|justice of the peace|representative|interpreter", "\\b(trained|official|qualified|reliable|accurate|briefed)\\b", "nearby|in the village|speaks|visit|weekly|every|reach"], "min": 2, "strong": "You judged both whether the source can be trusted and whether this group can actually reach it.", "weak": "Comment on both sides: why the source is credible (trained, official, briefed) AND why this group can reach it (in the village, speaks Creole, available on Saturdays)."}, {"criterion": "(d) Evaluation", "marks": 4, "look": ["more effective|better|stronger|outweigh", "however|but|although|limitation|weakness|disadvantage", "\\b(so I would|to manage|to overcome|by)\\b"], "min": 2, "strong": "You made a reasoned judgement and offered a practical way to manage the other medium's limitation. Next step: base your judgement on one feature of the group.", "weak": "Make a judgement (\"Radio would be more effective because...\") and then deal with the other medium's weakness (\"However, the van passes only once, so I would send it on two Saturdays and announce the registration times twice\")."}, {"criterion": "Justification (purpose, audience, context)", "marks": 6, "look": ["purpose", "audience", "context", "creole|english|bilingual|language|register"], "min": 3, "strong": "Your justification connects purpose, a precise audience and a real context, and explains your language choice. Next step: name the programme slot.", "weak": "Write 50–100 words explaining all three and your language choice: \"...The context of communication is a morning programme on community radio, with the announcement read in English and repeated in Haitian Creole, so that every homeowner understands how to register.\""}], "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> First, an access challenge: the notice requires registration on the Office's website, but many elderly homeowners in the village have no smartphone or internet, so they cannot act on it even if they read it. Second, an understanding challenge: the notice is written only in formal English, so homeowners who speak mainly Haitian Creole may not grasp what \"proof of ownership\" means or what they must bring.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Radio is appropriate because the announcement can be broadcast in both English and Haitian Creole several times a day, reaching homeowners at home while they work. A mobile loudspeaker van is also appropriate because it brings the message to the doorsteps of older residents who rarely travel down the hill, and its bilingual announcement can give the Saturday registration times directly.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Homeowners could consult the district building inspector at the community centre on Saturdays. He is credible because he is trained and runs the inspection programme, and accessible because he is in the village each week; with a Creole-speaking volunteer beside him, every homeowner could ask questions.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Radio would be more effective because it repeats the message daily and reaches homes across the whole hillside. However, the van passes only once, so I would send it on two Saturdays and have it stop outside the church after services.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of the radio announcement is to inform elderly homeowners about the free inspections and persuade them to register in person. The intended audience is homeowners over sixty in hillside villages, some speaking mainly Haitian Creole and many without internet. The context of communication is a morning community radio programme in April, read in Standard English and repeated in Haitian Creole, so that every listener understands how and where to register.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>At this level, precision and evaluation earn the marks. Make your two challenges genuinely different, give each medium a reason only it can offer, and judge rather than describe in (d). Keep the justification within 50–100 words and explain your language choice for a bilingual audience.</p>"}}]}, {"id": "P3U3", "title": "Section A, Question 2: Variety, Register, Message Points and Slogans", "objectives": ["Identify and explain the dialectal variety and language register a message uses.", "Select the TWO most important message points for a specific audience.", "Create a slogan using ONE named literary technique and explain how it works."], "videos": [{"title": "Language register explained", "q": "language register formal informal explained examples"}, {"title": "Caribbean creole continuum", "q": "Caribbean creole continuum acrolect mesolect basilect explained"}, {"title": "Literary devices in slogans", "q": "literary devices in advertising slogans rhyme alliteration pun allusion"}], "screens": [{"type": "learn", "title": "Q2(a): dialectal variety and register", "html": "<p>Question 2(a) in the specimen asks: <i>\"Explain TWO reasons why the public service announcement uses the dialectal variety and the language register which it does.\"</i> It is worth <b>4 marks</b>:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>What you write</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Correctly identify the <b>dialectal variety</b> (e.g. Standard English / acrolect) + an acceptable explanation</td><td>1 + 1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Correctly identify the <b>register</b> (e.g. formal) + an acceptable explanation</td><td>1 + 1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Identify both, without explanation</td><td>1 only</td></tr></table></div>\n<p><b>Reasons for Standard English (acrolect) in an official PSA:</b></p>\n<ul><li>It is the official variety supported by government for official communication.</li>\n<li>It is associated with seriousness when messaging the public.</li>\n<li>There is an assumption that everyone understands Standard English, even those who do not speak it.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Reasons for a formal register:</b></p>\n<ul><li>It conveys the seriousness of the message.</li>\n<li>Communication from government is formal and official, not casual.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Model (4 marks, 2026):</b> \"The PSA uses Standard English, the acrolect, because it is the official variety the government uses for public communication, which gives the notice authority. It uses a formal register because the message comes from an official agency about a serious matter, the safety of homes before hurricane season, so a casual tone would undermine it.\"</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Name the variety AND the register, then give a reason for each. Two separate sentences make it easy for the examiner to award all four marks.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "Two labels", "p": "<p>Variety = Standard English or Creole. Register = formal or informal.</p>"}, {"h": "Two reasons", "p": "<p>Standard English: official, serious, widely understood. Formal: serious message, official sender.</p>"}, {"h": "Marks", "p": "<p>Identify (1) + explain (1), twice. Identifying only = 1 mark total.</p>"}], "plain": "<p>Question 2(a) asks <b>why</b> a message uses its language variety and its register. First <b>name</b> them: the variety is usually <b>Standard English</b> (the acrolect) and the register is usually <b>formal</b>. Then give a <b>reason</b> for each. For example: a Barbados government notice uses Standard English because it is the official language of government, and a formal register because the subject is serious.</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: do not assume, read the evidence.</b> Not every official message is formal. A disaster office social media post that says \"Neighbours, have you checked your roof straps yet?\" is still Standard English, but its register is informal or consultative, chosen to sound friendly and build connection. Identify the register from the language itself: direct address, questions, contractions and greetings lower the register; passive voice (\"Homeowners are advised\"), official vocabulary and long, complete sentences raise it. Your reasons should link to the <b>source</b> (a government agency), the <b>purpose</b> (urgency, authority) and the <b>audience</b> (a mixed public that understands Standard English). A common error is giving one reason for both labels, which earns only half the explanation marks.</p>"}, {"type": "mcq", "title": "Which answer earns 4 marks?", "passage": "<p class=\"passage\">2027-style stimulus: a Ministry of Health PSA reads, \"Parents and guardians are advised that free blood-glucose screening for children aged 8–12 will be available at all community clinics from 2 March. Appointments must be scheduled in advance.\"</p>", "q": "Which answer to Q2(a) would earn the FULL 4 marks?", "options": [{"t": "\"It uses Standard English because that is the official variety used by government, giving the notice authority. It uses a formal register because a ministry is addressing parents about a serious health matter, and a casual tone would make it seem less important.\"", "ok": true, "fb": "Full marks. The variety and the register are each identified and each given a clear, relevant reason."}, {"t": "\"It uses Creole so that parents feel at home.\"", "fb": "This misidentifies the variety. \"Parents and guardians are advised\" is Standard English, the acrolect, not Creole."}, {"t": "\"The language is good and easy to understand.\"", "fb": "Too vague. It names neither the dialectal variety nor the register, so it cannot earn the identification marks."}, {"t": "\"It uses Standard English and a formal register.\"", "fb": "Both are correctly identified, but there is no explanation, so this earns only 1 mark."}], "tutor": [{"h": "Check two boxes", "p": "<p>Does the answer name the variety AND the register?</p>"}, {"h": "Then two reasons", "p": "<p>Does each one have a \"because\"?</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Identified or explained? (Foundation)", "q": "Which answer explains BOTH the variety and the register?", "options": [{"t": "\"It is Creole.\"", "fb": "The PSA is in Standard English."}, {"t": "\"Standard English and formal.\"", "fb": "Named only, no reasons."}, {"t": "\"Standard English because it is the official government variety; formal because the health message is serious.\"", "ok": true, "fb": "Yes. Each label has a reason."}]}, "up": {"title": "Which answer earns 4 marks? (Challenge)", "bloom": "Analyse", "passage": "<p class=\"passage\">2026-style stimulus: a Disaster Management Office post on its public page reads, \"Neighbours, hurricane season starts 1 June. Have you checked your roof straps yet? Book your free inspection today. It only takes ten minutes, and it could save your home.\"</p>", "q": "Which answer to Q2(a) would earn the FULL 4 marks for this post?", "options": [{"t": "\"The post uses Standard English and a formal register because it comes from a government office.\"", "fb": "Standard English is right, but the register is not formal: \"Neighbours\" and a direct question are friendly, informal features. One reason is also stretched to cover both labels."}, {"t": "\"The post uses Creole and an informal register, because 'Neighbours' is a Caribbean greeting.\"", "fb": "The register is right, but the variety is wrong. Every sentence follows Standard English grammar; a friendly greeting does not make it Creole."}, {"t": "\"The post uses Standard English because it is the official variety, understood by the whole mixed public. It uses an informal, friendly register, addressing readers as 'Neighbours' and asking a direct question, to build a sense of community so that homeowners feel personally invited to act.\"", "ok": true, "fb": "Full marks. Both labels are correct and each has its own reason. The answer reads the register from the evidence (the greeting and the question) instead of assuming that a government post must be formal."}, {"t": "\"The post uses Standard English and an informal register.\"", "fb": "Both are correctly identified, but neither is explained, so this earns only 1 mark."}]}}, {"type": "learn", "title": "Creole and Standard English: deliberate choices", "html": "<p>Module 2 taught you that Caribbean Creole is a full, rule-governed language. In Paper 032 you need to explain why a message uses a variety, and in your own writing you need to <b>choose</b> a variety deliberately.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Standard English suits...</th><th>Creole (or a touch of it) suits...</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Official notices, essays, news articles, your justification</td><td>A character's voice, a radio jingle, a slogan meant to feel local and warm</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Formal or mixed audiences (ministries, visitors)</td><td>Building identity and connection with a local audience</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Serious, authoritative messages</td><td>Humour, intimacy, everyday reality</td></tr></table></div>\n<p><b>Example.</b> A 2026 radio jingle for homeowners might say: <i>\"Before de breeze start blow, check yuh roof, yuh know!\"</i> Creole here is deliberate: it is catchy, local and suits radio. But the official PSA from the same office must be in Standard English.</p>\n<p>Creole becomes a problem only when it slips in <b>by accident</b> where Standard English is needed: \"Homeowners does need to register\" in a formal article. That costs expression marks.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> If you use Creole in your Section B piece, say so in your justification and say why. That shows the examiner it was a deliberate choice for your audience.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "Deliberate, not accidental", "p": "<p>Creole in a jingle or a character's speech can be a strength. Creole slipping into a formal article is an error.</p>"}, {"h": "Explain it", "p": "<p>Mention deliberate Creole in your justification.</p>"}], "plain": "<p>Creole and Standard English are both real languages, and each suits different jobs. <b>Standard English</b> suits official notices, essays and your justification. <b>Creole</b> can make a radio jingle or a character's voice feel local and warm. What matters is that your choice is <b>deliberate</b>. A Trinidadian jingle saying \"Doh wait for de breeze!\" is clever; \"Homeowners does need to register\" in a formal article is a mistake.</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: justify the choice through audience and purpose.</b> Creole can build identity, intimacy and trust with a local audience, and it can make a message memorable. Standard English carries authority and is understood across territories and by visitors. Skilled communicators switch deliberately: a campaign may use Standard English for its official notice and Creole for its radio jingle, because the media and purposes differ. In Section B, Creole is most effective inside dialogue or a quoted voice, with the narration in Standard English. If you use it, name it in your justification and explain the effect (\"The grandmother's Creole makes her voice authentic for local homeowners\"). The error examiners penalise is not Creole itself but accidental Creole grammar in a piece that requires Standard English.</p>"}, {"type": "multi", "game": true, "title": "Variety and register check", "intro": "<p>For each 2026 message aimed at homeowners, choose the BEST description.</p>", "items": [{"text": "\"Homeowners are reminded that hurricane shutters must be installed before an official warning is issued.\"", "options": ["Standard English, intimate", "Standard English, formal", "Creole, informal"], "answer": 1, "fb": "Passive voice, official vocabulary and no contractions: Standard English in a formal register."}, {"text": "\"Ay, neighbour! Yuh roof tie down good? Storm season reach!\"", "options": ["Creole, informal", "Standard English, formal", "Standard English, frozen"], "answer": 0, "fb": "\"Yuh\", \"tie down good\" and \"reach\" are Creole features, and the greeting is casual."}, {"text": "\"Hey homeowners! Got your hurricane kit ready? Here are five quick checks.\"", "options": ["Creole, formal", "Creole, informal", "Standard English, informal"], "answer": 2, "fb": "The grammar is Standard English, but the tone is chatty and friendly: informal register."}, {"text": "Why would a ministry use the FIRST message's style?", "options": ["It is official communication on a serious matter", "It wants to sound like a friend", "Creole is not allowed on paper"], "answer": 0, "fb": "Government communication is official and serious, so it uses Standard English in a formal register."}], "win": "Very good. Variety (Standard English or Creole) and register (formal or informal) are separate choices. A message can be Standard English yet informal.", "tutor": [{"h": "Two separate questions", "p": "<p>Variety: what grammar and words? Register: how formal is the tone?</p>"}, {"h": "Example", "p": "<p>\"Hey homeowners! Got your kit ready?\" is Standard English but informal.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Variety and register (Foundation)", "items": [{"text": "\"Homeowners are reminded that shutters must be installed.\"", "options": ["Standard English, formal", "Creole, informal"], "answer": 0, "fb": "Official wording: Standard English, formal."}, {"text": "\"Yuh roof tie down good?\"", "options": ["Creole, informal", "Standard English, formal"], "answer": 0, "fb": "\"Yuh\" and \"tie down good\" are Creole; the tone is casual."}], "win": "Good. You can tell official from casual."}, "up": {"title": "Variety and register check (Challenge)", "bloom": "Analyse", "intro": "<p>Harder judgements. Variety and register are separate choices, and the reasons behind them matter.</p>", "items": [{"text": "A neighbour calls over the fence: \"Ay, Miss Ivy! Yuh shutters up yet? Breeze coming tonight!\"", "options": ["Creole, formal", "Creole, informal", "Standard English, informal"], "answer": 1, "fb": "\"Yuh\" and \"Breeze coming\" without an auxiliary verb are Creole features, and the greeting is casual."}, {"text": "A disaster office post: \"Hey neighbours! Got your straps on yet? Book your free check before May ends.\"", "options": ["Standard English, informal", "Creole, informal", "Standard English, formal"], "answer": 0, "fb": "The grammar is Standard English, but the greeting, the question and the clipped \"Got your straps on yet?\" make the register informal."}, {"text": "\"Homeowners are advised that applications for the subsidy close on 15 May.\"", "options": ["Creole, formal", "Standard English, formal", "Standard English, informal"], "answer": 1, "fb": "Passive voice and official vocabulary: Standard English in a formal register."}, {"text": "The same office runs a radio jingle: \"Before de breeze start blow, check yuh roof, yuh know!\" Why use Creole HERE but not in its newspaper notice?", "options": ["The office made a mistake in the jingle", "Creole is easier to spell", "Creole is catchy, local and suited to radio, building connection; the notice must carry official authority"], "answer": 2, "fb": "Different media and purposes call for different varieties. The choice is deliberate."}, {"text": "A homeowner's letter to the newspaper says: \"Homeowners does need more help with the subsidy.\" What is the problem in a formal letter?", "options": ["An accidental Creole feature (\"does need\") where Standard English is required", "The register is too formal", "Nothing; it is correct Standard English"], "answer": 0, "fb": "In Standard English, \"Homeowners need more help\". Accidental Creole grammar costs expression marks in formal writing."}, {"text": "Which statement is ACCURATE?", "options": ["Creole can never be used in public messages", "A message in Standard English is always formal", "Variety and register are separate choices: Standard English can be informal, and Creole can carry a serious message"], "answer": 2, "fb": "Variety (Standard English or Creole) and register (formal to informal) vary independently."}], "win": "Very good. You separated variety from register and explained each choice by its medium, audience and purpose. That is the reasoning Question 2(a) rewards."}}, {"type": "learn", "title": "Q2(b) message points and Q2(c) slogans", "html": "<p><b>Q2(b): the TWO most important message points [2 marks].</b> One mark each for two clearly stated points the audience needs to know. Choose points that tell the audience <b>what is happening, when, and what they must do</b>. Skip background detail.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\">From the roof-inspection PSA: (1) \"Free roof and window inspections run from 1 to 31 May.\" (2) \"Homeowners without internet can register in person at the district community centre on Saturdays, 9 a.m. to noon.\"</p>\n<p><b>Q2(c): create a slogan using ONE literary technique [4 marks].</b> The specimen mark logic: <b>1</b> for a relevant slogan, <b>1</b> for including a literary technique, <b>2</b> for explaining how the technique works and linking it to the issue (an unclear or incomplete explanation earns 1).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Example slogan</th><th>How it works</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Allusion</td><td>\"A strap in time saves the roof\" (2026)</td><td>Echoes the proverb \"A stitch in time saves nine\", suggesting early action prevents bigger damage.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rhetorical question</td><td>\"You lock your door every night; why not strap your roof?\" (2026)</td><td>Makes homeowners reflect that roof safety is as basic as locking up.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rhyme</td><td>\"Don't wait, hydrate — water is great!\" (2027)</td><td>Rhyme makes the call to action memorable and urgent.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Alliteration</td><td>\"Swap soda, save smiles\" (2027)</td><td>Repeated \"s\" sounds make it catchy and stick in parents' minds.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pun</td><td>\"Don't let sugar steal their sweet future\" (2027)</td><td>Plays on \"sweet\" (sugar and happiness) to show what is at stake.</td></tr></table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Write it in three parts: the slogan in quotation marks, the technique named, then one or two sentences on how it works for this audience and issue.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "Message points", "p": "<p>What, when, and what to do. One mark each.</p>"}, {"h": "Slogan formula", "p": "<p>Slogan (1) + technique named (1) + explanation linked to the issue (2).</p>"}, {"h": "Techniques", "p": "<p>Allusion, rhetorical question, rhyme, alliteration, pun.</p>"}], "plain": "<p>For Question 2(b), pick the <b>two most important facts</b> people need: what is happening, when, and what they must do. For 2(c), write a short <b>slogan</b>, name its <b>technique</b> (such as rhyme or alliteration), and explain how it works. For example: \"Strap it tight, sleep all night\" uses rhyme, which makes the message easy for St. Lucian homeowners to remember.</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: half the slogan marks are in the explanation.</b> A full answer shows the technique at work: quote the feature (\"tight\" and \"night\"), explain the mechanism (rhyme makes the line memorable and gives it a confident rhythm) and link it to the issue and audience (homeowners recall it when they see their roof). Choose a technique that suits the message: antithesis (\"An hour of preparation saves a year of repair\") dramatises cost; a rhetorical question makes readers examine their own behaviour; allusion borrows the authority of a familiar saying. For 2(b), resist background detail: choose the points that change behaviour. A common error is listing three points when two are asked for, or naming a technique the slogan does not actually contain.</p>"}, {"type": "match", "game": true, "title": "Name the slogan's technique", "intro": "<p>Match each 2026 or 2027 slogan to the literary technique it uses.</p>", "pairs": [{"l": "Allusion", "r": "\"A strap in time saves the roof.\""}, {"l": "Rhetorical question", "r": "\"You buy a lock for your door; why not straps for your roof?\""}, {"l": "Rhyme", "r": "\"Pour the water, save your daughter.\""}, {"l": "Alliteration", "r": "\"Shutters, straps and safety start today.\""}, {"l": "Pun", "r": "\"Raise children, not their blood sugar.\""}], "win": "Excellent. Naming the technique correctly earns a mark; explaining how it works for the audience earns two more.", "tutor": [{"h": "Allusion", "p": "<p>Echoes something familiar: a proverb, song or saying.</p>"}, {"h": "Sound devices", "p": "<p>Rhyme = matching endings (water/daughter). Alliteration = repeated first sounds (s, s, s).</p>"}, {"h": "Pun", "p": "<p>One word, two meanings: \"raise\" children / \"raise\" blood sugar.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Name the technique (Foundation)", "pairs": [{"l": "Rhyme", "r": "\"Pour the water, save your daughter.\""}, {"l": "Rhetorical question", "r": "\"Why not strap your roof?\""}, {"l": "Alliteration", "r": "\"Shutters, straps and safety.\""}], "win": "Good. Rhyme, question, repeated sounds."}, "up": {"title": "Name the slogan's technique (Challenge)", "bloom": "Analyse", "intro": "<p>Eight 2026 slogans for homeowners, eight techniques. Match each slogan to the technique that drives it.</p>", "pairs": [{"l": "Allusion", "r": "\"Noah built before the rain. Strap your roof before the season.\""}, {"l": "Antithesis", "r": "\"An hour of preparation saves a year of repair.\""}, {"l": "Metaphor", "r": "\"Your roof is your family's umbrella: keep it fastened.\""}, {"l": "Personification", "r": "\"The wind never knocks before it enters. Strap your roof.\""}, {"l": "Rhyme", "r": "\"Strap it tight, sleep all night.\""}, {"l": "Alliteration", "r": "\"Brace, bolt and be ready.\""}, {"l": "Pun", "r": "\"Don't let the storm raise the roof.\""}, {"l": "Rhetorical question", "r": "\"You lock your car every night; why leave your roof unstrapped?\""}], "win": "Excellent. Now the harder part: explaining how each technique works on homeowners. For example, the pun plays on \"raise the roof\" (to celebrate loudly) and its literal meaning, turning a party phrase into a warning."}}, {"type": "checkin", "title": "Quick check-in"}, {"type": "order", "game": true, "title": "Build a full-mark slogan answer", "intro": "<p>Put the parts of Kemar's Q2(c) answer (2027 topic, parents) in the most effective order.</p>", "blocks": [{"t": "Slogan: \"Don't wait, hydrate.\""}, {"t": "Technique: rhyme (\"wait\" / \"hydrate\")."}, {"t": "How it works: the rhyme makes the message short and memorable, so parents recall it when packing lunch boxes."}, {"t": "Link to the issue: it urges parents to act now by replacing sweet drinks with water, reducing their children's risk of NCDs."}], "win": "That is a 4-mark structure: relevant slogan, named technique, explanation of how it works, and a clear link to the issue.", "tutor": [{"h": "Slogan first", "p": "<p>Give the slogan, then name the technique.</p>"}, {"h": "Then explain and link", "p": "<p>How does the technique work, and how does it connect to the issue and audience?</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Order the slogan answer (Foundation)", "blocks": [{"t": "\"Don't wait, hydrate.\"", "tag": "Slogan"}, {"t": "Rhyme.", "tag": "Technique"}, {"t": "It makes the message memorable for parents.", "tag": "Explanation"}], "win": "Good. Slogan, technique, explanation."}, "up": {"title": "Build a full-mark slogan answer (Challenge)", "bloom": "Analyse", "intro": "<p>Kemar's Question 2(c) answer for the 2026 roof-inspection campaign has five parts. Put them in the most effective order.</p>", "blocks": [{"t": "\"An hour of preparation saves a year of repair.\""}, {"t": "This slogan uses antithesis: it sets \"an hour\" against \"a year\" and \"preparation\" against \"repair\"."}, {"t": "The balanced contrast makes the difference in effort impossible to miss, and its rhythm makes it easy to remember."}, {"t": "For homeowners who think preparing is too much trouble, this shows that a small effort now prevents a far bigger burden later."}, {"t": "It therefore urges them to book a free roof inspection before the season begins, which is the main message of the PSA."}], "win": "That is a full-mark structure: the slogan, the technique named with its evidence, how it works, why it suits this audience, and the link back to the issue. Notice how each sentence refers back to the one before it (\"This slogan\", \"The balanced contrast\", \"this shows\", \"It therefore\")."}}, {"type": "write", "title": "Section A practice: Question 2 + a campaign slogan", "genre": "Short-answer responses + justification", "stimulus": "<blockquote class=\"passage\"><p><b>PUBLIC SERVICE ANNOUNCEMENT</b> (published in a weekend newspaper)</p><p>The Ministry of Health advises parents and guardians that free blood-glucose and blood-pressure screening for children aged 8–12 will be offered at all community clinics from 2 to 27 March. Screening takes approximately fifteen minutes and no fasting is required. Appointments may be booked by telephone or at the clinic reception. Parents are encouraged to bring their child's health card. Early detection of non-communicable diseases significantly improves long-term outcomes.</p></blockquote>", "prompt": "<p>Answer these Question 2-style parts on the stimulus (2027 topic: non-communicable diseases in children; audience: parents).</p><ol><li><b>(a)</b> Explain TWO reasons why the PSA uses the dialectal variety and language register which it does. [4]</li><li><b>(b)</b> List the TWO most important message points parents would need to know. [2]</li><li><b>(c)</b> Create a slogan to encourage parents to take their children for screening, using ONE literary technique, and explain it. [4]</li></ol><p><b>Then write a justification of 50–100 words</b> under the heading \"Justification\" for your slogan as part of a campaign: explain its <b>purpose</b>, <b>intended audience</b> and <b>context of communication</b> (where it would appear).</p>", "min": 140, "max": 320, "frame": "<p><b>(a)</b> The PSA uses Standard English because... It uses a formal register because...</p><p><b>(b)</b> 1. ... 2. ...</p><p><b>(c)</b> Slogan: \"...\" Technique: ... It works by... which links to...</p><p><b>Justification:</b> purpose, intended audience, context of communication.</p>", "checklist": ["In (a) I identified the variety AND the register and gave a reason for each.", "In (b) I listed two essential points (what, when, what to do).", "In (c) I wrote a relevant slogan, named the technique and explained how it works.", "I wrote a 50–100 word justification covering purpose, intended audience and context of communication."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "(a) Variety and register explained", "marks": 4, "look": ["standard english|acrolect", "formal", "\\b(because|official|serious|authority)"], "min": 3, "strong": "You identified both the variety and the register and justified each. Next step: make each reason specific to a health ministry addressing parents.", "weak": "Name both, then explain both: \"The PSA uses Standard English because it is the official variety of government communication. It uses a formal register because the Ministry is addressing a serious health matter.\""}, {"criterion": "(b) Message points", "marks": 2, "look": ["free|screening", "march|8.12|aged", "book|appointment|telephone|clinic|health card"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your two points tell parents what is offered, when and what to do. Next step: keep each point to one clean sentence.", "weak": "Choose the information parents must act on: \"Free screening for children aged 8–12 runs from 2 to 27 March at community clinics\" and \"Book by telephone or at the clinic reception.\""}, {"criterion": "(c) Slogan and technique", "marks": 4, "look": ["[\"“”]", "rhyme|alliteration|allusion|pun|rhetorical question|metaphor|repetition|antithesis|contrast", "\\b(memorable|reflect|echo|suggest|emphasi|urg)"], "min": 2, "strong": "A relevant slogan with a named technique and a clear explanation linked to screening. Next step: show how the technique suits parents specifically.", "weak": "Give the slogan in quotation marks, name the technique and explain it: \"'Check them early, keep them healthy' uses repetition of the 'k' sound... it makes the message memorable for busy parents.\""}, {"criterion": "Justification (purpose, audience, context)", "marks": 5, "look": ["purpose", "audience", "context", "parent"], "min": 3, "strong": "Your justification is complete and places the slogan in a real context. Next step: explain why that context reaches parents.", "weak": "Write 50–100 words: \"The purpose is to encourage parents to... The intended audience is parents of children aged 8–12... The context of communication is a banner at school gates and a radio jingle during March.\""}], "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The PSA uses Standard English, the acrolect, because it is the official variety used by government in public communication, which gives the Ministry's message authority and credibility. It uses a formal register because a government ministry is addressing parents about a serious health matter, children's risk of non-communicable diseases, so a casual tone would make the message seem less important.</p><p><b>(b)</b> 1. Free blood-glucose and blood-pressure screening for children aged 8–12 is available at community clinics from 2 to 27 March.<br>2. Parents should book an appointment by telephone or at the clinic reception and bring the child's health card.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Slogan: \"Fifteen minutes now, a lifetime of health.\" Technique: antithesis/contrast between \"fifteen minutes\" and \"a lifetime\". It works by showing how small the effort is compared with how great the benefit is, which persuades busy parents that screening is worth their time and makes the message memorable.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of this slogan is to persuade parents to book free screening for their children before the programme ends. The intended audience is parents and guardians of children aged 8–12, many of them busy working adults. The context of communication is a banner at primary school gates during school pick-up in March, supported by a radio jingle, so parents meet the message when their children are right beside them.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>Question 2 rewards labels plus reasons. For (a), name the variety and the register in separate sentences, each with \"because\". For (c), never leave a slogan unexplained: the explanation of the technique is worth half the marks.</p>", "easy": {"title": "Section A practice: Question 2 + a campaign slogan (Foundation)", "bloom": "Apply", "genre": "Short answers (guided)", "prompt": "<p>Answer these parts on the stimulus (topic: non-communicable diseases in children; audience: parents). Use the sentence frames.</p><ol><li><b>(a)</b> Name the dialectal variety and the register the PSA uses, and give ONE reason for each. [4]</li><li><b>(b)</b> Write the TWO most important things parents need to know. [2]</li><li><b>(c)</b> Write a short slogan that uses rhyme OR alliteration, name the technique, and say in one sentence why it works. [3]</li></ol><p><b>Then</b> write a short <b>justification</b> (50–80 words) for your slogan, using the three starters. [3]</p><p>[Total: 12 marks]</p>", "min": 100, "max": 220, "frame": "<p><b>(a)</b> The PSA uses Standard English because ... It uses a formal register because ...</p><p><b>(b)</b> 1. Free screening ... 2. Parents should ...</p><p><b>(c)</b> Slogan: \"...\" The technique is ... It works because ...</p><p><b>Justification</b><br>The purpose of my slogan is to ...<br>The intended audience is ...<br>The context of communication is ... (where parents will see or hear it)</p>", "checklist": ["In (a) I named Standard English and a formal register, with a reason for each.", "In (b) I wrote two key facts: what, when and what to do.", "In (c) I wrote a slogan, named rhyme or alliteration, and said why it works.", "My justification has purpose, audience and context."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "(a) Variety and register", "marks": 4, "look": ["standard english|acrolect", "formal", "\\b(because|official|serious)\\b"], "min": 3, "strong": "You named both and gave a reason for each.", "weak": "Name both, then give a reason for each: \"Standard English because it is the official language of government; formal because the health message is serious.\""}, {"criterion": "(b) Two key facts", "marks": 2, "look": ["free|screening", "march|book|appointment|telephone|clinic|health card"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your two facts tell parents what is offered and what to do.", "weak": "Choose what parents must act on: the free screening and its dates, and how to book."}, {"criterion": "(c) Slogan and technique", "marks": 3, "look": ["[\"“”]", "rhyme|alliteration", "\\b(because|memorable|remember|catchy)\\b"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your slogan, the technique and the reason are all there.", "weak": "Put your slogan in quotation marks, write \"The technique is rhyme\" (or alliteration) and add \"It works because...\""}, {"criterion": "Justification", "marks": 3, "look": ["purpose", "audience", "context"], "min": 3, "strong": "All three parts of the justification are there.", "weak": "Use all three starters: purpose, intended audience and context of communication."}], "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The PSA uses Standard English because it is the official language that the government uses for public notices. It uses a formal register because the Ministry is writing to parents about a serious health matter.</p><p><b>(b)</b> 1. Free screening for children aged 8–12 is available at community clinics from 2 to 27 March.<br>2. Parents should book by telephone or at the clinic and bring the child's health card.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Slogan: \"Check them early, keep them healthy.\" The technique is alliteration. It works because the repeated \"k\" sound in \"check\" and \"keep\" makes the message catchy and easy for parents to remember.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of my slogan is to encourage parents to book free screening for their children in March. The intended audience is parents of children aged 8 to 12. The context of communication is a banner at the primary school gate, where parents will see it every day when they collect their children.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>Every label needs a reason: write \"because\" after each one. For the slogan, the reason is worth as much as the slogan itself.</p>"}, "up": {"title": "Section A practice: Question 2 + a campaign slogan (Challenge)", "bloom": "Create", "genre": "Short-answer responses with adaptation + justification", "prompt": "<p>Answer these parts on the stimulus (topic: non-communicable diseases in children; audience: parents).</p><ol><li><b>(a)</b> Explain TWO reasons why the PSA uses the dialectal variety and language register which it does. [4]</li><li><b>(b)</b> List the TWO most important message points parents would need to know. [2]</li><li><b>(c)</b> Create a slogan for a banner at school gates, using ONE literary technique, and explain how the technique works and links to the issue. [4]</li><li><b>(d)</b> <b>Adapt</b> your slogan into a radio jingle line for parents in a rural district, using ONE deliberate Creole feature. Explain why the change of variety suits the new medium and audience. [4]</li></ol><p><b>Then</b>, under the heading \"Justification\", write <b>50–100 words</b> for the campaign as a whole, explaining how your banner and jingle relate to their <b>purpose</b>, <b>intended audience</b> and <b>contexts of communication</b>. [6]</p><p>[Total: 20 marks]</p>", "min": 220, "max": 400, "frame": "<p><b>(a)</b> Variety: Standard English (acrolect) because ... / Register: formal because ...</p><p><b>(b)</b> 1. ... 2. ...</p><p><b>(c)</b> Slogan: \"...\" Technique: ... (quote the feature). It works by ... which links to ...</p><p><b>(d)</b> Jingle line: \"...\" The Creole feature is ... It suits radio and rural parents because ...</p><p><b>Justification:</b> purpose → audience → the two contexts (banner and radio) and why each variety suits its context.</p>", "checklist": ["In (a) each label has its own reason linked to the source, purpose or audience.", "In (b) my two points tell parents what, when and what to do.", "In (c) I quoted the technique's feature and explained how it works for this issue.", "In (d) my Creole feature is deliberate, and I explained why it suits radio and rural parents.", "My 50–100 word justification connects both items to purpose, audience and context."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "(a) Variety and register explained", "marks": 4, "look": ["standard english|acrolect", "formal", "\\b(because|official|serious|authority|credib)"], "min": 3, "strong": "Both labels are correct and each has a distinct, relevant reason.", "weak": "Give each label its own reason: Standard English because it is the official variety of government communication; a formal register because a ministry is addressing a serious health risk to children."}, {"criterion": "(b) Message points", "marks": 2, "look": ["free|screening", "march|8.12|aged", "book|appointment|telephone|clinic|health card"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your two points are the ones that change what parents do.", "weak": "Choose the points parents must act on: what is offered and when, and how to book."}, {"criterion": "(c) Slogan and technique", "marks": 4, "look": ["[\"“”]", "rhyme|alliteration|allusion|pun|rhetorical question|metaphor|antithesis|personification|repetition", "\\b(memorable|contrast|echo|suggest|emphasi|urg|reflect)", "parent|child|screen"], "min": 3, "strong": "A relevant slogan with the technique named, its feature quoted and its effect linked to screening.", "weak": "Quote the feature that creates the technique (\"fifteen minutes\" against \"a lifetime\") and explain how it persuades parents to book screening."}, {"criterion": "(d) Adaptation with a deliberate Creole feature", "marks": 4, "look": ["creole|vernacular", "radio|jingle", "rural|local|warm|connection|identity|familiar|memorable", "\\b(because|so that|which)\\b"], "min": 3, "strong": "You adapted the slogan deliberately and explained why the variety suits radio and rural parents.", "weak": "Rewrite the slogan with one clear Creole feature (for example, \"doh\" or \"yuh\"), then explain why: on radio, a familiar local voice builds warmth and trust with rural parents and makes the line memorable."}, {"criterion": "Justification (purpose, audience, contexts)", "marks": 6, "look": ["purpose", "audience", "context", "banner|gate", "radio|jingle"], "min": 4, "strong": "Your justification connects both items to the purpose, the audience and two distinct contexts.", "weak": "In 50–100 words, explain the purpose, the parents you are targeting, and both contexts: a banner at school gates (Standard English, seen daily) and a radio jingle (a touch of Creole, heard in rural homes)."}], "model": "<p><b>(a)</b> The PSA uses Standard English, the acrolect, because it is the official variety the government uses for public communication, which gives the Ministry's message authority. It uses a formal register because a ministry is addressing parents about a serious risk to their children's health, so a casual tone would make the screening seem optional.</p><p><b>(b)</b> 1. Free blood-glucose and blood-pressure screening for children aged 8–12 runs at community clinics from 2 to 27 March.<br>2. Parents should book by telephone or at the clinic reception and bring the child's health card.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Slogan: \"Fifteen minutes now, a lifetime of health.\" Technique: antithesis, setting \"fifteen minutes\" against \"a lifetime\". The contrast shows how small the effort is beside the benefit, which persuades busy parents that a short visit is worth making this month.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Jingle line: \"Fifteen minutes, doh wait: check your child's health today!\" The Creole feature \"doh\" (don't) makes the line sound like a neighbour talking. On radio, that familiar voice builds warmth and trust with rural parents, and the rhythm makes it easy to sing along to.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of the campaign is to persuade parents to book free screening for children aged 8–12 before 27 March. The intended audience is busy parents, including those in rural districts. The banner uses Standard English at primary school gates, where parents see it daily beside their children. The jingle uses a touch of Creole on community radio in the mornings, so that rural parents hear a familiar, trusted voice.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>Adapting a message for a new medium is a Module 2 and Module 3 skill combined. Make the Creole feature deliberate and explain its effect, and keep the formal answers in Standard English. In the justification, a campaign with two items has two contexts: explain both.</p>"}}]}, {"id": "P3U4", "title": "Section B: The Reflective or Expository Piece", "objectives": ["Explain how the Section B piece is marked: creativity, organisation and expression, in bands out of 25.", "Profile the year's audience and follow the planning chain before writing.", "Write a reflective or expository piece of 200–250 words with an introduction, development and conclusion and at least one deliberate rhetorical appeal."], "videos": [{"title": "How to write a reflective piece", "q": "how to write a reflective piece of writing structure examples"}, {"title": "How to write a newspaper article", "q": "how to write a newspaper article headline lead structure students"}, {"title": "Ethos, pathos, logos and kairos", "q": "ethos pathos logos kairos rhetorical appeals explained with examples"}], "screens": [{"type": "learn", "title": "What Section B asks and how it is marked", "html": "<p>In the specimen, Question 3(a) reads: <i>\"Write a reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words based on the topic...\"</i> It is worth <b>25 marks</b>, awarded in bands for <b>creativity, organisation and expression</b> together.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Band</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent</td><td>24–25</td></tr><tr><td>Very good</td><td>20–23</td></tr><tr><td>Good</td><td>15–19</td></tr><tr><td>Adequate</td><td>11–14</td></tr><tr><td>Unsatisfactory</td><td>6–10</td></tr><tr><td>Weak</td><td>0–5</td></tr></table></div><p>Within the band, the 25 marks are split <b>Creativity 10</b>, <b>Organisation 7</b> and <b>Expression 8</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"trio\">\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Creativity</span><p>A unique use of the genre; <b>audience engagement</b>; originality of ideas and events. Do not just repeat the stimulus.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Organisation</span><p>A clear <b>introduction, development and conclusion</b>; ideas that hang together (coherence).</p></div>\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Expression</span><p>A <b>register appropriate to the genre</b>; grammatical language; helpful punctuation; careful proofreading.</p></div>\n</div>\n<p>200–250 words is short. That is roughly one introduction, two developing paragraphs and a brief conclusion. Every sentence must earn its place.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Count as you draft: about 40 words for the introduction, 70 each for two developing paragraphs, 40 for the conclusion. That lands you near 220, safely inside the limit.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "25 marks, three factors", "p": "<p>Creativity (originality, engaging the audience), organisation (intro, development, conclusion), expression (register, grammar, punctuation, proofreading).</p>"}, {"h": "Short and tight", "p": "<p>200–250 words: intro, two developing paragraphs, conclusion.</p>"}], "plain": "<p>Section B, Question 3(a), asks for a <b>reflective OR expository</b> piece of <b>200–250 words</b> on the year's topic. It is worth <b>25 marks</b>: <b>creativity</b> (fresh ideas that engage the audience), <b>organisation</b> (a clear beginning, middle and end) and <b>expression</b> (correct English in the right register). A Kittitian homeowner's journal entry that only retells the stimulus would score low on creativity.</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: the bands reward the whole piece working together.</b> Creativity (10 marks) is not decoration; it means a unique use of the genre and genuine audience engagement, such as a reflective entry with a turning point or a feature with a hook that speaks to the reader's real worries. Organisation (7) means coherence as well as structure: every paragraph must serve the main point. Expression (8) includes register: a newspaper feature and a journal entry need different voices. Because 200–250 words is so tight, the biggest danger is spreading thin. One sharp idea developed with a vivid detail and a clear lesson outscores five tips listed. Retelling the stimulus is the most common way strong writers lose creativity marks.</p>"}, {"type": "learn", "title": "Reflective vs expository: choosing your form", "html": "<p>When the question says <b>reflective OR expository</b>, choose the form that best serves your audience.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th></th><th>Reflective</th><th>Expository</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Voice</b></td><td>First person (\"I\"), personal</td><td>Often third person or a clear expert voice</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Aim</b></td><td>Look back on an experience and draw meaning from it</td><td>Explain, inform or advise</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Forms</b></td><td>Journal entry, reflective letter, personal essay</td><td>Essay, newspaper article, feature</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Key features</b></td><td>Feelings, a turning point, what was learnt</td><td>Thesis or lead, organised points, evidence, transitions</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2026 example</b></td><td>A homeowner's journal the morning after a hurricane passed close by</td><td>A newspaper feature: \"Five things every homeowner must do before June\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2027 example</b></td><td>A mother reflects on the day her son was diagnosed with high blood pressure</td><td>An essay explaining how parents can reduce children's NCD risk</td></tr></table></div>\n<p><b>Rhetorical appeals</b> lift either form. Plan at least one deliberately: <b>logos</b> (evidence: \"a roof without straps can lift in strong winds\"), <b>ethos</b> (credibility: \"as a builder for twenty years...\"), <b>pathos</b> (emotion: \"the sound of the galvanise tearing still wakes me at night\"), <b>kairos</b> (timeliness: \"with hurricane season six weeks away...\").</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A reflective piece still needs a clear point. Ask: \"Is that the complete main point?\" A homeowner's reflection should arrive at a lesson, such as \"preparation is cheaper than repair\", not just describe the storm.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "Reflective", "p": "<p>\"I\" looks back, feels, and learns something.</p>"}, {"h": "Expository", "p": "<p>Explains or advises in an organised way.</p>"}, {"h": "Appeals", "p": "<p>Logos, ethos, pathos, kairos. Plan at least one.</p>"}], "plain": "<p>A <b>reflective</b> piece looks back on an experience in the first person (\"I\") and shows what was learnt. An <b>expository</b> piece explains or advises, often with facts and organised points. Choose the one that suits your audience best. A Grenadian homeowner's journal about the night his roof lifted is reflective; a feature called \"Five checks before June\" is expository.</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: the form is defined by its purpose, not its pronoun.</b> An expository piece can use \"I\" (\"As a builder for twenty years, I recommend...\"), and a reflective piece can contain practical facts. What makes writing reflective is that it moves from experience to insight; what makes it expository is that it explains or advises in an organised way. Choose strategically: reflective writing creates pathos and identification, which suits an audience that knows the advice but has not acted; expository writing builds logos and clarity, which suits an audience that needs instructions. Whichever you choose, plan one deliberate appeal and name it in your justification. A common error is a reflective piece that narrates events but never arrives at a lesson.</p>"}, {"type": "sort", "game": true, "title": "Reflective or expository?", "intro": "<p>Sort each opening line (2026 or 2027 topic) by form.</p>", "main": "Opening lines", "bins": ["Reflective", "Expository"], "items": [{"t": "\"Firstly, check that your roof is fastened to the walls with hurricane straps.\"", "bin": 1, "fb": "Ordered advice with a transition: expository."}, {"t": "\"The day the nurse said 'pre-diabetic', I looked at the juice box in my son's hand and felt my stomach drop.\"", "bin": 0, "fb": "A personal moment and a feeling: reflective."}, {"t": "\"Every homeowner in Jamaica can take five simple, low-cost steps to protect their property before hurricane season.\"", "bin": 1, "fb": "An informing thesis aimed at readers: expository."}, {"t": "\"Looking back, I realise our cistern saved us that September.\"", "bin": 0, "fb": "\"Looking back, I realise\" is classic reflective language."}, {"t": "\"Non-communicable diseases in children are rising across the Caribbean, but parents hold the most powerful tools to reverse the trend.\"", "bin": 1, "fb": "States a main point and promises explanation: expository essay."}, {"t": "\"I still remember the silence after the wind stopped, and the morning light pouring through the hole where our kitchen roof had been.\"", "bin": 0, "fb": "First person, memory and feeling: reflective."}], "win": "Well sorted. Reflective writing looks back and learns; expository writing explains and advises. Both are acceptable when the question says \"reflective OR expository\".", "tutor": [{"h": "Clues for reflective", "p": "<p>\"I remember\", \"looking back\", feelings, a personal moment.</p>"}, {"h": "Clues for expository", "p": "<p>A thesis, advice, \"firstly\", explaining to the reader.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Reflective or expository? (Foundation)", "items": [{"t": "\"I still remember the silence after the wind stopped.\"", "bin": 0, "fb": "Personal memory: reflective."}, {"t": "\"Every homeowner can take five simple steps before hurricane season.\"", "bin": 1, "fb": "Advice to readers: expository."}, {"t": "\"Looking back, I realise our cistern saved us.\"", "bin": 0, "fb": "\"Looking back\" signals reflection."}], "win": "Good. Looking back = reflective; advising = expository."}, "up": {"title": "Reflective or expository? (Challenge)", "bloom": "Analyse", "intro": "<p>Harder openings from 2026 pieces for homeowners. The pronoun \"I\" does not decide the form: ask whether the line looks back to draw meaning, or explains and advises.</p>", "items": [{"t": "\"As a builder for twenty years, I can tell you the three places where roofs fail first.\"", "bin": 1, "fb": "First person, but the speaker uses experience to explain and advise: expository, with ethos."}, {"t": "\"I used to think straps were for people with money. Then September taught me what a roof really costs.\"", "bin": 0, "fb": "A changed belief, looked back on: reflective."}, {"t": "\"Three questions every homeowner should ask a roof inspector\"", "bin": 1, "fb": "A headline that promises organised advice: expository."}, {"t": "\"Dear neighbours, I am writing from the kitchen the storm took last year, to tell you what I have learnt.\"", "bin": 0, "fb": "A reflective letter: personal experience shared to draw a lesson."}, {"t": "\"Insurance rarely covers everything, so it pays to know what your policy excludes before the season begins.\"", "bin": 1, "fb": "Explains and advises: expository."}, {"t": "\"The night the shutters jammed, I understood that preparation is not about fear; it is about time.\"", "bin": 0, "fb": "A moment that produces an insight: reflective."}, {"t": "\"Unlike shutters, plywood boards must be cut, labelled and stored well in advance.\"", "bin": 1, "fb": "A comparison that informs: expository."}, {"t": "\"Even now, when the wind rises at night, I check the window latches twice.\"", "bin": 0, "fb": "Looking back on how an experience still shapes behaviour: reflective."}], "win": "Well analysed. Form follows purpose: reflective writing moves from experience to insight, while expository writing explains and advises, even when it uses \"I\"."}}, {"type": "learn", "title": "Plan with the audience: the planning chain", "html": "<p>Before writing, <b>profile your audience</b> and follow the <b>planning chain</b>: <b>Audience → Purpose → Context → Message → Language & Register → Tone & Medium</b>.</p>\n<p>Profile by <b>age</b>, <b>educational background</b> and <b>geographical location</b>; each one influences the medium or channel. Remember the difference between <b>content</b> (what you say) and <b>context</b> (where and how it appears).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Link</th><th>2026: homeowners</th><th>2027: parents</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Audience</td><td>Homeowners aged 35–70 across Dominica, mixed schooling, many on hillsides or near the coast</td><td>Parents of children aged 6–12, busy working adults</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Purpose</td><td>To <b>persuade</b> them to secure roofs and windows before June</td><td>To <b>warn</b> them about sweet drinks and <b>encourage</b> water and play</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Context</td><td>Weekend newspaper's home section in May</td><td>A school newsletter sent home in book bags</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Message</td><td>\"Preparation is cheaper than repair.\"</td><td>\"Small swaps today protect your child tomorrow.\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Language & Register</td><td>Clear, formal-but-friendly Standard English</td><td>Warm, plain Standard English, no medical jargon</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tone & Medium</td><td>Practical and urgent; newspaper feature</td><td>Caring and hopeful; newsletter article</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>Organise expository pieces with an <b>introduction</b> (with a clear thesis or lead), a <b>body</b> using <b>transitional words and phrases</b>, and a <b>conclusion</b> shorter than the introduction.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Jot the six links in the margin. They take one minute and give you most of your justification.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "Chain", "p": "<p>Audience → Purpose → Context → Message → Language & Register → Tone & Medium.</p>"}, {"h": "Profile", "p": "<p>Age, education, location.</p>"}, {"h": "Content vs context", "p": "<p>Content = what you say. Context = where and how it appears.</p>"}], "plain": "<p>Before you write, describe your audience: their <b>age</b>, <b>schooling</b> and <b>where they live</b>. Then follow the chain: <b>audience, purpose, context, message, language and register, tone and medium</b>. For homeowners in Antigua, you might plan: persuade them to fit straps before June, in the weekend newspaper, with the message \"Preparation is cheaper than repair\".</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: profile a subgroup, then let every link follow from it.</b> \"Homeowners\" covers retirees in hillside homes, first-time buyers with mortgages and families who inherited old wooden houses, and each needs a different message. A first-time buyer worries about cost, so logos (comparing straps with one month's mortgage) fits; a retiree who has survived many storms may resist advice, so ethos (an inspector's voice) or pathos (grandchildren) may work better. Context must be real and timed: \"the home section of a weekend newspaper in May\" tells the examiner why readers would be receptive. Remember the difference between content (what you say) and context (where and how it appears). Your six planning notes become most of your justification.</p>"}, {"type": "scenario", "game": true, "title": "Planning for homeowners", "intro": "<p>It is a 2026 practice paper. The topic is natural disasters; the audience is homeowners. Make the planning decisions.</p>", "nodes": {"start": {"text": "<p>You must write a reflective OR expository piece of 200–250 words. Which plan fits?</p>", "choices": [{"t": "A rap for teenagers about earthquakes.", "to": "bad1", "fb": "Wrong audience and the wrong form: the question wants reflective or expository writing for homeowners."}, {"t": "A newspaper feature advising homeowners how to prepare their homes before hurricane season.", "to": "n2", "fb": "A strong expository choice: it suits the topic and speaks directly to homeowners."}]}, "n2": {"text": "<p>Good. What is your purpose statement?</p>", "choices": [{"t": "\"To talk about hurricanes.\"", "to": "bad2", "fb": "That is a topic, not a purpose."}, {"t": "\"To persuade homeowners in Barbados to secure their roofs and windows before June.\"", "to": "n3", "fb": "Precise verb, specific audience, clear action."}]}, "n3": {"text": "<p>You have 230 words planned. Which appeal will you build in deliberately?</p>", "choices": [{"t": "Kairos and logos: \"With the season six weeks away, a set of straps costs far less than a new roof.\"", "to": "good", "fb": "Timely and logical, perfectly suited to practical homeowners."}, {"t": "No appeal; just list facts from the stimulus.", "to": "bad3", "fb": "Copying the stimulus shows little creativity, and the piece will not engage its audience."}]}, "good": {"text": "<p><b>Excellent plan.</b> Right form, right audience, precise purpose and a deliberate appeal. You are ready to write, and half your justification is already done.</p>", "end": "good"}, "bad1": {"text": "<p><b>Not quite.</b> Always write for the year's audience (homeowners) and in the form the question asks (reflective or expository). Try again.</p>", "end": "bad"}, "bad2": {"text": "<p><b>Not quite.</b> A purpose needs \"to\" + a precise verb + audience + action. Try again.</p>", "end": "bad"}, "bad3": {"text": "<p><b>Not quite.</b> Creativity marks reward originality and audience engagement. Build an appeal and use the stimulus selectively. Try again.</p>", "end": "bad"}}, "tutor": [{"h": "Check the question", "p": "<p>Form (reflective or expository), topic and audience must all match.</p>"}, {"h": "Then purpose and appeal", "p": "<p>Precise purpose verb; at least one deliberate appeal.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Planning for homeowners (Foundation)", "nodes": {"start": {"text": "<p>2026: natural disasters for homeowners. Which piece fits?</p>", "choices": [{"t": "A newspaper feature advising homeowners how to prepare for hurricane season.", "to": "good", "fb": "Yes: expository, on topic, for homeowners."}, {"t": "A rap for teenagers.", "to": "bad1", "fb": "Wrong audience and form."}]}, "good": {"text": "<p><b>Well done.</b> Right form and audience.</p>", "end": "good"}, "bad1": {"text": "<p><b>Not quite.</b> Write for homeowners in a reflective or expository form. Try again.</p>", "end": "bad"}}}, "up": {"title": "Planning for homeowners (Challenge)", "bloom": "Evaluate", "intro": "<p>A harder planning task. It is a 2026 practice paper: natural disasters, for homeowners. You have 200–250 words, and every decision has a cost.</p>", "nodes": {"start": {"text": "<p>Your research shows three groups: retirees in hillside homes, young families renting near the lagoon, and first-time owners who have just taken out a mortgage. Which approach gives you the strongest piece?</p>", "choices": [{"t": "Choose ONE subgroup, first-time owners with mortgages, and write an expository feature for the home section of a weekend newspaper.", "to": "n2", "fb": "A focused subgroup lets every sentence speak to real worries."}, {"t": "Write for the young families renting near the lagoon.", "to": "bad1", "fb": "Renters are not homeowners. The year's audience is part of the question."}, {"t": "Write for all homeowners about all disasters: hurricanes, earthquakes, floods and fires.", "to": "n1b", "fb": "Broad coverage in 250 words means shallow coverage."}]}, "n1b": {"text": "<p>Your plan covers four disasters and five audiences in 230 words. A classmate says it reads like a brochure. What now?</p>", "choices": [{"t": "Keep it broad. Covering more earns more content marks.", "to": "bad2", "fb": "Section B rewards creativity and engagement, not the number of topics mentioned."}, {"t": "Narrow it to one subgroup and one hazard (hurricanes), so that each paragraph can go deeper.", "to": "n2", "fb": "Good recovery. Depth and engagement beat coverage."}]}, "n2": {"text": "<p>What are your purpose and context?</p>", "choices": [{"t": "To persuade first-time homeowners to budget for straps and shutters before June; the home section of a weekend newspaper in May, when new owners are planning work on their homes.", "to": "n3", "fb": "A precise verb and action, a specific audience, and a real, timed context."}, {"t": "To persuade homeowners about hurricanes, in a newspaper.", "to": "n2b", "fb": "The pieces are there, but every part is vague."}]}, "n2b": {"text": "<p>Your teacher writes beside your plan: \"Which homeowners? What action? Which section of the paper, and when?\" How do you fix it?</p>", "choices": [{"t": "Add a precise action, the specific subgroup, and a context with a place and a time.", "to": "n3", "fb": "Now your plan doubles as a strong justification."}, {"t": "Add more facts from the stimulus instead.", "to": "bad3", "fb": "More facts do not fix a vague purpose and context."}]}, "n3": {"text": "<p>Which plan for appeals and for using the stimulus is strongest?</p>", "choices": [{"t": "Pathos only: a dramatic storm scene with no practical advice.", "to": "ok", "fb": "It engages, but first-time owners also need to know what to do and what it costs."}, {"t": "Logos from one stimulus fact in your own words, kairos (\"six weeks to the season\"), ethos from an inspector's advice, and one original local image.", "to": "good", "fb": "Deliberate, varied appeals and selective use of the stimulus: top-band planning."}, {"t": "Copy three sentences of the PSA word for word, because they are accurate.", "to": "bad4", "fb": "Copying the stimulus shows no creativity and wastes your word limit."}]}, "good": {"text": "<p><b>Top-band plan.</b> A specific subgroup, a precise purpose, a real context, deliberate appeals and selective use of the stimulus. Your justification is already half written.</p>", "end": "good"}, "ok": {"text": "<p><b>Engaging, but incomplete.</b> The scene will hold readers, but your purpose was to persuade them to budget for straps. Add one practical, costed step and an appeal to reason.</p>", "end": "ok"}, "bad1": {"text": "<p><b>Wrong audience.</b> The year's audience is homeowners. Renters cannot fit straps to a roof they do not own. Try again.</p>", "end": "bad"}, "bad2": {"text": "<p><b>Spread too thin.</b> Breadth without depth reads like a list and loses creativity marks. Try again.</p>", "end": "bad"}, "bad3": {"text": "<p><b>Still vague.</b> Facts cannot replace a clear purpose, audience and context. Try again.</p>", "end": "bad"}, "bad4": {"text": "<p><b>No creativity.</b> The examiner has already read the stimulus. Use it selectively, in your own words. Try again.</p>", "end": "bad"}}}}, {"type": "checkin", "title": "Quick check-in"}, {"type": "mcq", "title": "Which opening engages the audience?", "q": "For a 2027 expository piece aimed at parents, which opening BEST shows creativity and audience engagement?", "options": [{"t": "\"Children de drink too much sweet drink an' dem parents ent care.\"", "fb": "Accidental Creole in an expository piece for parents costs expression marks, and blaming the audience will lose them."}, {"t": "\"Non-communicable diseases are diseases that are not communicable.\"", "fb": "This is circular and flat. It defines the term with itself and gives parents no reason to keep reading."}, {"t": "\"In this essay I will write about NCDs in children because it is the topic.\"", "fb": "Announcing the topic like this sounds mechanical. A thesis should state your main point, not just your subject."}, {"t": "\"Tomorrow morning, when you pack your child's lunch, you will make a health decision that could last a lifetime.\"", "ok": true, "fb": "Excellent. It speaks directly to parents (\"you\"), uses a familiar daily moment and creates urgency (kairos). It engages before it informs."}], "tutor": [{"h": "Engage first", "p": "<p>Speak to the audience and use a moment from their own lives.</p>"}, {"h": "Avoid", "p": "<p>Circular definitions, mechanical announcements, accidental Creole and blaming your readers.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Best opening (Foundation)", "q": "Which opening speaks directly to parents and grabs attention?", "options": [{"t": "\"Tomorrow, when you pack your child's lunch, you make a health decision.\"", "ok": true, "fb": "Yes. It speaks to \"you\" about a real moment."}, {"t": "\"NCDs are diseases that are not communicable.\"", "fb": "Flat and circular."}, {"t": "\"In this essay I will write about NCDs.\"", "fb": "Mechanical; it does not engage."}]}, "up": {"title": "Which opening engages the audience? (Challenge)", "bloom": "Evaluate", "q": "For a 2026 expository feature aimed at FIRST-TIME homeowners in Antigua and Barbuda, which opening BEST shows creativity and audience engagement?", "options": [{"t": "\"Hurricanes are powerful storms that can cause serious damage to homes and property.\"", "fb": "Accurate but flat. It tells first-time owners nothing they do not know and gives them no reason to read on."}, {"t": "\"According to the Disaster Management Office, homeowners are urged to complete the following before 31 May: secure roofs with hurricane straps and fit shutters.\"", "fb": "This repeats the stimulus almost word for word. It shows no originality, so it earns little for creativity."}, {"t": "\"Imagine the most terrifying storm in history destroying everything you have ever loved, leaving you with nothing but tears.\"", "fb": "Pathos pushed into melodrama. The exaggeration may make practical new owners switch off, and it does not point towards any action."}, {"t": "\"Your mortgage will last thirty years. Fitting hurricane straps takes one afternoon. Which one are you protecting?\"", "ok": true, "fb": "Excellent. It speaks to the specific subgroup (owners with mortgages), uses a striking contrast and ends with a question that makes each reader examine their own choices."}]}}, {"type": "write", "title": "Section B practice: newspaper feature for homeowners", "genre": "Journalistic piece (expository)", "stimulus": "<blockquote class=\"passage\"><p><b>Stimulus:</b> After last September's storm, the Disaster Management Office reported that most damaged homes in one district had roofs without hurricane straps, and many households had no emergency water supply. Insurance agents note that preparation costs are a fraction of repair bills.</p></blockquote>", "prompt": "<p><b>2026 topic: natural disasters. Audience: homeowners.</b></p><p>Write an <b>expository newspaper feature of 200–250 words</b> for the home section of a weekend newspaper, advising homeowners in Saint Lucia how to prepare their homes before hurricane season. Include a headline, a lead, organised advice with transitions, and at least one deliberate rhetorical appeal. Do not copy the stimulus.</p><p><b>Then, in the same box, write a justification of 50–100 words</b> under the heading \"Justification\", explaining the <b>purpose</b>, <b>intended audience</b> and <b>context of communication</b>. (Total for both parts: about 250–350 words.)</p>", "min": 250, "max": 350, "frame": "<p><b>Headline</b> + <b>lead</b> (who, what, why now).</p><p><b>Development:</b> two or three pieces of advice with transitions (\"First...\", \"Next...\", \"Finally...\").</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> a short call to action.</p><p><b>Justification:</b> purpose, intended audience, context of communication.</p>", "checklist": ["My feature is 200–250 words, with a headline, lead, development and conclusion.", "I wrote directly for homeowners and used at least one deliberate appeal.", "I used the stimulus selectively and added my own ideas.", "I wrote a separate 50–100 word justification covering purpose, intended audience and context of communication.", "I proofread for Standard English and punctuation."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "Creativity and audience engagement", "marks": 5, "look": ["\\b(you|your)\\b", "homeowner|household|home", "\\b(imagine|now|today|weeks|before june|season)\\b"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your feature speaks directly to homeowners and creates urgency. Next step: add one vivid, original detail that only a reader in your territory would recognise.", "weak": "Engage your audience directly: \"Look up at your roof tonight. Would it survive a Category 4?\" Use \"you\" and a timely hook."}, {"criterion": "Organisation", "marks": 5, "look": ["\\b(first|firstly|next|then|also|in addition|finally|lastly)\\b", "strap|shutter|cistern|water|kit|insurance|trim|drain"], "min": 2, "strong": "Clear lead, ordered advice and a conclusion. Next step: make your transitions show logic (\"Because roofs fail first, start there\").", "weak": "Organise your advice: lead, then \"First... Next... Finally...\", then a short call to action."}, {"criterion": "Justification (purpose, audience, context)", "marks": 5, "look": ["purpose", "audience", "context", "newspaper|home section"], "min": 3, "strong": "Complete and specific. Next step: link your register to your audience in one clause.", "weak": "Add 50–100 words: \"The purpose is to persuade homeowners... The intended audience is... The context of communication is the home section of a weekend newspaper in May.\""}, {"criterion": "Expression", "marks": 5, "look": ["[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]", ","], "min": 2, "strong": "Accurate Standard English in a register that suits a newspaper. Next step: tighten long sentences.", "weak": "Proofread for grammar and punctuation. Choose the correct word, not the biggest word."}], "model": "<p><b>Strap It Before You Lose It</b></p><p>Hurricane season is six weeks away. If a storm passed over Saint Lucia tonight, would your roof still be there in the morning? For many homeowners, the honest answer is \"I'm not sure\", and that uncertainty is expensive.</p><p>First, look up. Roofs fail before walls do, and a roof fastened only by nails can lift in strong winds. Hurricane straps connect the roof to the walls and cost far less than replacing galvanise, rafters and everything beneath them. Ask a qualified builder to check yours this month, and book one of the free inspections while appointments are still available. One afternoon of work now can save months of repairs later.</p><p>Next, protect your openings. Measure every window and glass door now, while hardware stores still have shutters and plywood in stock. Label each panel so that, when a warning comes, fitting them takes an hour rather than a panic.</p><p>Finally, think beyond the house. Clean your cistern and gutters, trim branches that overhang the roof, and pack a kit with water, torches, medication and copies of your insurance papers in a sealed bag.</p><p>None of this is dramatic. That is the point. Preparation is quiet, cheap and boring; repair is loud, costly and heartbreaking. Choose boring.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of this feature is to persuade homeowners to secure their roofs, windows and supplies before hurricane season. The intended audience is homeowners across Saint Lucia, from young families to retirees, with mixed educational backgrounds. The context of communication is the home section of a weekend newspaper in May, when readers have time to plan. I used clear Standard English, direct address, and kairos and logos to make the advice urgent and convincing.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>In 200–250 words, a newspaper feature needs a hook, two or three organised pieces of advice and a sharp ending. Speak to your audience as \"you\", use one deliberate appeal, and proofread: expression covers register, grammar, punctuation and careful checking.</p>", "easy": {"title": "Section B practice: newspaper feature for homeowners (Foundation)", "bloom": "Apply", "genre": "Newspaper feature (guided)", "prompt": "<p>Topic: natural disasters. Audience: homeowners.</p><p>Write a short <b>newspaper feature</b> of 120–180 words for homeowners in Saint Lucia, giving advice on preparing their homes before hurricane season. Build it in four parts:</p><ol><li><b>Headline:</b> a short, catchy title.</li><li><b>Opening:</b> one or two sentences that speak to the reader (\"you\") and say why now.</li><li><b>Advice:</b> two or three tips, each starting with a linking word (\"First\", \"Next\", \"Finally\").</li><li><b>Ending:</b> one sentence telling readers what to do this week.</li></ol><p><b>Then</b> write a <b>justification</b> of 50–80 words, using the three starters.</p><p>[12 marks]</p>", "min": 170, "max": 260, "frame": "<p><b>Headline:</b> ...<br><b>Opening:</b> Hurricane season is ... away. Is your ...?<br><b>Advice:</b> First, ... Next, ... Finally, ...<br><b>Ending:</b> This week, ...</p><p><b>Justification</b><br>The purpose of this feature is to ...<br>The intended audience is ...<br>The context of communication is ...</p>", "checklist": ["I wrote a headline and an opening that speaks to \"you\".", "I gave two or three tips, each with a linking word.", "I ended by telling readers what to do this week.", "My justification has purpose, audience and context.", "I used capital letters and full stops correctly."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "Ideas for homeowners", "marks": 4, "look": ["\\b(you|your)\\b", "strap|shutter|cistern|water|kit|gutter|tree|insurance", "season|june|weeks|this week|now"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your advice is practical and speaks directly to homeowners. Next step: add one detail that makes a tip easy to picture.", "weak": "Speak to the reader as \"you\", give practical tips (straps, shutters, water) and say why now (\"Hurricane season is six weeks away\")."}, {"criterion": "Order of the feature", "marks": 3, "look": ["\\b(first|next|then|also|finally)\\b", "this week|today|now"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your feature moves from opening to tips to a clear ending.", "weak": "Start each tip with a linking word (\"First\", \"Next\", \"Finally\"), and finish with what readers should do this week."}, {"criterion": "Justification", "marks": 3, "look": ["purpose", "audience", "context"], "min": 3, "strong": "All three parts of the justification are there.", "weak": "Use all three starters: purpose, intended audience and context of communication."}, {"criterion": "Clear writing", "marks": 2, "look": ["[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]", ","], "min": 1, "strong": "Your sentences are clear and correctly punctuated.", "weak": "Start each sentence with a capital letter, end it with a full stop, and keep sentences short."}], "model": "<p><b>Is Your Roof Ready?</b></p><p>Hurricane season is six weeks away. If a storm came tonight, would your roof still be there in the morning?</p><p>First, check your roof. Hurricane straps hold the roof to the walls, and they cost far less than a new roof. Book a free inspection this month, before the appointments run out.</p><p>Next, protect your windows. Measure every window now and buy shutters while the hardware stores still have them. Label each one so you can fit it quickly.</p><p>Finally, store water. Clean your cistern and fill some bottles, so your family has safe water to drink if the pipes stop working after a storm.</p><p>This week, pick one of these jobs and finish it before Sunday.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of this feature is to persuade homeowners to prepare their homes before hurricane season. The intended audience is homeowners in Saint Lucia, especially those who have not yet fitted straps or shutters. The context of communication is the home section of a weekend newspaper in May, when readers have time to plan.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>Keep each tip short and practical, and speak to the reader as \"you\". Your justification should match your feature: who it is for, why you wrote it and where it would appear.</p>"}, "up": {"title": "Section B practice: newspaper feature for homeowners (Challenge)", "bloom": "Create", "genre": "Journalistic piece (expository) with justification (Section B standard, 40 marks)", "prompt": "<p><b>Topic: natural disasters. Audience: homeowners.</b></p><p>Write an <b>expository newspaper feature of 200–250 words</b> for the home section of a weekend newspaper, aimed at <b>first-time homeowners</b> in Saint Lucia who have not yet prepared their homes. Your feature must:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>open with a headline and a lead that speaks to this subgroup's real concerns;</li><li>follow a <b>problem → solution</b> pattern;</li><li>use the stimulus <b>selectively and in your own words</b>;</li><li><b>anticipate and answer</b> the objection \"I can't afford it right now\";</li><li>use at least TWO deliberate appeals, one of which is kairos;</li><li>end with a specific call to action.</li></ol><p><b>Then</b>, under the heading \"Justification\", write <b>50–100 words</b> explaining how your feature relates to its <b>purpose</b>, <b>intended audience</b> and <b>context of communication</b>.</p><p>[40 marks: piece 25 (Creativity 10, Organisation 7, Expression 8); justification 15 (purpose 5, intended audience 5, context of communication 5)]</p>", "min": 250, "max": 350, "frame": "<p><b>Headline + lead:</b> a hook aimed at new owners (mortgage, first home, first season).<br><b>Problem:</b> what fails first and what it costs (stimulus fact in your own words).<br><b>Solution:</b> two or three steps, in order of priority, with transitions.<br><b>Objection:</b> \"I can't afford it\" stated fairly, then answered (cost comparison, phased steps, free inspection).<br><b>Call to action:</b> one task, one deadline.</p><p><b>Justification:</b> purpose → specific audience → context, and how your register and appeals suit them.</p>", "checklist": ["My headline and lead speak to first-time owners, and the feature is 200–250 words.", "The feature follows problem → solution, with clear transitions.", "I used the stimulus selectively, in my own words.", "I stated and answered the cost objection, used kairos and at least one other appeal, and ended with a specific call to action.", "My 50–100 word justification explains how the feature relates to its purpose, intended audience and context."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "Creativity (unique use of genre, audience engagement, originality)", "marks": 10, "look": ["mortgage|first (home|house|season)|new (owner|home)", "afford|cost|budget|price|can't|cannot", "season|june|weeks|before the|this month", "\\b(you|your)\\b", "inspector|builder|expert|fraction"], "min": 3, "strong": "Your feature is written for new owners, not homeowners in general: the hook, the objection and the solutions all answer their real concerns. Next step: add one original local detail no other candidate would think of.", "weak": "Engage the subgroup directly: tie your hook to the mortgage or the first season in a new home, answer \"I can't afford it\" with a cost comparison or a phased plan, and use kairos (\"six weeks before the season\") to create urgency."}, {"criterion": "Organisation (introduction, development, conclusion, coherence)", "marks": 7, "look": ["\\b(first|next|then|however|but|because|so|finally)\\b", "\\n\\s*\\n|</p>\\s*<p>"], "min": 2, "strong": "A clear problem → solution structure, with the objection placed where it naturally arises and a crisp call to action. Next step: make your final line echo the headline.", "weak": "Organise the feature: headline and lead, the problem, the solutions in order of priority, the objection and its answer, then one specific call to action."}, {"criterion": "Expression (register suited to genre, grammar, punctuation, proofreading)", "marks": 8, "look": ["[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]", ",", "\\?"], "min": 2, "strong": "A confident, readable journalistic register in accurate Standard English. Next step: cut any sentence that repeats a point.", "weak": "Use a clear newspaper register: direct address, short paragraphs and accurate Standard English. Proofread for agreement and punctuation."}, {"criterion": "Justification: purpose", "marks": 5, "look": ["purpose", "\\bto (persuade|encourage|advise|inform|convince)"], "min": 2, "strong": "A precise purpose, connected to what the feature does.", "weak": "State the purpose with a precise verb and action: \"to persuade first-time homeowners to fit straps and shutters before June\"."}, {"criterion": "Justification: intended audience", "marks": 5, "look": ["audience", "first-time|new (owner|homeowner)|mortgage"], "min": 2, "strong": "A specific subgroup, with a detail that explains your choices.", "weak": "Name the subgroup and a relevant detail: \"first-time homeowners still paying a mortgage, who worry about extra costs\"."}, {"criterion": "Justification: context of communication", "marks": 5, "look": ["context", "newspaper|home section|weekend", "register|standard english|logos|kairos|appeal|question"], "min": 2, "strong": "A real, timed context, linked to your register and appeals.", "weak": "Give the place and time (the home section of a weekend newspaper in May) and explain how it shaped your register and appeals."}], "model": "<p><b>Your First Hurricane Season as a Homeowner</b></p><p>You have the keys, the mortgage and a long list of things to buy. Hurricane straps are probably not near the top. With the season six weeks away, they should be.</p><p>Roofs fail before walls. After last September's storm, inspectors found that most damaged homes in one district had roofs without hurricane straps. Once a roof lifts, rain finishes the job: ceilings, furniture, electrics and the savings you spent on them.</p><p>The solution starts above your head. Book one of the free roof inspections this month, then ask a qualified builder to fit straps where the inspector recommends them. Next, measure every window and glass door and buy or cut shutters before the hardware stores run out.</p><p>\"I can't afford it right now,\" you may be thinking. Insurance agents would answer that you cannot afford not to: preparation costs a fraction of a repair bill. If money is tight, do the roof first and the shutters next month. The inspection costs nothing.</p><p>New homeowners often feel they have plenty of time to prepare. The weather does not agree.</p><p>This weekend, book your inspection. Your first season in your own home should be one you remember for the right reasons, not for the repairs.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of this feature is to persuade first-time homeowners to secure their roofs and windows before hurricane season. The intended audience is new owners in Saint Lucia still paying mortgages, who worry about extra costs, so I answered the cost objection directly. The context of communication is the home section of a weekend newspaper in May, so I used a direct, conversational journalistic register, kairos and a cost comparison (logos).</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>Aiming at a subgroup is the quickest route to creativity marks, because every detail can answer a real concern. Answer the objection respectfully and practically, then end with one task and one deadline. Keep the piece within 200–250 words; the justification needs its own 50–100.</p>"}}]}, {"id": "P3U5", "title": "Section B: The Justification, the Notes Sheet and Exam Strategy", "objectives": ["Write a 50–100 word justification that explains how the piece relates to its purpose, intended audience and context of communication.", "Build a 10-point notes sheet from researched, evaluated sources on the year's topic.", "Manage time across Sections A and B and self-check before handing in."], "videos": [{"title": "Writing a rationale: purpose, audience, context", "q": "how to write a rationale purpose audience context for a piece of writing"}, {"title": "Exam time management", "q": "exam time management strategies writing paper students"}, {"title": "Making effective notes sheets", "q": "how to make effective notes sheets notes key points"}], "screens": [{"type": "learn", "title": "The justification: 15 marks in 50–100 words", "html": "<p>Question 3(b) asks for a paragraph of <b>50–100 words</b> justifying <b>the purpose</b>, <b>the intended audience</b> and <b>the context of communication</b> of your piece. It is worth <b>15 marks</b>, more than all of Question 1. Do not forget it.</p>\n<p>The bands reward how well you <b>explain how your piece is related to</b> your stated purpose, intended audience and the context in which it would be disseminated:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Quality of explanation</th><th>Marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Excellent</td><td>13–15</td></tr><tr><td>Good</td><td>10–12</td></tr><tr><td>Satisfactory</td><td>8–9</td></tr><tr><td>Fair</td><td>5–7</td></tr><tr><td>Weak</td><td>3–4</td></tr><tr><td>Unacceptable</td><td>0–2</td></tr></table></div><p>Within the band, the 15 marks are split equally: <b>purpose 5</b>, <b>audience 5</b> and <b>context 5</b>.</p>\n<p>So naming the three is not enough; the top band <b>connects</b> them to the piece. A reliable template:</p>\n<p class=\"passage\">The purpose of this [form] is to [precise verb] [audience] to [action]. The intended audience is [specific group], who [relevant detail]. The context of communication is [real-world place and occasion], so I used [register/language choice] and [technique or appeal] to [effect on this audience].</p>\n<div class=\"trio\">\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Purpose</span><p>Precise verb: inform, explain, persuade, warn, encourage, educate, entertain.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Audience</span><p>Specific: \"homeowners over sixty in hillside districts\", not \"everyone\".</p></div>\n<div class=\"tcard\"><span class=\"lbl\">Context</span><p>Where and how it appears: \"the home section of a weekend newspaper in May\". \"About hurricanes\" is content, not context.</p></div>\n</div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Draft the justification straight after planning, before writing the piece, so it can never be left out. Write it in Standard English, always.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "Worth 15", "p": "<p>The justification is worth 15 of the 40 Section B marks.</p>"}, {"h": "Connect, don't just list", "p": "<p>Explain how your piece's choices fit the purpose, audience and context.</p>"}, {"h": "When", "p": "<p>Draft it right after planning.</p>"}], "plain": "<p>After your piece, you write a <b>justification</b> of 50–100 words. It is worth <b>15 marks</b>, more than all of Question 1. Explain three things: the <b>purpose</b> (what you want readers to do), the <b>intended audience</b> (exactly who), and the <b>context</b> (where and when it would appear). For example: \"a feature in the home section of a Jamaican weekend newspaper in May\".</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: connect, do not list.</b> Three labelled sentences reach the middle bands; the top band explains how choices in your piece relate to each element. Add one \"so\" clause per element: \"...first-time owners still paying mortgages, <i>so</i> I compared the cost of straps with one month's payment.\" Precision matters everywhere: a purpose needs a precise verb and an action; an audience needs a subgroup and a relevant detail; a context needs a place, a time and a reason the audience would be receptive there. The most common error is writing content as context (\"The context is hurricanes\"). Another is overshooting 100 words by summarising the piece. Draft the justification straight after planning, so it is never squeezed out by time.</p>"}, {"type": "pick", "game": true, "title": "Find the context sentence", "intro": "<p>Read Kimani's 2027 justification. Tap the sentence that names the <b>context of communication</b>.</p>", "q": "Tap the context sentence.", "lines": ["The purpose of this reflective piece is to encourage parents to replace sweet drinks with water by sharing one mother's experience.", "The intended audience is parents of primary school children in Trinidad and Tobago, many of them busy and without medical training.", "It would be published in the parents' newsletter sent home in book bags during Health Week.", "The first-person voice and the image of the juice box create pathos so that parents see themselves in the story."], "correct": [2], "win": "Correct. A newsletter sent home during Health Week is where and how the piece reaches its audience: the context of communication.", "miss": "Look for the sentence that answers \"where and how would people meet this piece?\"", "tutor": [{"h": "Purpose = why", "p": "<p>\"To encourage parents...\"</p>"}, {"h": "Audience = who", "p": "<p>\"Parents of primary school children...\"</p>"}, {"h": "Context = where and how", "p": "<p>A newsletter, sent home, during Health Week.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Find the context sentence (Foundation)", "lines": ["The purpose is to encourage parents to choose water.", "It would be published in the parents' newsletter sent home in book bags.", "The audience is parents of primary school children."], "correct": [1], "win": "Yes. The newsletter is the context.", "miss": "Which sentence names a place or publication?"}, "up": {"title": "Find the context sentence (Challenge)", "bloom": "Evaluate", "intro": "<p>Read Tamika's justification for her 2026 newspaper feature for first-time homeowners. Some sentences only name a label; others <b>connect</b> a choice in her piece to her purpose, audience or context. The connecting sentences are what lift a justification into the top band.</p>", "q": "Tap EVERY sentence that connects a choice in her piece to its purpose, audience or context.", "lines": ["The purpose of this feature is to persuade first-time homeowners to fit hurricane straps before June.", "The intended audience is homeowners in Belize who have bought their first house in the last five years.", "Because many of them are still paying a mortgage, I compared the cost of straps with one month's mortgage payment, which makes the advice feel affordable.", "The context of communication is the home section of a weekend newspaper in May.", "Readers browse that section at leisure, so I used a question headline and short paragraphs that can be read over a Saturday breakfast.", "The feature is about hurricanes and roofs.", "I wrote in clear Standard English with direct address (\"you\"), so that new owners feel personally responsible for their roofs."], "correct": [2, 4, 6], "win": "Exactly. Sentences 3, 5 and 7 each link a choice (a cost comparison, the headline and paragraphing, the register and direct address) to the audience or the context. Sentences 1, 2 and 4 are useful labels, but on their own they only name. Sentence 6 is content, not context.", "miss": "Look for a choice in the piece AND the reason for it (\"because...\", \"so...\"). A sentence that only states the purpose, audience or context names a label; a sentence about the subject is content, not context."}}, {"type": "mcq", "title": "Which justification reaches the top band?", "q": "For a 2026 reflective journal entry by a homeowner, which justification is strongest?", "options": [{"t": "\"The purpose of this reflective entry is to persuade fellow homeowners to prepare early by sharing the cost of my own delay. The intended audience is homeowners in Grenada who, like me, tend to wait for an official warning. The context of communication is a community blog run by a district residents' association, read in the weeks before hurricane season, so I used a personal first-person voice and pathos to make the lesson feel real.\"", "ok": true, "fb": "Top band. Precise purpose, a specific audience with a relevant detail, a real-world context, and a clear explanation of how the voice and appeal suit that audience. About 80 words."}, {"t": "\"The purpose is to inform. The audience is homeowners. The context is a blog.\"", "fb": "All three labels appear, but nothing is explained and it is far below 50 words. It names without connecting, so it cannot reach the top band."}, {"t": "\"I wrote a journal because journals are personal. It is about hurricanes and it is for people.\"", "fb": "No purpose verb, a vague audience (\"people\") and content (\"about hurricanes\") instead of context. This would sit in a low band."}, {"t": "\"My journal entry has imagery, a turning point and a conclusion, and it is well organised and interesting.\"", "fb": "This describes features of the piece, which can be one supporting sentence, but it never addresses purpose, audience or context."}], "tutor": [{"h": "Three checks", "p": "<p>Precise purpose? Specific audience? Real-world context?</p>"}, {"h": "Plus connection", "p": "<p>Does it explain how the piece's choices suit them? That lifts it to the top band.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Strongest justification (Foundation)", "q": "Which justification names a precise purpose, a specific audience AND a real-world context?", "options": [{"t": "\"It is about hurricanes and it is for people.\"", "fb": "Vague audience, and \"about hurricanes\" is content."}, {"t": "\"It has imagery and a conclusion.\"", "fb": "That describes features, not purpose, audience or context."}, {"t": "\"The purpose is to persuade homeowners in Grenada to prepare early. It would appear on a residents' association blog before hurricane season.\"", "ok": true, "fb": "Yes. Purpose, audience and context are all specific."}]}, "up": {"title": "Which justification reaches the top band? (Challenge)", "bloom": "Evaluate", "q": "Four justifications for the same 2026 expository feature for first-time homeowners. Which would reach the TOP band?", "options": [{"t": "\"This feature persuades people. Everyone should prepare for hurricanes, whether they own a home or not, because storms affect us all. It would be published somewhere people can read it, and the language is simple so that it is understood.\"", "fb": "There is an attempt to connect language to understanding, but \"everyone\" is not an audience and \"somewhere people can read it\" is not a context."}, {"t": "\"The purpose of this expository feature is to inform homeowners about hurricanes. The intended audience is homeowners. The context of communication is hurricanes and how they damage roofs, so I used facts.\"", "fb": "The purpose verb is weak for a feature that urges action, the audience is not specific, and the \"context\" is really content."}, {"t": "\"The purpose of this feature is to persuade first-time homeowners in the Cayman Islands to fit hurricane straps before June. Many are still paying mortgages and fear extra costs, so I compared the price of straps with a single month's payment. The context of communication is the home section of a weekend newspaper in May, when readers have time to plan, so I used a question headline, short paragraphs and clear Standard English.\"", "ok": true, "fb": "Top band. Each element is precise, and each is connected to a choice in the piece. At about 75 words, it is comfortably within the limit."}, {"t": "\"The purpose is to persuade, the audience is first-time homeowners, and the context is a weekend newspaper.\"", "fb": "Accurate labels, but nothing is explained or connected, and at under 20 words it is far below the 50-word minimum."}]}}, {"type": "learn", "title": "Building your notes sheet from research", "html": "<p>Your notes (for 2027, <b>a single A4 sheet, double-sided</b>) may hold <b>no more than 10 points</b> related to the topic of your response. Because you know the topic and audience in advance, you can build it from real research.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1: start from tested research.</b> Use only sources you have already evaluated for credibility, currency and reliability (see 4.2, Researching and evaluating sources for Section A).</p>\n<p><b>Step 2: condense to 10.</b> Keep points that are relevant, usable in any form, hard to remember exactly, and short.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Type</th><th>2026 example point (homeowners)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Key terms</td><td>Storm surge; hurricane straps; watch vs warning</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Risks</td><td>Roofs fail first; flying debris breaks windows</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Actions</td><td>Straps, shutters, trim trees, clean cistern and drains</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kit</td><td>Water, torch, radio, medication, documents in sealed bag</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Local detail</td><td>Hillside runoff; coastal flooding near the lagoon</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Source</td><td>\"National disaster management preparedness guide\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hooks</td><td>Kairos: \"six weeks to season\"; pathos: the sound of galvanise tearing</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Practise expanding every point into a paragraph from memory. If you cannot, the point is not earning its place.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "Tested research only", "p": "<p>Use the sources you evaluated in 4.2.</p>"}, {"h": "Ten or fewer", "p": "<p>Short, relevant, useful points: terms, risks, actions, local detail, a source, hooks.</p>"}], "plain": "<p>Your one notes sheet can hold up to <b>10 short points</b>. Build it from sources you have already checked (see 4.2). Keep only short, useful points, such as key words, facts, one source and a hook. A government disaster guide passes the test; a forwarded voice note about \"storm-proof paint\" in Belize does not.</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: design the sheet for any question the paper might ask.</b> You do not know the exact form or stimulus in advance, so each point must be portable: usable in a journal entry, a feature or an essay. Balance three kinds of point: <b>knowledge</b> (accurate terms and facts with a source, for logos and ethos), <b>audience insight</b> (what this audience worries about, for engagement) and <b>craft</b> (a hook, a closing line, a planning prompt for the justification). Test every point with two questions: Could I expand this into a paragraph from memory? Would this audience care? Cut points that fail either test. A common error is copying sentences onto the sheet, which wastes space and tempts you to paste them into your piece, costing creativity marks.</p>"}, {"type": "sort", "game": true, "title": "Does it earn a place on the sheet?", "intro": "<p>Jaheim is building his 2027 notes sheet (NCDs in children; parents). Sort each possible point.</p>", "main": "Possible notes sheet points", "bins": ["Keep", "Cut"], "items": [{"t": "Adult lung cancer statistics from North America", "bin": 1, "fb": "Wrong age group, wrong focus, wrong region."}, {"t": "Hook: \"Small swaps today protect your child tomorrow\"", "bin": 0, "fb": "A ready-made message line for parents."}, {"t": "A copied paragraph from a website", "bin": 1, "fb": "Paragraphs waste space and tempt copying. Turn it into a short keyword point."}, {"t": "Causes: daily sweet drinks; screens over outdoor play", "bin": 0, "fb": "Short, relevant and easy to expand."}, {"t": "\"Health is important for everyone.\"", "bin": 1, "fb": "Too vague. It carries no information you could not invent on the day."}, {"t": "NCD = not contagious; e.g. type 2 diabetes, hypertension", "bin": 0, "fb": "A key definition he can use in any form."}, {"t": "Source: regional health agency report on school children's diets (recent)", "bin": 0, "fb": "A credible, current source he can cite for ethos and logos."}], "win": "Well judged. Keep short, relevant, credible points; cut the vague, off-topic and copied ones.", "tutor": [{"h": "Keep", "p": "<p>Definitions, causes, actions, sources, hooks: all relevant to children and parents.</p>"}, {"h": "Cut", "p": "<p>Off-topic, vague or copied material.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Keep or cut? (Foundation)", "items": [{"t": "NCD definition with examples", "bin": 0, "fb": "Useful and relevant."}, {"t": "Adult lung cancer in North America", "bin": 1, "fb": "Off-topic."}, {"t": "\"Health is important.\"", "bin": 1, "fb": "Too vague."}], "win": "Good. Relevant and specific stays."}, "up": {"title": "Does it earn a place on the sheet? (Challenge)", "bloom": "Evaluate", "intro": "<p>Jaheim is building his notes sheet for the 2026 topic: natural disasters, for homeowners. He has twelve possible points and room for ten at most. Judge each one: keep it, or cut it.</p>", "items": [{"t": "Straps: roof-to-wall; cost a fraction of a roof repair", "bin": 0, "fb": "Short, accurate and useful in any form for homeowners."}, {"t": "Watch = conditions possible within 48 hours; warning = expected within 36 hours", "bin": 0, "fb": "Precise terms that are easy to confuse under pressure: worth a place."}, {"t": "Source: national disaster office preparedness guide (current season)", "bin": 0, "fb": "A credible, current source he can cite for ethos."}, {"t": "Insurance: check exclusions; photograph contents before the season", "bin": 0, "fb": "Practical advice that homeowners care about."}, {"t": "Kit: water (1 gallon per person per day), torch, radio, medication, documents", "bin": 0, "fb": "Specific and hard to remember exactly: exactly what the sheet is for."}, {"t": "Hook: \"Preparation is quiet; repair is loud\"", "bin": 0, "fb": "A ready-made line for an opening or closing."}, {"t": "The history of a famous eighteenth-century hurricane", "bin": 1, "fb": "Interesting, but it will not help homeowners act this season, and it takes a precious point."}, {"t": "Earthquake drill steps for primary schools", "bin": 1, "fb": "Wrong audience. Homeowners, not schools."}, {"t": "The full introduction of his practice essay, written in tiny print", "bin": 1, "fb": "A copied paragraph wastes space and tempts him to reproduce it instead of writing for the question."}, {"t": "Forwarded voice note: \"hurricane-proof paint\" stops roof damage", "bin": 1, "fb": "Unverified and unreliable. It fails the credibility test."}, {"t": "\"Hurricanes are dangerous.\"", "bin": 1, "fb": "Too vague. It carries nothing he could not think of on the day."}, {"t": "Survey: about 6 in 10 households waited for the warning", "bin": 1, "fb": "This figure comes from one practice stimulus, not from his research. On exam day, the stimulus is provided, so a sheet point should come from a source he has checked himself."}], "win": "Well judged. Keep points that are accurate, sourced, portable and useful to this audience. Cut anything vague, off-topic, copied or unverified."}}, {"type": "checkin", "title": "Quick check-in"}, {"type": "learn", "title": "Section B exam strategy: time, plan, write, check", "html": "<p>Section B is worth <b>40 of the 60 marks</b>, twice Section A, so your exam routine must protect time for it.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What you do</th><th>Share of your time</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1. Read and mark up</b></td><td>Read the stimulus twice. Underline the command words, the form (\"reflective OR expository\"), the audience and the word limits.</td><td>About 10%</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2. Section A</b></td><td>Answer Questions 1 and 2 concisely: explain, do not just identify. A two-mark comment needs one well-explained point, not a paragraph.</td><td>About 30%</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3. Plan Section B</b></td><td>Use the planning chain (Audience → Purpose → Context → Message → Language and Register → Tone and Medium) and choose the notes-sheet points you will use.</td><td>About 10%</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4. Draft the justification</b></td><td>Write the 50–100 word justification early, while your plan is fresh: purpose, intended audience, context of communication.</td><td>About 10%</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>5. Write the piece</b></td><td>Write the 200–250 word reflective or expository piece, using your notes-sheet points as anchors.</td><td>About 30%</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>6. Self-check</b></td><td>Every part answered; word limits kept; justification complete; Standard English proofread.</td><td>About 10%</td></tr></table></div>\n<h4>Five strategy rules</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Let the marks plan your time.</b> Section B earns two-thirds of the marks; give it about 60% of your time.</li>\n<li><b>Never leave the justification to the end.</b> At 15 marks it is worth more than all of Question 1.</li>\n<li><b>Count words, do not guess them.</b> Count three typical lines, find the average per line and multiply by your number of lines.</li>\n<li><b>Write for the stated audience.</b> Every choice of register, tone and example should suit them, and your justification should say so.</li>\n<li><b>Do not copy the notes sheet.</b> Expand each point in your own words; copied sentences cost Creativity marks.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> If time runs short, finish the justification and a complete conclusion before polishing anything else. A complete answer scores more than a perfect half.</p>", "plain": "<p>Section B is worth the most marks, so give it the most time. Read and underline first, answer Section A briefly, plan Section B, write the justification early, then write your piece and check everything at the end.</p>", "deeper": "<p><b>Challenge: build your personal timing card.</b> Using the shares above, write down the clock time at which you will start each stage in your actual exam session. Practise one full Paper 032 against your card and note where you lost time; adjust the card, not your Section B time.</p>", "tutor": [{"h": "Marks decide minutes", "p": "<p>Section B is 40 of 60 marks: give it about 60% of your time.</p>"}, {"h": "Justify early", "p": "<p>Draft the 50–100 word justification straight after your plan.</p>"}, {"h": "Check last", "p": "<p>All parts answered, limits kept, Standard English proofread.</p>"}]}, {"type": "order", "game": true, "title": "Your 2 hours 15 minutes", "intro": "<p>Put Shanice's exam-day routine in the most effective order.</p>", "blocks": [{"t": "Read the central stimulus twice and read every question carefully, underlining command words."}, {"t": "Identify the marks for each part and plan time in proportion (Section A = 20 marks, Section B = 40)."}, {"t": "Annotate the stimulus: target group, variety and register, key message points."}, {"t": "Answer Section A, Questions 1 and 2: explain, do not just identify."}, {"t": "Plan Section B with the planning chain, then draft the justification."}, {"t": "Write the 200–250 word piece, using notes sheet points as anchors."}, {"t": "Self-check: all parts answered, word limits kept, justification complete, Standard English proofread."}], "win": "Exactly. Read → Identify the marks → Annotate → Plan → Write → Check. Section B is worth twice Section A, so protect your time for it.", "tutor": [{"h": "Understand first", "p": "<p>Read, identify marks and annotate before writing anything.</p>"}, {"h": "Section B time", "p": "<p>40 of 60 marks: give it the most time, and draft the justification early.</p>"}, {"h": "Check last", "p": "<p>Always finish with a self-check.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Order the routine (Foundation)", "blocks": [{"t": "Read the stimulus and questions.", "tag": "Read"}, {"t": "Answer Section A.", "tag": "Section A"}, {"t": "Plan, justify and write Section B.", "tag": "Section B"}, {"t": "Self-check everything.", "tag": "Check"}], "win": "Good. Read, Section A, Section B, check."}, "up": {"title": "Your 2 hours 15 minutes (Challenge)", "bloom": "Analyse", "intro": "<p>Shanice's full exam-day routine has eight steps, and each one gives its reason. Put them in the most effective order.</p>", "blocks": [{"t": "Read the stimulus twice and every question once, underlining command words and limits such as \"TWO\", \"other than print\" and \"200–250 words\", because one missed word can cost a whole part."}, {"t": "Note the marks for each part and set finishing times, so that Section B, worth twice Section A, gets about twice the time."}, {"t": "Annotate the stimulus with the target group, the variety and register, and the key message points, because Section A draws on all three."}, {"t": "Answer Questions 1 and 2, explaining rather than identifying, and stop at the time you set even if one answer could be polished."}, {"t": "Plan Section B with the planning chain and choose the form that best suits the audience."}, {"t": "Draft the justification while the plan is fresh, so that its 15 marks cannot be squeezed out at the end."}, {"t": "Write the 200–250 word piece, using notes-sheet points as anchors rather than sentences to copy."}, {"t": "Check every part: word counts, all questions answered, justification complete, Standard English proofread."}], "win": "Excellent. A workable timing is about 15 minutes to read, plan the time and annotate, about 35 minutes for Section A, about 70 minutes for Section B (plan, justification, piece) and 15 minutes to check. Drafting the justification before the piece protects 15 marks."}}, {"type": "multi", "game": true, "title": "Common mistakes clinic", "intro": "<p>Each student made a common Paper 032 mistake. Choose the best fix.</p>", "items": [{"text": "Andre's Q1(a): \"Literacy and cost.\"", "options": ["Write it in capitals", "Add a third challenge", "Explain each challenge and its effect on the target group"], "answer": 2, "fb": "Identifying only earns 1 mark in total; explaining each earns 2."}, {"text": "Bianca's Q1(b) named the newspaper and Instagram.", "options": ["Add Facebook too", "Choose traditional media other than print, such as radio or a loudspeaker van", "Explain the newspaper more"], "answer": 1, "fb": "The question excluded print and asked for traditional media."}, {"text": "Carlos wrote a 340-word Section B piece.", "options": ["Remove the conclusion only", "Cut to 200–250 words", "Keep it; more is better"], "answer": 1, "fb": "Stay within 200–250 words; staying within the limit is part of the skill."}, {"text": "Dana's justification: \"The audience is everyone.\"", "options": ["Write \"the whole world\"", "Leave the audience out", "Name a specific group, e.g. \"homeowners over sixty in hillside districts\""], "answer": 2, "fb": "\"Everyone\" is not an audience. Be specific."}, {"text": "Errol's slogan had no explanation.", "options": ["Add a picture", "Make the slogan longer", "Name the technique and explain how it works for the issue"], "answer": 2, "fb": "The explanation of the technique is worth half the slogan marks."}], "win": "Great clinic work. Explain rather than identify, obey the question's limits, keep word counts, be specific about audience, and always explain your technique.", "tutor": [{"h": "Common traps", "p": "<p>Identify-only answers, ignoring \"traditional\" or \"other than print\", word counts, vague audiences, unexplained slogans.</p>"}, {"h": "Fix pattern", "p": "<p>Explain, obey, count, specify, link.</p>"}], "easy": {"title": "Mistakes clinic (Foundation)", "items": [{"text": "\"Literacy and cost.\"", "options": ["Keep it", "Explain each one"], "answer": 1, "fb": "Explaining earns the marks."}, {"text": "A 340-word Section B piece.", "options": ["Keep it", "Cut to 200–250"], "answer": 1, "fb": "Stay within the limit."}, {"text": "\"The audience is everyone.\"", "options": ["Keep it", "Name a specific group"], "answer": 1, "fb": "Be specific."}], "win": "Good. Explain, count, specify."}, "up": {"title": "Common mistakes clinic (Challenge)", "bloom": "Evaluate", "intro": "<p>Subtler mistakes this time. Each answer looks reasonable at first glance. Choose the fix that would earn the most marks.</p>", "items": [{"text": "Fatima's Q1(b): \"Radio is appropriate because it is cheap and reaches everyone. Television is appropriate because it is cheap and reaches everyone.\"", "options": ["Add a third medium", "Replace television with a flyer", "Give each medium its own reason linked to the group, such as television showing how to fit straps", "Shorten both answers"], "answer": 2, "fb": "Two identical reasons justify neither medium well. Each needs a strength only it offers, tied to the group. A flyer is print."}, {"text": "Gerard's Q2(a): \"The PSA uses Standard English because it is formal, and a formal register because it is Standard English.\"", "options": ["Give each label its own real reason: Standard English is the official variety; the register is formal because the matter is serious", "Add a third label", "Say the PSA uses Creole", "Swap the two sentences"], "answer": 0, "fb": "A circular answer explains nothing. Each label needs an independent reason."}, {"text": "Hana's justification: \"The context of communication is natural disasters and how they affect homes.\"", "options": ["Move it to the end", "Add more about disasters", "Replace the content with where and how the piece appears, such as the home section of a weekend newspaper in May", "Delete the context sentence"], "answer": 2, "fb": "Natural disasters are the content. Context is the place, time and channel through which the audience meets the piece."}, {"text": "Ivan's reflective piece describes the storm vividly but ends: \"And then the storm passed.\"", "options": ["Add a title", "Turn it into a list of tips", "Add more description of the wind", "Add a turning point or lesson that shows what he learnt and why it matters to homeowners"], "answer": 3, "fb": "Reflection must arrive at insight. Without a lesson, it is only a narrative."}, {"text": "Jada's 245-word piece copies four sentences from the PSA.", "options": ["Copy the whole PSA for accuracy", "Put quotation marks around the copied sentences and keep them", "Cut the piece to 150 words", "Use one or two facts selectively, in her own words, and add original ideas and details"], "answer": 3, "fb": "Quotation marks avoid plagiarism, but long quotations still show little creativity. Use the stimulus selectively and make the piece your own."}, {"text": "Kofi's justification is 140 words long and summarises his piece paragraph by paragraph.", "options": ["Cut it to 50–100 words by removing the summary and keeping purpose, audience and context, each connected to his choices", "Remove the audience sentence", "Keep it; more explanation earns more marks", "Turn it into bullet points"], "answer": 0, "fb": "The justification must be 50–100 words. A summary of the piece is not a justification; connections to purpose, audience and context are."}], "win": "Great clinic work. The fixes all come down to precision: a distinct reason for every choice, context not content, insight not narration, selective use of the stimulus and word limits respected."}}, {"type": "write", "title": "Section B practice: expository essay for parents", "genre": "Expository essay", "stimulus": "<blockquote class=\"passage\"><p><b>Stimulus:</b> A school nurse writes in a local newsletter: \"Ten years ago I rarely spoke to parents about blood sugar. Now it is a regular conversation. Most of what I see could be prevented by changes at home: fewer sweet drinks, more active play, and parents who model healthy habits.\"</p></blockquote>", "prompt": "<p><b>2027 topic: non-communicable diseases in children. Audience: parents.</b></p><p>Write an <b>expository essay of 200–250 words</b> explaining to parents how everyday choices at home can reduce their children's risk of NCDs. Include a clear thesis, organised body paragraphs with transitional words and phrases, at least one deliberate rhetorical appeal, and a conclusion shorter than your introduction.</p><p><b>Then, in the same box, write a justification of 50–100 words</b> under the heading \"Justification\", explaining the <b>purpose</b>, <b>intended audience</b> and <b>context of communication</b>, and how your choices suit them. (Total for both parts: about 250–350 words.)</p>", "min": 250, "max": 350, "frame": "<p><b>Introduction:</b> context → main point → purpose → thesis.</p><p><b>Body:</b> two paragraphs following the thesis order, with transitions.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> short; no new points.</p><p><b>Justification:</b> \"The purpose of this essay is to... The intended audience is... The context of communication is..., so I...\"</p>", "checklist": ["My essay is 200–250 words with a clear thesis, body paragraphs in thesis order, and a short conclusion.", "I used transitional words and phrases and at least one deliberate appeal.", "I wrote for parents in clear Standard English, without jargon.", "I wrote a separate 50–100 word justification covering purpose, intended audience and context of communication, and how my choices suit them."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "Content and audience engagement", "marks": 5, "look": ["\\bparent", "sweet drink|soda|sugar|water", "play|active|screen|exercise", "\\b(you|your)\\b"], "min": 3, "strong": "You explain practical home choices and speak directly to parents. Next step: anticipate one objection (\"my child won't drink water\") and answer it.", "weak": "Focus on what parents can do at home and speak to them: \"When you swap one sweet drink a day for water, you...\""}, {"criterion": "Organisation", "marks": 5, "look": ["\\b(firstly|first|secondly|in addition|furthermore|moreover|however|therefore|as a result|finally|in conclusion)\\b", "\\b(this essay|can reduce|three|two)\\b"], "min": 2, "strong": "Clear thesis and well-linked paragraphs. Next step: make the conclusion echo your opening image.", "weak": "End your introduction with a thesis (\"Parents can reduce their children's risk through drinks, play and example\") and open each body paragraph with a transition."}, {"criterion": "Justification (purpose, audience, context)", "marks": 5, "look": ["purpose", "audience", "context", "newsletter|newspaper|radio|blog|magazine"], "min": 3, "strong": "Your justification connects your choices to purpose, audience and context. Next step: name the appeal you relied on most.", "weak": "Write 50–100 words: \"The purpose is to explain... The intended audience is parents of children aged 6–12... The context of communication is a school newsletter..., so I used plain Standard English.\""}, {"criterion": "Expression", "marks": 5, "look": ["[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]", ","], "min": 2, "strong": "Accurate, plain Standard English that parents can follow easily. Next step: vary sentence openings.", "weak": "Proofread for grammar and punctuation; avoid accidental Creole and showy words."}], "model": "<p><b>Small Choices, Strong Children</b></p><p>Type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure were once adult illnesses. Today, school nurses across the Caribbean are discussing them with parents of ten-year-olds. The good news is that most of this risk is shaped at home. Parents can protect their children through what they serve, how their children spend their afternoons, and the example they set.</p><p>Firstly, what children drink matters. A sweet drink with every meal adds sugar that a growing body does not need. Keeping cold water in the fridge, adding a slice of lime or cucumber for flavour, and saving juice for birthdays and special occasions is cheap, simple and powerful. Within a few weeks, most children stop asking for soda at every meal.</p><p>In addition, children need to move. Hot afternoons and screens keep many indoors, yet one hour of play, whether football, swimming at the beach or dancing in the yard, strengthens the heart and burns energy. Setting a simple rule, such as no screens until after an hour outdoors, makes play a habit rather than a battle.</p><p>Finally, children copy what they see. A parent who drinks water and takes an evening walk on the boardwalk teaches more than any lecture.</p><p>Small choices, repeated daily, become a child's future.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of this essay is to explain how everyday home choices reduce children's risk of NCDs and to encourage parents to act. The intended audience is parents of primary school children in Guyana, often busy and without medical training. The context of communication is the school newsletter sent home during Health Week, so I used plain Standard English, short paragraphs, clear transitions and logos with a gentle appeal to parental care.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>At 200–250 words, an essay needs a crisp thesis, two or three short developed points and a brief conclusion. Then make your justification connect your choices to the audience: the top band rewards explanation, not a list of three labels.</p>", "easy": {"title": "Section B practice: expository essay for parents (Foundation)", "bloom": "Apply", "genre": "Expository essay (guided)", "prompt": "<p>Topic: non-communicable diseases in children. Audience: parents.</p><p>Write a short <b>expository essay</b> of 120–180 words explaining to parents how choices at home can keep their children healthy. Build it in four parts:</p><ol><li><b>Introduction:</b> one or two sentences on the problem, then a thesis naming your two points.</li><li><b>Point 1:</b> drinks (for example, water instead of sweet drinks).</li><li><b>Point 2:</b> play (for example, an hour outdoors instead of screens).</li><li><b>Conclusion:</b> one short sentence.</li></ol><p><b>Then</b> write a <b>justification</b> of 50–80 words, using the three starters.</p><p>[12 marks]</p>", "min": 170, "max": 260, "frame": "<p><b>Introduction:</b> Today, more children ... Parents can help through ... and ...<br><b>Point 1:</b> Firstly, ... For example, ...<br><b>Point 2:</b> In addition, ... For example, ...<br><b>Conclusion:</b> Small choices ...</p><p><b>Justification</b><br>The purpose of this essay is to ...<br>The intended audience is ...<br>The context of communication is ...</p>", "checklist": ["My introduction ends with a thesis naming my two points.", "Each body paragraph starts with a linking word and gives an example.", "My conclusion is one short sentence.", "My justification has purpose, audience and context.", "I wrote for parents in clear Standard English."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "Ideas for parents", "marks": 4, "look": ["\\bparent", "sweet drink|soda|sugar|water|juice", "play|active|screen|exercise|outdoor"], "min": 2, "strong": "You give parents practical choices about drinks and play. Next step: add one example that is easy to picture, such as cold water in the fridge.", "weak": "Give parents two practical choices: drinks (water instead of sweet drinks) and play (an hour outdoors), each with an example."}, {"criterion": "Order of the essay", "marks": 3, "look": ["\\b(firstly|first|in addition|also|secondly|finally)\\b", "\\b(for example|such as)\\b"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your essay moves from thesis to two points to a short conclusion.", "weak": "Begin each point with a linking word (\"Firstly\", \"In addition\") and give an example (\"For example...\")."}, {"criterion": "Justification", "marks": 3, "look": ["purpose", "audience", "context"], "min": 3, "strong": "All three parts of the justification are there.", "weak": "Use all three starters: purpose, intended audience and context of communication."}, {"criterion": "Clear writing", "marks": 2, "look": ["[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]", ","], "min": 1, "strong": "Your writing is clear and correct.", "weak": "Write in short, complete Standard English sentences, with capital letters and full stops."}], "model": "<p><b>Healthy Habits Start at Home</b></p><p>Today, more children are developing illnesses such as type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure. Parents can help at home through what their children drink and how they play.</p><p>Firstly, choose water. Many children drink juice or soda with every meal, which adds a lot of sugar to their bodies. For example, keep a jug of cold water in the fridge and save sweet drinks for birthdays and holidays.</p><p>In addition, help children move. Screens keep many children indoors after school, sitting still for hours. For example, make a family rule: one hour of football, skipping or swimming before any screen time.</p><p>Small choices at home today can protect your child's health for the rest of their life.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of this essay is to explain to parents how simple choices at home can lower their children's risk of illness. The intended audience is parents of primary school children, many of them busy working adults. The context of communication is the school newsletter sent home in book bags during Health Week.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>Name your two points in your thesis, then write one paragraph for each, in the same order. Keep your language plain: parents want clear, practical advice.</p>"}, "up": {"title": "Section B practice: expository essay for parents (Challenge)", "bloom": "Create", "genre": "Expository essay with justification (Section B standard, 40 marks)", "prompt": "<p><b>Topic: non-communicable diseases in children. Audience: parents.</b></p><p>Write an <b>expository essay of 200–250 words</b> for parents of primary school children in Barbados, explaining how everyday choices at home can reduce their children's risk of NCDs. Your essay must:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>open with an engaging introduction and a thesis that previews your points;</li><li>use a <b>cause → effect</b> pattern in at least one body paragraph;</li><li>use the nurse's observation in the stimulus <b>selectively and in your own words</b> to build ethos;</li><li><b>anticipate and answer</b> a realistic objection, such as \"My child refuses to drink water\";</li><li>end with a conclusion shorter than your introduction.</li></ol><p><b>Then</b>, under the heading \"Justification\", write <b>50–100 words</b> explaining how your essay relates to its <b>purpose</b>, <b>intended audience</b> and <b>context of communication</b>.</p><p>[40 marks: piece 25 (Creativity 10, Organisation 7, Expression 8); justification 15 (purpose 5, intended audience 5, context of communication 5)]</p>", "min": 250, "max": 350, "frame": "<p><b>Introduction:</b> a hook from family life → the problem → thesis previewing two or three points.<br><b>Body 1 (cause → effect):</b> a habit → what it does to a child's body → the long-term risk.<br><b>Body 2:</b> the nurse's observation in your own words (ethos) → a practical change.<br><b>Objection:</b> state it fairly (\"Many parents will say...\") → a practical answer.<br><b>Conclusion:</b> one or two sentences, shorter than the introduction.</p><p><b>Justification:</b> purpose → specific audience → context, and how your pattern, appeals and register suit them.</p>", "checklist": ["My introduction hooks parents and ends with a thesis previewing my points; the essay is 200–250 words.", "At least one body paragraph follows cause → effect.", "I used the nurse's observation selectively, in my own words, to build ethos.", "I stated and answered a realistic objection, and my conclusion is shorter than my introduction.", "My 50–100 word justification explains how the essay relates to its purpose, intended audience and context."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "Creativity (unique use of genre, audience engagement, originality)", "marks": 10, "look": ["\\bparent", "nurse|school nurse", "refuse|won't|will not|many parents|you may (say|think)|but my child", "sweet drink|soda|sugar|juice|water", "play|active|screen|outdoor|walk", "\\b(you|your)\\b"], "min": 4, "strong": "Your essay engages parents as partners: a familiar hook, the nurse's credible voice in your own words and an objection answered with practical empathy. Next step: add one original, local detail that makes the advice feel Barbadian rather than generic.", "weak": "Engage parents directly with a moment from family life, use the nurse's observation in your own words for credibility, and answer a real objection (\"My child refuses water\") with a practical tip (add lime or cucumber; let the child choose a special bottle)."}, {"criterion": "Organisation (introduction, development, conclusion, coherence)", "marks": 7, "look": ["\\b(firstly|first|in addition|furthermore|however|therefore|as a result|consequently|finally)\\b", "\\b(leads to|causes|because|as a result|over time)\\b", "\\n\\s*\\n|</p>\\s*<p>"], "min": 2, "strong": "The thesis previews the body, the cause → effect paragraph is easy to follow, and the conclusion is brief. Next step: make the conclusion echo your opening image.", "weak": "Preview your points in the thesis and follow that order. In one paragraph, show cause and effect clearly (\"Because..., ... As a result, ...\"). Keep the conclusion shorter than the introduction."}, {"criterion": "Expression (register suited to genre, grammar, punctuation, proofreading)", "marks": 8, "look": ["[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]", ",", "[;:]"], "min": 2, "strong": "Plain, warm, accurate Standard English that parents can follow easily, with no jargon. Next step: vary sentence openings.", "weak": "Use plain Standard English and explain any medical term. Proofread for agreement and punctuation, and avoid both accidental Creole and showy words."}, {"criterion": "Justification: purpose", "marks": 5, "look": ["purpose", "\\bto (explain|encourage|persuade|inform|show)"], "min": 2, "strong": "A precise purpose connected to what the essay does.", "weak": "State the purpose with a precise verb: \"to explain how everyday home choices reduce children's NCD risk and encourage parents to act\"."}, {"criterion": "Justification: intended audience", "marks": 5, "look": ["audience", "primary|aged|busy|working|barbad"], "min": 2, "strong": "A specific audience, with a detail that explains your choices.", "weak": "Name the audience precisely and add a detail that shaped your writing: \"parents of primary school children in Barbados, many of them busy working adults without medical training\"."}, {"criterion": "Justification: context of communication", "marks": 5, "look": ["context", "newsletter|newspaper|radio|magazine|health week", "register|standard english|plain|ethos|logos|pathos|appeal"], "min": 2, "strong": "A real, timed context, linked to your register and appeals.", "weak": "Give the place and time (a school newsletter sent home during Health Week) and explain how it shaped your plain register and appeals."}], "model": "<p><b>The Habits They Will Keep</b></p><p>At six o'clock on a school morning, a parent reaches for a carton of juice because it is quick and the child likes it. That small, loving decision, repeated every day, matters more than most of us realise. Parents can lower their children's risk of NCDs through what children drink, how they move and the example adults set.</p><p>Drinks are the place to start. A child who has a sweet drink with every meal takes in sugar that the body must work hard to process. Over time, this can lead to weight gain and raise the risk of type 2 diabetes, an illness once seen mostly in adults.</p><p>School nurses report that conversations about children's blood sugar, once rare, are now routine, and that most of what they see could be prevented at home. Many parents will say, \"My child refuses to drink water.\" Try adding a slice of lime or cucumber, and let your child choose a special bottle. Children accept new habits faster when they have some control.</p><p>Movement matters too. An hour of play after school, whether cricket in the yard or a swim at the beach, strengthens the heart.</p><p>Finally, children copy what they see. A parent with a water bottle teaches without a lecture.</p><p>Small habits, kept daily, become a healthy life.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of this essay is to explain how everyday home choices reduce children's NCD risk and to encourage parents to act. The intended audience is parents of primary school children in Barbados, many of them busy and without medical training, so I avoided jargon and answered a common objection. The context of communication is a school newsletter sent home during Health Week, so I used plain Standard English, a cause-and-effect pattern and the nurse's ethos.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>At Section B standard, the essay must do more than list tips: show cause and effect, borrow credibility from the stimulus in your own words, and answer the objection parents actually raise. Keep the piece within 200–250 words and make each justification element connect to a choice you made.</p>"}}]}], "test": {"title": "Paper 3 (Paper 032) Test", "pass": 70, "items": [{"q": "Which statement correctly describes Paper 032?", "options": ["Three compulsory questions covering the three Modules, based on a central stimulus", "Three optional questions on three different stimuli", "Two essays chosen from five topics", "One long essay on the student's own topic"], "answer": 0, "why": "The regulations state three compulsory questions, covering the three Modules and based on a central stimulus. Option B is tempting because it mentions three questions, but they are compulsory and share one stimulus."}, {"q": "What may a candidate bring into Paper 032?", "options": ["Any handwritten notes", "One sheet of notes plus a printed guide", "Two sheets with 5 points each", "A single sheet of double-sided A4 paper with no more than 10 points related to the topic of the response"], "answer": 3, "why": "For the 2027 examination, only a single double-sided A4 sheet with no more than 10 points, and nothing else, is permitted. Option C looks equivalent because the total is still 10 points, but only one sheet is allowed."}, {"q": "What are the topic and intended audience for 2027?", "options": ["Non-communicable diseases in children, parents", "Cyberbullying, parents", "Climate change, teenagers", "Natural disasters, homeowners"], "answer": 0, "why": "2027 is non-communicable diseases in children, for parents. Option B is tempting because its audience is also parents, but cyberbullying is the 2030 topic."}, {"q": "A candidate answers Q1(a) with: \"Literacy and cost.\" Based on the specimen mark scheme, what is this likely to earn out of 4?", "options": ["0", "4", "3", "1"], "answer": 3, "why": "Two challenges identified but not explained earn 1 mark; 2 marks each require clear explanation. Option A is too harsh, because correct identification is still credited."}, {"q": "Which answer best fits: \"Justify ONE appropriate traditional medium, other than the printed medium, for informing elderly homeowners in a rural village about free roof inspections\"?", "options": ["\"A newspaper advert, because it reaches many readers.\"", "\"A flyer, because people can keep it.\"", "\"Instagram, because it is popular.\"", "\"Radio, because it is cheap to own, reaches rural districts and can repeat the message several times a day while older listeners go about their chores.\""], "answer": 3, "why": "Radio is traditional, not print, and the answer gives reasons linked to the target group. Options A and B are print, which the question excludes, and Instagram is digital rather than traditional."}, {"q": "Which is the strongest comment on a local source of information about a children's health screening programme?", "options": ["\"Parents could consult the nurse at their community clinic, who is trained and works directly with the screening programme, so her information is accurate and current.\"", "\"The internet.\"", "\"A friend's group chat, because it is fast.\"", "\"Television.\""], "answer": 0, "why": "It names a credible local source and explains its authority and currency, earning the full 2 marks. Option C is fast but unreliable, and options B and D are identification without comment."}, {"passage": "<p class=\"passage\">\"Homeowners are advised that applications for the shutter subsidy close on 30 April. Proof of ownership must be submitted.\"</p>", "q": "Which answer to \"Explain the dialectal variety and register used\" earns full marks?", "options": ["\"Standard English and formal.\"", "\"Standard English, because it is the official variety used by government; formal register, because the notice is official and concerns a serious financial matter.\"", "\"Creole, to connect with homeowners.\"", "\"Good, clear language.\""], "answer": 1, "why": "Each feature is identified and explained, earning 1 + 1 twice. Option A identifies both correctly but explains neither, so it earns only 1 mark."}, {"q": "\"A strap in time saves the roof.\" Which literary technique does this slogan use?", "options": ["Allusion", "Onomatopoeia", "Rhetorical question", "Personification"], "answer": 0, "why": "It alludes to the proverb \"A stitch in time saves nine\", suggesting early action prevents greater damage. It is not a rhetorical question, since it is a statement, not a question."}, {"q": "How is Question 3(a), the 200–250 word piece, marked in the specimen?", "options": ["Out of 25, in bands, for creativity, organisation and expression", "Out of 40, for length", "Out of 10, for grammar only", "Out of 15, for purpose, audience and context only"], "answer": 0, "why": "The piece is marked out of 25 in bands considering creativity, organisation and expression. Option D describes the justification, which is the separate 15-mark part."}, {"q": "Which sentence in a justification names the CONTEXT of communication?", "options": ["\"It would be published in the home section of a weekend newspaper in May.\"", "\"It is about hurricanes.\"", "\"The purpose is to persuade homeowners to secure their roofs.\"", "\"The intended audience is homeowners in hillside districts.\""], "answer": 0, "why": "Context means where and how the piece appears in the real world. Option B is tempting, but \"about hurricanes\" is content, not context."}, {"q": "\"With hurricane season six weeks away, there has never been a better moment to check your roof.\" Which rhetorical appeal is this?", "options": ["Pathos", "Kairos", "Logos", "Ethos"], "answer": 1, "why": "Kairos appeals to timeliness: why act now. Logos may seem possible because a reason is given, but the reason rests on timing rather than evidence."}, {"q": "A student has 15 minutes left. Section B's piece is finished, but there is no justification. What should the student do FIRST?", "options": ["Polish the piece's opening", "Recheck Section A", "Leave early", "Write the 50–100 word justification of purpose, intended audience and context"], "answer": 3, "why": "The justification is worth 15 marks, so securing it is the priority. Polishing the opening (A) feels productive but cannot recover 15 missing marks."}]}, "paper2": {"type": "write", "title": "Paper 3 practice: production + justification", "genre": "Section B: reflective or expository piece + justification", "stimulus": "<p><b>Mock Paper 032: 2026 topic, natural disasters; intended audience, homeowners</b></p><p>The following public service announcement was published in a daily newspaper and read on radio.</p><blockquote class=\"passage\"><p><b>HURRICANE SEASON STARTS 1 JUNE: IS YOUR HOME READY?</b></p><p>The National Disaster Preparedness Office (NDPO) reminds all homeowners that the Atlantic hurricane season begins on 1 June. Last season, many residents learned at great cost that a home is only as strong as its weakest point. In the districts most affected, NDPO inspectors found that roofs secured with nails alone were the most likely to be damaged or lost, while homes with fitted shutters suffered far less water damage inside.</p><p>Homeowners are therefore urged to complete the following before 31 May: secure roofs with hurricane straps; fit or repair shutters for every window and glass door; clear gutters and drains; trim trees overhanging the roof; clean and fill cisterns; and assemble an emergency kit containing water, non-perishable food, a battery-powered radio, a torch, first-aid supplies, medication, and copies of important documents in a waterproof bag.</p><p>Free roof inspections are available throughout May. Homeowners may register on the NDPO website or in person at their district community centre on Saturdays from 9:00 a.m. to 12:00 noon. Approved low-income households may qualify for a subsidy towards hurricane straps and shutters. Applications close on 15 May.</p><p>\"Preparation is not panic,\" said an NDPO spokesperson. \"It is simply the cheapest insurance a homeowner can buy.\"</p></blockquote><p><b>Figures from a NDPO post-storm survey of 300 households (for practice only):</b></p><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table><tr><th>Survey finding</th><th>Result</th></tr><tr><td>Households that had fitted hurricane straps before the storm</td><td>About 1 in 3</td></tr><tr><td>Households with an emergency kit ready before the warning</td><td>About 2 in 5</td></tr><tr><td>Households that said they \"waited for the official warning\" before preparing</td><td>About 6 in 10</td></tr><tr><td>Households that reported damage to their roof</td><td>About half</td></tr></table></div><p><i>(In the full paper, Section A would ask Question 1 and Question 2 on this stimulus. Practise those in Units 2 and 3. This task is Section B.)</i></p>", "prompt": "<p><b>SECTION B, Question 3</b></p><p><b>(a)</b> Write a <b>reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words</b> based on the topic <b>natural disasters</b>, aimed at <b>homeowners</b>. You may write, for example, a homeowner's reflective journal entry, a reflective letter to neighbours, a newspaper feature or an expository essay. Use the stimulus selectively; do not copy it. [25 marks]</p><p><b>(b)</b> Based on your response in (a), write a paragraph of <b>50–100 words</b>, under the heading \"Justification\", justifying: <b>the purpose</b>, <b>the intended audience</b>, <b>the context of communication</b>. [15 marks]</p><p>Write both parts in the box below: the piece first, then the justification. The piece itself must be 200–250 words; the box allows up to 350 words in total so that your justification fits.</p>", "min": 250, "max": 350, "frame": "<p><b>Plan (3 minutes):</b> Audience → Purpose → Context → Message → Language & Register → Tone & Medium.</p><p><b>Draft the justification now</b>, then write the piece.</p><p><b>Reflective:</b> a date or setting, a remembered moment, feelings, a turning point, the lesson learnt.</p><p><b>Expository:</b> a headline or thesis, two or three organised points with transitions, evidence from the stimulus, a short conclusion or call to action.</p><p><b>Check:</b> piece 200–250 words; justification 50–100 words; Standard English proofread.</p>", "checklist": ["My piece is reflective or expository, on natural disasters, written for homeowners, and 200–250 words long.", "It has a clear introduction, development and conclusion, and engages the audience with original ideas and at least one deliberate appeal.", "I used the stimulus selectively without copying it.", "My register suits the genre, and I proofread grammar and punctuation.", "I wrote a 50–100 word justification explaining how my piece relates to its purpose, intended audience and context of communication."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "Creativity and content (unique use of genre, audience engagement, originality)", "marks": 10, "look": ["\\b(you|your|I|we)\\b", "roof|strap|shutter|cistern|kit|gutter|drain", "\\b(imagine|remember|now|before june|weeks|season)\\b", "homeowner|neighbour|household|home"], "min": 3, "strong": "Your piece uses its genre with flair, speaks to homeowners and goes beyond the stimulus with original detail. Next step: sharpen one image or moment so readers cannot forget it.", "weak": "Make the piece your own and engage homeowners directly. Add a personal moment or vivid detail (\"the sound of galvanise peeling off at 3 a.m.\") and speak to the reader (\"Look up at your roof tonight\")."}, {"criterion": "Organisation (introduction, development, conclusion, coherence)", "marks": 7, "look": ["\\b(first|firstly|next|then|in addition|furthermore|however|finally|looking back|that night|now)\\b", "\\n\\s*\\n|</p>\\s*<p>"], "min": 1, "strong": "Your piece moves clearly from introduction through development to conclusion. Next step: let the conclusion echo your opening for a satisfying close.", "weak": "Give your piece a clear shape: a short introduction, two developing paragraphs linked by transitions (\"First...\", \"Looking back...\"), and a brief conclusion."}, {"criterion": "Expression (register suited to genre, grammar, punctuation, proofreading)", "marks": 8, "look": ["[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]", ",", "[;:]"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your register suits the genre and your Standard English is accurate. Next step: vary sentence length for emphasis at key moments.", "weak": "Proofread carefully: sentence boundaries, agreement and punctuation. Use the correct word, not the biggest word, and keep any Creole deliberate."}, {"criterion": "Justification: purpose, intended audience, context of communication (50–100 words)", "marks": 15, "look": ["purpose", "audience", "context", "justification", "newspaper|radio|blog|newsletter|community|association"], "min": 3, "strong": "Your justification explains how the piece relates to its purpose, intended audience and real-world context, which is top-band work. Next step: add one clause linking your register or appeal to the audience.", "weak": "Your justification must explain, not just list. Write 50–100 words: \"The purpose of this [form] is to [verb] homeowners to... The intended audience is [specific homeowners]... The context of communication is [where and how], so I used [register/appeal] to...\""}], "model": "<p><i>(Model reflective piece: a homeowner's journal entry.)</i></p><p><b>Saturday 31 May, Salem</b></p><p>Tomorrow the season begins, and for the first time in twenty years of owning this house, I am ready.</p><p>Last September I was not. Like most of my neighbours, I waited for the official warning. By then the hardware store had no shutters left, and I spent the night in the bathroom with my grandchildren, listening to the galvanise lift and slap above us like a wounded bird. By morning, half the kitchen roof was in the neighbour's yard, and the rain had found everything we owned.</p><p>The repairs cost more than I earn in four months. The straps the inspector recommended afterwards cost less than one week's groceries.</p><p>That is the sum that keeps me awake. I did not lose my roof because the storm was cruel. I lost it because I believed there was always more time.</p><p>So this year I started in April. The inspector came on a Tuesday. The straps went on the next weekend. The shutters are labelled and stacked, the cistern is clean, and the kit sits by the door: water, torch, radio, tablets, papers in a sealed bag.</p><p>Tonight, for the first time in months, the wind sounds like wind and nothing more.</p><p>Preparation is not panic. It is peace.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of this reflective journal entry is to persuade homeowners to prepare before hurricane season by sharing the cost of one homeowner's delay. The intended audience is homeowners in Montserrat who, like most surveyed households, wait for an official warning. The context of communication is a residents' association blog shared on community noticeboards in May. The first-person voice and the image of the lifting roof create pathos, while the cost comparison provides logos, and the language remains clear Standard English.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>Section B is worth 40 of the paper's 60 marks: 25 for the piece and 15 for the justification. Plan with the chain, draft the justification first, and keep the piece within 200–250 words with a clear introduction, development and conclusion. Use the stimulus selectively; the creativity marks reward originality and audience engagement, not a retelling of the PSA. Finally, make the justification explain how your piece serves its purpose, audience and context, because the top band rewards connection, not a list.</p>", "easy": {"title": "Paper 3 practice: production + justification (Foundation)", "bloom": "Apply", "genre": "Section B (guided): reflective journal entry + justification", "prompt": "<p><b>SECTION B, Question 3 (guided practice)</b></p><p><b>(a)</b> Write a <b>reflective journal entry</b> of <b>120–180 words</b> by a homeowner, the night before hurricane season begins, about why they decided to prepare their home this year. Build it in four parts:</p><ol><li><b>Date and place:</b> for example, \"Saturday 31 May, Salem\".</li><li><b>Last year:</b> what happened when you waited for the warning.</li><li><b>This year:</b> TWO things you have done to prepare (use the stimulus, in your own words).</li><li><b>The lesson:</b> one sentence about what you have learnt.</li></ol><p>[11 marks]</p><p><b>(b)</b> Under the heading \"Justification\", write <b>50–80 words</b> using the three starters: purpose, intended audience and context of communication. [9 marks]</p><p>[Total: 20 marks]</p>", "min": 170, "max": 260, "frame": "<p><b>Saturday 31 May, ...</b><br>Last year, I waited until ... Then ...<br>This year, I ... I also ...<br>Now I know that ...</p><p><b>Justification</b><br>The purpose of this journal entry is to ...<br>The intended audience is homeowners who ...<br>The context of communication is ...</p>", "checklist": ["I wrote a date and place and used \"I\" throughout.", "I described what happened last year when I waited.", "I named two things I have done this year, in my own words.", "I ended with a one-sentence lesson.", "My justification has purpose, audience and context, in 50–80 words."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "Ideas and feeling", "marks": 5, "look": ["\\b(I|my|we)\\b", "roof|strap|shutter|cistern|kit|water|gutter", "last (year|september|season)|waited", "\\b(felt|afraid|scared|relieved|ready|worried)\\b"], "min": 3, "strong": "Your entry is personal and shows how the homeowner changed. Next step: add one sound or image from last year's storm.", "weak": "Write as the homeowner (\"I\"), say what went wrong last year, name two things you have done this year and say how you feel now."}, {"criterion": "Shape of the entry", "marks": 3, "look": ["\\b(monday|tuesday|wednesday|thursday|friday|saturday|sunday)\\b", "last year|this year|now"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your entry moves clearly from last year to this year to the lesson.", "weak": "Follow the four parts in order: date, last year, this year, the lesson."}, {"criterion": "Clear English", "marks": 3, "look": ["[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]", ","], "min": 1, "strong": "Your sentences are clear and correctly punctuated.", "weak": "Write in complete Standard English sentences with capital letters and full stops."}, {"criterion": "Justification: purpose", "marks": 3, "look": ["purpose"], "min": 1, "strong": "Your purpose is clear.", "weak": "Start with \"The purpose of this journal entry is to...\" and use a verb such as \"persuade\" or \"encourage\"."}, {"criterion": "Justification: intended audience", "marks": 3, "look": ["audience", "homeowner"], "min": 2, "strong": "You named the audience: homeowners.", "weak": "Write \"The intended audience is homeowners who...\" and add one detail about them."}, {"criterion": "Justification: context of communication", "marks": 3, "look": ["context", "blog|newspaper|newsletter|radio|noticeboard|association"], "min": 2, "strong": "You named where the entry would be shared.", "weak": "Say where readers would find it, such as a residents' association blog in May."}], "model": "<p><b>Saturday 31 May, Salem</b></p><p>Tomorrow hurricane season begins, and this year I am ready.</p><p>Last year, I waited until the official warning. By then, the hardware store had sold out of shutters. That night I sat in the bathroom with my grandchildren while the wind lifted part of our kitchen roof. The repairs took four months and most of our savings.</p><p>This year, I booked a free roof inspection in April, and a builder fitted hurricane straps the next weekend. I also cleaned the cistern and packed a kit with water, a torch, a radio and copies of our papers in a sealed bag.</p><p>Tonight the wind is only wind. Now I know that preparing early costs far less than repairing late.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of this journal entry is to persuade homeowners to prepare their homes before hurricane season. The intended audience is homeowners in Montserrat who usually wait for the official warning before they act. The context of communication is a residents' association blog, shared in May before the season begins.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>Write as the homeowner, show what changed between last year and this year, and end with the lesson. Keep the justification separate and use all three starters.</p>"}, "up": {"title": "Paper 3 practice: production + justification (Challenge)", "bloom": "Create", "genre": "Section B (extended challenge): reflective or expository piece + justification", "prompt": "<p><b>SECTION B, Question 3 (challenge version)</b></p><p><b>(a)</b> Write a <b>reflective OR expository piece of between 200 and 250 words</b> on the topic <b>natural disasters</b>, aimed at <b>homeowners who waited for the official warning last season</b> (about 6 in 10 of the households surveyed). Your piece must:</p><ul><li>use a form you have chosen deliberately for this audience;</li><li>integrate TWO figures from the survey <b>accurately and in your own words</b>;</li><li>answer the belief that \"there is always time once the warning comes\";</li><li>use at least TWO deliberate rhetorical appeals;</li><li>end with a conclusion that is shorter than your opening and that leaves the reader with one clear action.</li></ul><p>[25 marks: Creativity 10, Organisation 7, Expression 8]</p><p><b>(b)</b> Under the heading \"Justification\", write <b>50–100 words</b> explaining how your piece relates to its <b>purpose</b>, <b>intended audience</b> and <b>context of communication</b>, including why you chose your form. [15 marks: purpose 5, intended audience 5, context of communication 5]</p><p>The piece must be 200–250 words; the box allows up to 350 words in total so that your justification fits.</p>", "min": 250, "max": 350, "frame": "<p><b>Plan (3 minutes):</b> Which form will move people who know the advice but delay? (Reflective builds identification; expository builds a case.)</p><p><b>Opening:</b> a moment or a question that names the delay.<br><b>Development:</b> the cost of waiting (figure 1, in your own words) → why \"there is always time\" is false (figure 2) → what to do instead.<br><b>Appeals:</b> plan two (for example, pathos from a remembered night; logos from the figures; kairos from the date).<br><b>Conclusion:</b> short; one action.</p><p><b>Justification:</b> purpose → this specific audience → context → why this form.</p>", "checklist": ["My piece is reflective or expository, chosen for homeowners who delay, and 200–250 words long.", "I used TWO survey figures accurately and in my own words.", "I answered the belief that \"there is always time once the warning comes\".", "I used at least two deliberate appeals, and my conclusion is short and gives one action.", "My 50–100 word justification explains purpose, audience and context, including why I chose my form."], "rubric": [{"criterion": "Creativity (unique use of genre, audience engagement, originality)", "marks": 10, "look": ["waited|wait|warning", "(1|one|a) in (3|three)|(2|two) in (5|five)|(6|six) in (10|ten)|half|third|most", "always time|plenty of time|more time|too late|sold out", "\\b(you|your|I|we)\\b", "roof|strap|shutter|cistern|kit"], "min": 4, "strong": "Your piece speaks to people who delay, uses the survey figures as evidence rather than decoration, and dismantles \"there is always time\" with something the reader can picture. Next step: sharpen one image so it stays with the reader.", "weak": "Write for the delayers specifically. Work two figures into your own sentences (\"Only about one household in three had straps fitted before the storm\"), show why waiting fails (stores sell out, builders are booked, the warning comes too close) and engage the reader directly."}, {"criterion": "Organisation (introduction, development, conclusion, coherence)", "marks": 7, "look": ["\\b(first|then|but|however|by the time|instead|so|finally|now)\\b", "\\n\\s*\\n|</p>\\s*<p>"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your piece moves from the delay, through its cost, to the alternative and one clear action, with a conclusion shorter than the opening. Next step: let the final line echo the opening.", "weak": "Shape the piece: an opening that names the delay, development that shows its cost and answers \"there is always time\", and a short conclusion with one action."}, {"criterion": "Expression (register suited to genre, grammar, punctuation, proofreading)", "marks": 8, "look": ["[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]", ",", "[;:—–]"], "min": 2, "strong": "Your register suits the form you chose, and your Standard English is accurate and varied. Next step: proofread the figures as carefully as the grammar.", "weak": "Match the register to your form (personal for a journal entry, clear and direct for a feature), and proofread grammar, punctuation and figures."}, {"criterion": "Justification: purpose", "marks": 5, "look": ["purpose", "\\bto (persuade|encourage|convince|warn)"], "min": 2, "strong": "A precise purpose, connected to what the piece does.", "weak": "State a precise purpose: \"to persuade homeowners who usually wait for the warning to prepare before the season\"."}, {"criterion": "Justification: intended audience", "marks": 5, "look": ["audience", "wait|delay|6 in 10|six in ten"], "min": 2, "strong": "A specific audience, linked to your choice of form and appeals.", "weak": "Name the audience precisely (homeowners who waited for the warning last season) and say how that shaped your form or appeals."}, {"criterion": "Justification: context of communication", "marks": 5, "look": ["context", "blog|newspaper|newsletter|radio|noticeboard|association|may", "form|journal|feature|reflective|expository"], "min": 2, "strong": "A real, timed context, with a clear reason for your form.", "weak": "Give the place and time (for example, a residents' association blog in May) and explain why your form suits readers there."}], "model": "<p><i>(Model expository piece: a feature for a residents' association blog.)</i></p><p><b>The Warning Is Not the Starting Gun</b></p><p>Most of us have a plan for hurricane season. It goes like this: wait for the warning, then act. Last season, about six in every ten surveyed households followed exactly that plan. About half reported damage to their roofs.</p><p>Here is why the plan fails. A warning gives you roughly a day and a half. In that time, every other household in your district is joining the same queue at the hardware store. Shutters sell out. Builders stop answering their phones. A roof that needed hurricane straps in April cannot get them on the evening before landfall.</p><p>The survey shows the difference preparation makes. Only about one household in three had fitted straps before the storm, and inspectors found that roofs held down by nails alone were the most likely to be damaged or lost. Straps cost a fraction of a new roof; delay costs the roof itself.</p><p>\"There is always time once the warning comes\" feels like common sense. It is not. It is the reason hardware stores run out and roofs come off.</p><p>So change one thing this year. Do not wait for the warning to start; let it find you finished. This week, book your free roof inspection.</p><p><b>Justification</b></p><p>The purpose of this feature is to persuade homeowners who usually wait for the official warning to prepare before the season. The intended audience is those homeowners, about six in ten of the households surveyed, who already know the advice but delay. The context of communication is a residents' association blog shared in May. I chose an expository form because these readers need their belief challenged with logos, the survey figures, and kairos, not more description.</p>", "teacherTip": "<p>This version tests judgement: choose the form for the audience, make the figures work as evidence, and take the reader's belief seriously before you dismantle it. Count both parts separately: 200–250 words for the piece, 50–100 for the justification. The justification should explain why you chose your form, because that choice is part of your purpose.</p>"}}});
